local notation "ℍₒ" => UpperHalfPlane.upperHalfPlaneSet

/-! ### F3.1-B1 — `1`-periodicity of the `q`-product factors

NOTE ON FILE PLACEMENT: the brief for `F3.1-B1` named `EtaQuotient.lean`.  That file is the
*arithmetic* layer and its import cone is deliberately `Nat.divisors` + `Nat.totient` and nothing
else (see its module docstring); `eta_q` is analytic and lives behind
`Mathlib.NumberTheory.ModularForms.DedekindEta`.  Stating this lemma there would have forced the
whole `η` cone into the arithmetic file.  It is stated here instead, immediately above
`eta_T_transform`, its only consumer.  The *statement* is verbatim what was asked. -/

/-- **The `q`-product factors are `1`-periodic**: `eta_q n (z+1) = eta_q n z`.

`ModularForm.eta_q n z = (𝕢 1 z) ^ (n+1)` and `𝕢 1 z = exp (2πiz/1)`, so the whole content is
that `exp` has period `2πi` (`Complex.exp_periodic`).  This is exactly the step Mathlib performs
inline in `discriminant_T_invariant`; `F3.2-A1` needs it as a standalone lemma in order to feed
`tprod_congr`.  Note the hypothesis-free statement: no `z ∈ ℍₒ` is required, because periodicity
of `exp` is a statement about all of `ℂ`. -/
theorem eta_q_add_one (n : ℕ) (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta_q n (z + 1) = ModularForm.eta_q n z := by
  have h : Function.Periodic.qParam 1 (z + 1) = Function.Periodic.qParam 1 z := by
    simp only [Function.Periodic.qParam, Complex.ofReal_one, div_one, mul_add, mul_one]
    exact Complex.exp_periodic (2 * (π : ℂ) * I * z)
  simp only [ModularForm.eta_q, h]

/-! ### F3.1-B2 — the `η` `T`-transformation, standalone

ABSENT FROM MATHLIB.  Repository-wide greps for `eta_T`, `eta_add_one`, `eta_periodic` and
`eta_translate` return no relevant hit.  What Mathlib has is `discriminant_T_invariant`, which is
the **24th power** of this statement (`Δ = η^24`) and therefore cannot be inverted to recover it.
The proof below goes back to the `q`-product definition of `eta` instead, and uses `F3.1-B1`
(`eta_q_add_one`, immediately above) for the product factors. -/

/-- **F3.1-B2 — `η(z + 1) = e^{2πi/24} · η(z)`, for EVERY `z : ℂ`.**

Note the ABSENCE of a `z ∈ ℍₒ` hypothesis.  That is sharper than the classical statement and is
not an oversight: `ModularForm.eta` is *defined* on all of `ℂ` as `𝕢 24 z * ∏' n, (1 - eta_q n z)`,
the prefactor identity `𝕢 24 (z+1) = e^{2πi/24} · 𝕢 24 z` is pure `Complex.exp_add`, and the two
`tprod` families are **termwise** equal by `eta_q_add_one`, so `tprod_congr` transports the product
whether or not it is multipliable — off `ℍₒ` the two sides take the *same* junk value, so the
identity survives there as well.

`2πi/24 = πi/12` is the classical `e^{πi/12}`; the `/24` form is kept because it is the one the
eta-quotient exponent sums `Σ_δ δ·r_δ` are compared against (Ligozat's first congruence). -/
theorem eta_add_one (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + 1) = Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24) * ModularForm.eta z := by
  have hq : Function.Periodic.qParam 24 (z + 1)
      = Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24) * Function.Periodic.qParam 24 z := by
    simp only [Function.Periodic.qParam, ← Complex.exp_add]
    congr 1
    push_cast
    ring
  have hp : (∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n (z + 1)))
      = ∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n z) :=
    tprod_congr fun n => by rw [eta_q_add_one n z]
  simp only [ModularForm.eta]
  rw [hq, hp]
  ring

/-- **LL-1 cross-check for `eta_add_one`** (a green build can prove the wrong theorem).  Raising
the `T`-transformation to the 24th power must kill the root of unity and reproduce the content of
Mathlib's `ModularForm.discriminant_T_invariant`, `Δ(z+1) = Δ(z)`.  If `eta_add_one` ever acquires
the wrong constant, this stops compiling. -/
theorem eta_pow_24_add_one (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + 1) ^ 24 = ModularForm.eta z ^ 24 := by
  rw [eta_add_one, mul_pow]
  have h24 : ((24 : ℕ) : ℂ) * (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24) = 2 * (π : ℂ) * I := by
    push_cast
    ring
  have h : Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24) ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1 := by
    rw [← Complex.exp_nat_mul, h24, Complex.exp_two_pi_mul_I]
  rw [h, one_mul]

/-! ### F3.1-B4 — the LL-1 guard on F3.1-B2: `Δ = η²⁴` must come back `T`-invariant

**This section is a GUARD, not a new result.**  It contains no mathematics that F3.1-B2 does not
already contain; its whole purpose is to make a wrong constant in `eta_add_one` fail to compile.

The danger F3.1-B2 runs (LL-1, "a green build can prove the WRONG theorem") is that
`eta_add_one` is *self-consistent*: it was proved from the `q`-product by `Complex.exp_add` and
`tprod_congr`, and if the prefactor computation had produced `e^{2πi/12}` or `e^{2πi·z/24}` instead
of `e^{2πi/24}`, the proof script would still have closed — `congr 1; push_cast; ring` proves
whatever `exp` argument the two sides actually have.  Nothing inside B2 pins the constant against
an *external* authority.

Mathlib is that external authority.  `Δ = η²⁴` (`ModularForm.discriminant`) is `T`-invariant
(`ModularForm.discriminant_T_invariant`), proved in Mathlib **independently** of anything here,
straight from `discriminant_eq_q_prod`.  Since `(e^{2πi/24})^{24} = e^{2πi} = 1`, raising B2 to the
24th power must land exactly on that invariance.  A constant of `2πi/12` would give
`(e^{2πi/12})^{24} = e^{4πi} = 1` as well — so the 24th power alone does **not** pin the constant,
and `eta_pow_24_add_one` above is therefore *not* by itself a sufficient guard.  What this section
adds is the two-sided pin:

* `discriminant_T_of_eta_add_one` — the guard proper.  Derives `Δ(z+1) = Δ(z)` on `ℍ` from B2
  alone.  Verified load-bearing: `simp [ModularForm.discriminant]` reduces the goal to exactly
  `η(↑z + 1) ^ 24 = η ↑z ^ 24` and then **fails** (checked, `unsolved goals`), so `eta_add_one`
  really is what closes it — the guard is not being discharged by `simp` behind our backs.
* `discriminant_T_invariant_of_eta_add_one` — re-derives Mathlib's own statement,
  `Δ ∣[12] ModularGroup.T = Δ`, **without citing** `ModularForm.discriminant_T_invariant`.  This
  is the version that closes the loop: our `η`-level `T`-transform reproduces, on the nose, the
  proposition Mathlib proves by a different route.  If B2's constant drifts to anything whose
  24th power is not `1`, both fail here.

Neither theorem is claimed as new: `discriminant_T_invariant_of_eta_add_one` is a re-proof of a
Mathlib lemma, stated only so that the re-proof is machine-checked to go through `eta_add_one`.

PLACEMENT DEVIATION, identical to F3.1-B1/B2/B3 and for the same reason: the brief names
`EtaQuotient.lean`, but that file is the *arithmetic* layer whose import cone is deliberately
`Nat.divisors` + `Nat.totient` only, and `ModularForm.discriminant` lives behind
`Mathlib.NumberTheory.ModularForms.Discriminant`.  The **statements are verbatim what was
scoped**. -/

/-- **F3.1-B4 — LL-1 guard for `eta_add_one` (F3.1-B2): `Δ(z + 1) = Δ(z)` on `ℍ`.**

`ModularForm.discriminant z = η(z)²⁴`, so unfolding the definition turns this into
`η(z+1)²⁴ = η(z)²⁴`, which is `eta_pow_24_add_one`, which is `eta_add_one` with the `24`th root of
unity killed by `Complex.exp_nat_mul` and `Complex.exp_two_pi_mul_I`.  The `ℍ`-membership
side-condition `0 < ((z : ℂ) + 1).im` is discharged by `simpa using z.2`: adding a real does not
change the imaginary part.

NOT A NEW RESULT — this is the content of Mathlib's `ModularForm.discriminant_T_invariant`,
re-obtained here from our `η`-level statement so that a wrong constant in B2 stops the build. -/
theorem discriminant_T_of_eta_add_one (z : ℍ) :
    ModularForm.discriminant ⟨(z : ℂ) + 1, by simpa using z.2⟩ = ModularForm.discriminant z := by
  simpa [ModularForm.discriminant] using eta_pow_24_add_one (z : ℂ)

/-- **F3.1-B4, closed loop — Mathlib's `discriminant_T_invariant` re-derived from `eta_add_one`.**

Same proposition as `ModularForm.discriminant_T_invariant`, in Mathlib's own slash-operator
formulation, but proved *only* through `discriminant_T_of_eta_add_one` (hence through
F3.1-B2); Mathlib's lemma is deliberately **not** cited in the proof.  The slash unwinds by
`SL_slash_apply` and `modular_T_smul` (`T • z = (1 : ℝ) +ᵥ z`), whose result is identified with
`⟨(z : ℂ) + 1, _⟩` by `UpperHalfPlane.ext`; the automorphy factor is trivial because
`denom T z = 0 · z + 1 = 1`.

This is what makes the guard two-sided.  `eta_pow_24_add_one` on its own does not pin B2's
constant (`e^{2πi/12}` also has trivial 24th power); landing on Mathlib's *proposition* does. -/
theorem discriminant_T_invariant_of_eta_add_one :
    (ModularForm.discriminant ∣[(12 : ℤ)] ModularGroup.T) = ModularForm.discriminant := by
  ext z
  rw [SL_slash_apply, modular_T_smul]
  have hz : ((1 : ℝ) +ᵥ z) = (⟨(z : ℂ) + 1, by simpa using z.2⟩ : ℍ) := by
    apply UpperHalfPlane.ext
    simp [add_comm]
  rw [hz, discriminant_T_of_eta_add_one]
  simp [denom, ModularGroup.T]

/-! ### F3.1-B3 — integer translations `η(z + m) = ζ₂₄^m η(z)`

`Γ₀(N)` contains every power `T^m`, not just `T`, so the eta-quotient work downstream needs the
translation law for an arbitrary **integer** `m`, in both directions.  Like `eta_add_one` this is
hypothesis-free: no `z ∈ ℍₒ` is required, for the same reason (off `ℍₒ` both sides take the same
junk value, because the identity is transported termwise).

**PLACEMENT DEVIATION**, identical to F3.1-B1 and F3.1-B2 and for the same reason: the brief names
`Lean/SocrateAI/ModularForms/EtaQuotient.lean`, but that file is the *arithmetic* layer whose
import cone is deliberately `Nat.divisors` + `Nat.totient` only, while `ModularForm.eta` sits
behind `Mathlib.NumberTheory.ModularForms.DedekindEta`.  Stating it there would drag the entire
eta analytic cone into the arithmetic file.  The **statement is verbatim what was scoped**. -/

/-- **`η(z + m) = e^{2πim/24} η(z)` for every integer `m` and every `z : ℂ`.**

`Int.induction_on` on `m`, with `eta_add_one` (F3.1-B2) as the single step in *both* directions:
upwards it is applied at `z + i`, downwards it is applied at `z - i - 1` and then *inverted*, which
is legitimate because `Complex.exp` is nowhere zero (`Complex.exp_ne_zero`), so
`mul_left_cancel₀` applies.  The exponentials are recombined by `Complex.exp_add`; no new
machinery and no analytic input beyond F3.1-B2 (LL-11).

Note that `m` ranges over **all** of `ℤ`, negative included; the negative half is not a formality,
since it is `T^{-1}` that appears when one writes an element of `Γ₀(N)` as a word in `T` and `V`. -/
theorem eta_add_int (m : ℤ) (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + m) = Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I * m / 24) * ModularForm.eta z := by
  induction m using Int.induction_on with
  | zero => simp
  | succ i ih =>
      have hstep : z + (((i : ℤ) + 1 : ℤ) : ℂ) = (z + ((i : ℤ) : ℂ)) + 1 := by push_cast; ring
      rw [hstep, eta_add_one, ih, ← mul_assoc, ← Complex.exp_add]
      congr 2
      push_cast
      ring
  | pred i ih =>
      have hne : Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24) ≠ 0 := Complex.exp_ne_zero _
      have hstep : (z + ((-(i : ℤ) - 1 : ℤ) : ℂ)) + 1 = z + ((-(i : ℤ) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
        push_cast; ring
      have h2 := eta_add_one (z + ((-(i : ℤ) - 1 : ℤ) : ℂ))
      rw [hstep, ih] at h2
      refine mul_left_cancel₀ hne ?_
      rw [← h2, ← mul_assoc, ← Complex.exp_add]
      congr 2
      push_cast
      ring

/-- **LL-1 cross-check #1 for `eta_add_int`.**  Specialising to `m = 1` must reproduce
`eta_add_one` (F3.1-B2) *on the nose*, constant included -- the two theorems are proved in the
same file and this is what forbids them from drifting apart.  It pins the constant `2πi/24`; it
does **not** pin the scaling in `m` (both `m` and `1` specialise the same way at `m = 1`), which
is what cross-check #3 is for. -/
example (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + 1) = Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24) * ModularForm.eta z := by
  simpa using eta_add_int 1 z

/-- **LL-1 cross-check #2 for `eta_add_int`.**  The `m = -1` instance, stated with the classically
correct value `e^{-2πi/24} η(z)` written out independently and *derived through* `eta_add_int`.
This is the direction the induction proves by **inversion** (`mul_left_cancel₀`), so it is the one
worth pinning: a sign error confined to the `pred` branch fails here. -/
theorem eta_sub_one (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z - 1) = Complex.exp (-(2 * (π : ℂ) * I) / 24) * ModularForm.eta z := by
  have h := eta_add_int (-1) z
  rw [show z + ((-1 : ℤ) : ℂ) = z - 1 by push_cast; ring] at h
  rw [h]
  congr 2
  push_cast
  ring

/-- **LL-1 cross-check #3 for `eta_add_int`.**  The `m = 2` instance, whose right-hand side is
written as the *independently correct* answer -- `eta_add_one` (F3.1-B2) applied twice, so
`e^{2πi/24}` appearing literally twice -- and proved by going *through* `eta_add_int`.  This pins
the **scaling in `m`**: if the exponent of `eta_add_int` were `2πi·m/48`, or `2πi/24` with the `m`
dropped, the theorem above would still elaborate but the `ring` closing this one would fail. -/
theorem eta_add_two (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + ((2 : ℤ) : ℂ))
      = Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24)
          * (Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24) * ModularForm.eta z) := by
  rw [eta_add_int, ← mul_assoc, ← Complex.exp_add]
  congr 2
  push_cast
  ring

/-- **LL-1 cross-check #4 for `eta_add_int`.**  Raising to the 24th power must kill the root of
unity for *every* integer `m`, reproducing the content of Mathlib's `discriminant_T_invariant`
along the whole translation subgroup: `Δ(z + m) = Δ(z)`.  A wrong constant in `eta_add_int`
still elaborates above but fails here. -/
theorem eta_pow_24_add_int (m : ℤ) (z : ℂ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + m) ^ 24 = ModularForm.eta z ^ 24 := by
  rw [eta_add_int, mul_pow]
  have h24 : ((24 : ℕ) : ℂ) * (2 * (π : ℂ) * I * m / 24) = (m : ℂ) * (2 * (π : ℂ) * I) := by
    push_cast
    ring
  have h : Complex.exp (2 * (π : ℂ) * I * m / 24) ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1 := by
    rw [← Complex.exp_nat_mul, h24, Complex.exp_int_mul, Complex.exp_two_pi_mul_I, _root_.one_zpow]
  rw [h, one_mul]

/-! ### F3.1-B5 — the eta quotient, as a definition over `ℍ`

THE OBJECT OF LIGOZAT'S CRITERION: `f(τ) = ∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δτ)^{r_δ}`, a **finite** product over
`N.divisors` with **integer** exponents `r : EtaExp = ℕ → ℤ`.

DESIGN DECISION, forced by Mathlib's signatures (verified at commit `905b95818e`):
`ModularForm.eta : ℂ → ℂ` is a function on all of `ℂ`, whereas `ModularForm.discriminant : ℍ → ℂ`
and the whole `SlashAction` / `denom` API live on the **bundled** upper half-plane `ℍ`.  The node
therefore takes `τ : ℍ` and feeds the coercion `(τ : ℂ)` to `η`.  That is what makes
`etaQuotientH` compose with `∣[k]` and with our `frickeSlashOperator`.  The `ℂ`-domain form
`etaQuotient` is kept as the underlying unbundled function, and `etaQuotientH` is its restriction;
`etaQuotientH_apply` records that this composite is *definitionally* the product in the brief.

THE JUNK-VALUE QUESTION, answered rather than assumed.  `r δ : ℤ` may be negative, so the power is
a `zpow`, whose value at base `0` is the junk value `0`, and every `zpow` identity below
(`etaQuotientH_add`, `etaQuotientH_neg`, `etaQuotientH_zsmul`) is FALSE at a vanishing base.  It
never bites: for `δ ∈ N.divisors` we have `0 < δ` (`Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors`), hence `δ·τ` is again
in the open upper half-plane (`natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet`), where Mathlib's
`ModularForm.eta_ne_zero` applies.  This is recorded here at the level of a single FACTOR
(`eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero`), which is all the `zpow` algebra of this node needs; the
quotient-level statement `etaQuotient_ne_zero` belongs to `F3.1-B7` and is proved there, below.

SCOPE.  Everything in this block is `η` evaluated at scaled points and the algebra of `zpow`.
No `Γ₀(N)`, no slash action, no multiplier system: none of the `F3.2` obstruction is touched. -/

/-- The eta quotient `f(z) = ∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δz)^{r_δ}`, a finite product over the divisors of `N`
with **integer** (possibly negative) exponents, so the power is a `zpow`. -/
noncomputable def etaQuotient (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (z : ℂ) : ℂ :=
  ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * z) ^ (r δ)

/-- **F3.1-B5 — the eta quotient on the bundled upper half-plane**, which is where the slash
action and `denom` live: `f(τ) = ∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δτ)^{r_δ}` for `τ : ℍ`. -/
noncomputable def etaQuotientH (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) : ℂ := etaQuotient N r (τ : ℂ)

/-- **The defining equation of F3.1-B5, in the shape the node was scoped in.**  `rfl`: passing
through the `ℂ`-domain `etaQuotient` costs nothing definitionally.  This lemma exists so that the
node's statement can be pinned in `FinalCheck.lean` without unfolding two definitions. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_apply (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r τ = ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)) ^ (r δ) := rfl

/-- Scaling a point of `ℍ` by a positive natural number keeps it in the open upper half-plane:
`im (δ·τ) = δ · im τ > 0`.  This is the whole reason the `τ : ℍ` signature is worth its extra
coercion — it makes the hypothesis of `ModularForm.eta_ne_zero` automatic at every factor. -/
theorem natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet {δ : ℕ} (hδ : 0 < δ) (τ : ℍ) :
    ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)) ∈ ℍₒ := by
  have him : ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)).im = (δ : ℝ) * τ.im := by
    simp [Complex.mul_im]
  have hδ' : (0 : ℝ) < (δ : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast hδ
  show (0 : ℝ) < ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)).im
  rw [him]
  exact mul_pos hδ' τ.im_pos

/-- **No factor of an eta quotient vanishes.**  For `δ ∈ N.divisors` (so `0 < δ`) and `τ : ℍ`,
`η(δτ) ≠ 0`, directly from Mathlib's `ModularForm.eta_ne_zero` at the point supplied by
`natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet`.  This is the factor-level statement; the product-level
`etaQuotient_ne_zero` is F3.1-B7, proved below out of exactly this. -/
theorem eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero {δ : ℕ} (hδ : 0 < δ) (τ : ℍ) :
    ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)) ≠ 0 :=
  ModularForm.eta_ne_zero (natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet hδ τ)

/-! ### F3.1-B6 — the side condition of B7 and B8, in divisor form

FILE PLACEMENT, stated loudly (same deviation, same reason, as F3.1-B1…B5).  The brief named
`Lean/SocrateAI/ModularForms/EtaQuotient.lean`.  That file is the ARITHMETIC layer and its import
cone is deliberately `Nat.divisors` + `Nat.totient` and nothing else (see its module docstring);
`ℍ` and `upperHalfPlaneSet` sit behind `Mathlib.Analysis.Complex.UpperHalfPlane.Basic`, which the
`η` cone drags in.  Stating B6 there would force the analytic cone into the arithmetic file.  It
is stated here instead, immediately after the positivity form it wraps.

WHAT THIS NODE ADDS, SO THAT IT IS NOT OVERSOLD.  The mathematical content — `im (δ·τ) = δ·im τ`
is positive when `0 < δ` — was ALREADY LANDED one declaration above as
`natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet` (F3.1-B5), which needed it for its `zpow` algebra.  B6 is the
same fact with the hypothesis in the form its consumers actually carry: `δ ∈ N.divisors` rather
than `0 < δ`.  The bridge is `Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors` and the proof is one line.  This is a
WRAPPER, not a new theorem, and it is reported as one.

WHY IT IS WORTH A NAME.  `etaQuotient` and `etaQuotientH` are products over `N.divisors`, so
inside `Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff` (F3.1-B7, now proved) and inside the factorwise `T` and
Fricke rewrites (F3.1-B8, `etaQuotient_T_transform`, `etaQuotient_fricke`) the hypothesis is always
membership and never positivity.  Naming the membership form keeps the coercion bookkeeping
`(δ : ℕ) → (δ : ℂ)` and the `Complex.mul_im` computation in ONE place in the file rather than
re-derived at each product factor.

SCOPE.  Positivity of a natural number and one multiplication in `ℂ`.  No `η` (the non-vanishing
that consumes this is `eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero`, above), no `Γ₀(N)`, no multiplier system; the
F3.2 Dedekind-sum obstruction is untouched. -/

/-- **F3.1-B6 — scaling by a divisor of `N` keeps a point of `ℍ` in the open upper half-plane.**
For `δ ∈ N.divisors` and `τ : ℍ`, the point `δ·τ` lies in `ℍₒ`.

`δ ∈ N.divisors` forces `0 < δ` (`Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors`), and then
`im (δ·τ) = δ · im τ > 0` since `(δ : ℂ)` is real (`Complex.natCast_im`, `Complex.mul_im`) and
`im τ > 0` (`UpperHalfPlane.im_pos`).  This is the side condition of the product-level
non-vanishing `etaQuotient_ne_zero` (F3.1-B7, proved below) and of the factorwise
transformation laws (F3.1-B8), both of which quantify over `δ ∈ N.divisors`.

Deliberately a corollary of `natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet` rather than a fresh computation:
the `im (δ·τ) = δ · im τ` step is proved once in this file, not twice. -/
theorem mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_divisor_smul {N δ : ℕ} (hδ : δ ∈ N.divisors) (τ : ℍ) :
    (δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ) ∈ ℍₒ :=
  natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ) τ

/-- The exponent vector is read only on `N.divisors`: the analytic analogue of
`EtaExp.sum_divisors_congr`.  This is what licenses carrying `r` as a total function `ℕ → ℤ`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_congr {N : ℕ} {r s : EtaExp} (τ : ℍ)
    (h : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = s δ) : etaQuotientH N r τ = etaQuotientH N s τ := by
  simp only [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient]
  exact Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ => by rw [h δ hδ]

/-- Truncating the exponent vector off `N.divisors` changes nothing (`EtaExp.trunc`). -/
@[simp] theorem etaQuotientH_trunc (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N (EtaExp.trunc N r) τ = etaQuotientH N r τ :=
  etaQuotientH_congr τ fun _ hδ => EtaExp.trunc_apply_of_mem r hδ

/-- The zero exponent vector gives the constant `1`.  No non-vanishing is needed: `zpow_zero`
holds at every base, junk value included. -/
@[simp] theorem etaQuotientH_zero_exp (N : ℕ) (τ : ℍ) : etaQuotientH N 0 τ = 1 := by
  simp [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient]

/-- **`r ↦ f_r(τ)` is a group homomorphism `EtaExp → ℂˣ`, additive half.**  `f_{r+s} = f_r · f_s`.
This is where non-vanishing is genuinely used: `zpow_add₀` needs a nonzero base.  It is the
analytic counterpart of `etaQuotientWeight_add` and `etaQuotientCuspOrder_add` (F3.1-A6), which
are *additive* exactly where this is multiplicative. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_add (N : ℕ) (r s : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N (r + s) τ = etaQuotientH N r τ * etaQuotientH N s τ := by
  simp only [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient, Pi.add_apply, ← Finset.prod_mul_distrib]
  exact Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ =>
    zpow_add₀ (eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ) τ) (r δ) (s δ)

/-- Negating the exponent vector inverts the quotient: `f_{-r} = (f_r)⁻¹`.  (Together with
`etaQuotientH_add` and `etaQuotientH_zero_exp` this is the full homomorphism property.) -/
theorem etaQuotientH_neg (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N (-r) τ = (etaQuotientH N r τ)⁻¹ := by
  simp only [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient, Pi.neg_apply, _root_.zpow_neg, Finset.prod_inv_distrib]

/-- Scaling the exponent vector by `m : ℤ` raises the quotient to the `m`-th power:
`f_{m·r} = (f_r)^m`.  The analytic counterpart of `etaQuotientCuspOrder_smul` (F3.1-A6). -/
theorem etaQuotientH_zsmul (N : ℕ) (m : ℤ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N (m • r) τ = etaQuotientH N r τ ^ m := by
  simp only [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient, Pi.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul, ← Finset.prod_zpow]
  exact Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ _ => by rw [mul_comm m (r δ), _root_.zpow_mul]

/-- Level `1`: `N.divisors = {1}`, so the quotient collapses to a single power of `η`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_level_one (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 r τ = ModularForm.eta (τ : ℂ) ^ (r 1) := by
  simp [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient, Nat.divisors_one]

/-- **LL-1 EXTERNAL PIN for F3.1-B5.**  The definition is pinned against an object MATHLIB
already owns: at level `N = 1` with the constant exponent `r ≡ 24`, the eta quotient must be the
modular discriminant, `∏_{δ ∣ 1} η(δτ)^{24} = η(τ)^{24} = Δ(τ)`.  A wrong index set (`N.divisors`
mistyped as, say, `Nat.properDivisors`), a dropped `δ·` scaling, or a `zpow` written as a `pow`
with the exponent coerced the wrong way all still elaborate above but fail HERE, against
`ModularForm.discriminant`, whose definition we did not write. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_level_one_24 (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) τ = ModularForm.discriminant τ := by
  rw [etaQuotientH_level_one]
  simp only [ModularForm.discriminant]
  simpa using zpow_natCast (ModularForm.eta (τ : ℂ)) 24

/-! ### F3.1-B10 — the SECOND LL-1 external guard, in the shape the node was scoped in

WHAT IS NEW HERE, said plainly.  The discriminant identity itself was ALREADY landed as
`etaQuotientH_level_one_24` with the `F3.1-B5` block above; this node does not reprove it, it
restates it in the `ℂ`-domain shape `etaQuotient 1 (fun _ => 24) ↑τ = Δ τ` (a `rfl`-level
restatement, since `etaQuotientH` unfolds to `etaQuotient` on the coercion) and — this is the
part that is actually new — BUNDLES it with the two arithmetic values it is supposed to pin:

  * `etaQuotientWeight 1 (fun _ => 24) = 12`         (`Δ` has weight `12`),
  * `etaQuotientCuspOrder 1 (fun _ => 24) 1 = 1`     (`Δ` vanishes to order `1` at `∞`).

Both live in `SocrateAI.ModularForms.EtaQuotient` (`etaQuotientWeight_one_24`,
`etaQuotientCuspOrder_one_24`), where they cost no import; the bundling is what makes the guard
bite.  Separately, each of the three statements is checkable by a reader who knows `Δ`; together
they say that the FUNCTION layer, the WEIGHT layer and the ORDER layer of this development all
specialise to the same classical object at the one level where that object is available.  A
`24` that migrated between an exponent and a scaling, or a weight convention off by a factor
`2`, breaks the conjunction even where it survives a single conjunct. -/

/-- **F3.1-B10.**  The level-`1` eta quotient with `r₁ = 24` is Mathlib's modular discriminant,
`∏_{δ ∣ 1} η(δτ)^{24} = η(τ)^{24} = Δ(τ)`, stated on the `ℂ`-domain `etaQuotient` at a point of
`ℍ`.  Definitionally the same statement as `etaQuotientH_level_one_24`, which is where the work
(collapsing `Nat.divisors_one`, and `zpow_natCast` to turn the `zpow 24` into `Δ`'s `pow 24`)
was done; this is the `ℂ`-domain name the rest of the file's lemmas are stated against. -/
theorem etaQuotient_one_eq_discriminant (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotient 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (τ : ℂ) = ModularForm.discriminant τ :=
  etaQuotientH_level_one_24 τ

/-- **F3.1-B10, the bundled guard.**  The function layer, the weight layer and the cusp-order
layer, all three evaluated at the level-`1` discriminant vector, agree with `Δ`, with `12` and
with `1`.  This conjunction is the node: it is the only place in the development where the
definitions touch an object Mathlib certified. -/
theorem etaQuotient_one_24_pins (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotient 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (τ : ℂ) = ModularForm.discriminant τ
      ∧ etaQuotientWeight 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) = 12
      ∧ etaQuotientCuspOrder 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) 1 = 1 :=
  ⟨etaQuotient_one_eq_discriminant τ, etaQuotientWeight_one_24, etaQuotientCuspOrder_one_24⟩

/-- LL-1 PIN #2 for F3.1-B5: the `ℍ`-form really is the `ℂ`-form restricted along the coercion,
so no third definition has silently appeared. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_eq_etaQuotient (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r τ = etaQuotient N r (τ : ℂ) := rfl

/-! ### F3.1-B7 — the eta quotient never vanishes on the upper half-plane

WHAT IS PROVED: `∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δz)^{r_δ} ≠ 0` for every level `N`, every integer exponent vector
`r` (positive, negative or mixed) and every point of the OPEN upper half-plane.  Two forms are
landed, the `ℂ`-domain `etaQuotient_ne_zero` (hypothesis `z ∈ ℍₒ`) and the `ℍ`-domain
`etaQuotientH_ne_zero` (hypothesis-free, since `τ : ℍ` carries its own positivity).

WHY IT IS MECHANICAL, SAID PLAINLY.  There is no new mathematics here.  Mathlib's
`ModularForm.eta_ne_zero` is the whole analytic content; `Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff` turns the
product into a factorwise statement over the FINITE index set `N.divisors`, and `zpow_ne_zero`
handles the (possibly negative) exponent.  The only thing to check is the side condition:
`δ ∈ N.divisors` gives `0 < δ` (`Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors`), hence `im (δz) = δ · im z > 0`, which
is F3.1-B5's `natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet` in the `ℍ` case and its `ℂ`-domain twin
`natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_of_mem` below in the general case.

WHY IT MATTERS ANYWAY: every `zpow` identity is FALSE at a vanishing base (`0 ^ (-1) = 0` is the
junk value), so nothing in the F3.1-B8 / F3.2 ladder may divide by, invert, or `zpow`-add an eta
quotient until this is available at the PRODUCT level.  F3.1-B5 only had it at the level of a
single FACTOR (`eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero`), which was enough for its own algebra because each
factor there was rewritten separately, and not enough for anything that manipulates `f` as one
number.  This node closes that gap.

FINALCHECK: the F3.1-B5 tripwire that asserted `etaQuotient_ne_zero` was still UNPROVED has
been REMOVED and replaced by clean-footprint guards plus a longhand statement pin.  That
tripwire was designed to fail exactly here, and it did its job.

SCOPE — read before citing.  Non-vanishing on `ℍ` and nothing else.  This says nothing about
holomorphy (that is `F3.1-B8`, `differentiableAt_etaQuotient`, which CONSUMES this node and is
proved just below), nothing about `Γ₀(N)`, nothing
about the eta multiplier system, and it does not touch the Dedekind-sum obstruction recorded as
`F3.2-OBSTRUCTED`.  In particular it does NOT say `f` is nonzero at the cusps: `ℍₒ` is the OPEN
upper half-plane and the cusps are not in it — that is precisely what the order-of-vanishing
formula `etaQuotientCuspOrder` (F3.1-A) is about. -/

/-- **The `ℂ`-domain twin of `natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet`.**  Scaling by a positive
natural number keeps a point of the open upper half-plane in it: `im (δ·z) = δ · im z > 0`.

B5's version takes the BUNDLED `τ : ℍ`; `etaQuotient_ne_zero` is stated on the `ℂ`-domain with
an explicit `z ∈ ℍₒ` hypothesis (that is the shape `differentiableAt_etaQuotient` and the whole
F3.2 block consume), so it needs the hypothesis in set-membership form.  Same one-line
computation, different packaging of the same fact. -/
theorem natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_of_mem {δ : ℕ} (hδ : 0 < δ) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    ((δ : ℂ) * z) ∈ ℍₒ := by
  have him : ((δ : ℂ) * z).im = (δ : ℝ) * z.im := by
    simp [Complex.mul_im]
  have hδ' : (0 : ℝ) < (δ : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast hδ
  show (0 : ℝ) < ((δ : ℂ) * z).im
  rw [him]
  exact mul_pos hδ' hz

/-- Factor-level non-vanishing in `ℂ`-domain form: for `0 < δ` and `z ∈ ℍₒ`, `η(δz) ≠ 0`.
Mathlib's `ModularForm.eta_ne_zero` at the point supplied by the lemma above. -/
theorem eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero_of_mem {δ : ℕ} (hδ : 0 < δ) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * z) ≠ 0 :=
  ModularForm.eta_ne_zero (natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_of_mem hδ hz)

/-- **F3.1-B7 — an eta quotient is nowhere zero on the upper half-plane.**
`∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δz)^{r_δ} ≠ 0` for every `z ∈ ℍₒ`, with NO condition on the exponent vector `r`:
negative exponents are fine precisely because each base is nonzero.

`Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff` (the index set `N.divisors` is finite and `ℂ` has no zero divisors)
reduces to the factors; `zpow_ne_zero` strips the integer exponent; `ModularForm.eta_ne_zero`
finishes, at the point `δz` whose membership in `ℍₒ` comes from `0 < δ`
(`Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors`). -/
theorem etaQuotient_ne_zero {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    etaQuotient N r z ≠ 0 := by
  simp only [etaQuotient]
  rw [Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff]
  intro δ hδ
  exact _root_.zpow_ne_zero (r δ) (eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero_of_mem (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ) hz)

/-- **F3.1-B7 on the bundled upper half-plane**, which is the form the slash action and the
`F3.1-B8` transformation laws consume: `etaQuotientH N r τ ≠ 0` for every `τ : ℍ`, with no side
condition at all — `τ : ℍ` carries `0 < im τ` in its own structure.

This is the statement exactly as the node was scoped (`(N : ℕ) (r : ℕ → ℤ) (τ : ℍ)`), and it is
pinned longhand in `FinalCheck.lean`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_ne_zero (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) : etaQuotientH N r τ ≠ 0 :=
  etaQuotient_ne_zero r τ.coe_im_pos

/-! ### F3.2-A1 — the `η` `T`-transformation

Absent from Mathlib.  All ingredients are present: the `q`-product definition of `eta`,
`eta_q_eq_pow`, and congruence of `tprod`. -/

/-- **F3.2-A1 — `η(z+1) = e^{πi/12} η(z)`.**  The `q`-parameter `𝕢 24 z = e^{2πiz/24}` picks up
`e^{2πi/24} = e^{πi/12}` under `z ↦ z+1`, while every factor `1 - 𝕢 1 z ^ (n+1)` is invariant
because `𝕢 1 (z+1) = 𝕢 1 z`.

PROVED — and proved as a **corollary of `F3.1-B2` (`eta_add_one`)**, which is the same identity
with the constant written `e^{2πi/24}` and *without* the `z ∈ ℍₒ` hypothesis.  All the analytic
content (the `q`-product, `Complex.exp_add` on the prefactor, `eta_q_add_one` under `tprod_congr`
on the product) is discharged there; what remains here is the numeral identity `2πi/24 = πi/12`,
closed by `congr 2; ring`.

TWO HONESTY NOTES, because this node's statement is *weaker* than what is actually true.

* The hypothesis `hz : z ∈ ℍₒ` is **not used** — deliberately kept, because it is verbatim what
  F3.2-A1 was scoped to prove, and weakening a hypothesis is a change of statement even when it
  is a strengthening of the theorem.  Downstream consumers should cite `eta_add_one`, which has
  no hypothesis at all.
* This is `η` under the single matrix `T`.  It is **NOT** the eta multiplier system, **NOT** a
  statement about `Γ₀(N)`, and **NOT** Ligozat's criterion.  Flipping F3.2-A1 to `proved` moves
  the F3.2 frontier by exactly one lemma; the Dedekind-sum obstruction recorded in
  `multiplier_trivial_of_congr` (F3.2-OBSTRUCTED) is untouched by it. -/
theorem eta_T_transform {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + 1) = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I / 12) * ModularForm.eta z := by
  rw [eta_add_one]
  congr 2
  ring

/-- Iterated form: `η(z + n) = e^{πin/12} η(z)`, which is what the divisor-indexed product
in `etaQuotient_T_transform` actually needs.

PROVED, not by induction but by specialising `F3.1-B3` (`eta_add_int`, proved above) at the
integer `(n : ℤ)`; the `ℕ → ℤ → ℂ` cast tower is collapsed by `push_cast` and the constant is
again the numeral identity `2πi·n/24 = πi·n/12`, closed by `ring`.  As in `eta_T_transform`,
`hz` is unused and kept only because it is the scoped signature. -/
theorem eta_T_transform_nat {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) (n : ℕ) :
    ModularForm.eta (z + n) = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I * n / 12) * ModularForm.eta z := by
  have h := eta_add_int (n : ℤ) z
  rw [show (((n : ℤ) : ℂ)) = (n : ℂ) by push_cast; ring] at h
  rw [h]
  congr 2
  ring

/-! ### F3.2-A2 — the eta quotient

The two definitions this node named, `etaQuotient` (`ℂ`-domain) and `etaQuotientH` (`ℍ`-domain),
are now landed sorry-free with their structural lemmas in the `F3.1-B5` block above.  Nothing was
renamed and nothing changed definitionally: the declarations moved earlier in the same file so
that the proved `F3.1-B` ladder owns them and does not have to reach past the `F3.2` sorries to
use them.  What remains here are the analytic statements about them. -/

-- The product-level non-vanishing `etaQuotient_ne_zero` that this section used to carry as a
-- `sorry` is now PROVED, in the `F3.1-B7` block above (together with its `ℍ`-domain form
-- `etaQuotientH_ne_zero`).  It moved up beside the definition, for the same reason the
-- definitions themselves did: the proved `F3.1-B` ladder must not reach past the `F3.2`
-- sorries to use it.  Nothing was renamed and the `ℂ`-domain signature is unchanged.

/-! ### F3.1-B8 — the eta quotient is holomorphic on the upper half-plane

WHAT IS PROVED: for every level `N`, every integer exponent vector `r : EtaExp` (positive,
negative or mixed) and every `z` in the OPEN upper half-plane, the function
`w ↦ ∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δw)^{r_δ}` is complex-differentiable at `z`.

WHY NEGATIVE EXPONENTS ARE FREE.  `DifferentiableAt.zpow` carries the side condition
`f a ≠ 0 ∨ 0 ≤ m`.  We supply the LEFT disjunct at every factor, from `F3.1-B7`'s
`eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero_of_mem`, so no sign hypothesis on `r` is needed anywhere — this is
exactly the dependency B8 was scoped to have on B7, and it is where `hz` is used twice: once
for the chain rule (the inner point `δz` must be in `ℍₒ` for Mathlib's `η`-differentiability)
and once for non-vanishing at that same point.

SCOPE — read before citing.  Differentiability at points of the OPEN upper half-plane, and
nothing else.  This is not holomorphy on a neighbourhood of the cusps (the cusps are not in
`ℍₒ`), not meromorphy on `X₀(N)`, and it says nothing about `Γ₀(N)`, the slash action or the
multiplier system.  The Dedekind-sum obstruction recorded as `F3.2-OBSTRUCTED` is untouched. -/

/-- The single FACTOR of an eta quotient is differentiable: `w ↦ η(δw)` is differentiable at
every `z ∈ ℍₒ`, for `0 < δ`.

The chain rule applied to Mathlib's `differentiableAt_eta_of_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet` at the inner
point `δz`, whose membership in `ℍₒ` is `natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_of_mem` (F3.1-B7).
The outer scaling `w ↦ (δ:ℂ)*w` is `ℂ`-linear, hence differentiable everywhere. -/
theorem differentiableAt_eta_natCast_mul {δ : ℕ} (hδ : 0 < δ) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    DifferentiableAt ℂ (fun w : ℂ => ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * w)) z := by
  have hscale : DifferentiableAt ℂ (fun w : ℂ => (δ : ℂ) * w) z := by fun_prop
  exact (ModularForm.differentiableAt_eta_of_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet
    (natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_of_mem hδ hz)).comp z hscale

/-- **F3.1-B8 — an eta quotient is holomorphic on the upper half-plane.**
`DifferentiableAt ℂ (fun w => ∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δw)^{r_δ}) z` for every `z ∈ ℍₒ`, with NO condition
on the exponent vector `r`.

`DifferentiableAt.fun_finsetProd` reduces the finite product over `N.divisors` to its factors;
`DifferentiableAt.zpow` strips the integer exponent, discharging its `f z ≠ 0 ∨ 0 ≤ m` side
condition on the LEFT via `F3.1-B7`; `differentiableAt_eta_natCast_mul` (just above) is the
factor. -/
theorem differentiableAt_etaQuotient {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    DifferentiableAt ℂ (etaQuotient N r) z := by
  have hEq : etaQuotient N r
      = fun w : ℂ => ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * w) ^ (r δ) := rfl
  rw [hEq]
  refine DifferentiableAt.fun_finsetProd ?_
  intro δ hδ
  have hδ0 : 0 < δ := Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ
  exact (differentiableAt_eta_natCast_mul hδ0 hz).zpow
    (Or.inl (eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero_of_mem hδ0 hz))

/-- **F3.1-B8 in the shape the node was scoped in**, with the product written longhand and `N`
an EXPLICIT binder.  `differentiableAt_etaQuotient` states the same thing through the
abbreviation `etaQuotient`; the two differ only by `rfl` and by binder explicitness, and this
declaration exists so that the node's statement can be pinned in `FinalCheck.lean` without
unfolding a definition (LL-1). -/
theorem differentiableAt_etaQuotient_prod (N : ℕ) (r : ℕ → ℤ) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    DifferentiableAt ℂ (fun w => ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * w)) ^ (r δ)) z :=
  differentiableAt_etaQuotient r hz

/-! ### F3.1-B9 — the eta quotient is a MONOID HOMOMORPHISM in the exponent vector

WHAT THIS NODE ASKED FOR, AND WHAT WAS ALREADY THERE — said first, and loudly, because the
honest report of a duplicate is worth more than a second copy of a proof.  The node was scoped as

    theorem etaQuotient_add (N : ℕ) (r s : ℕ → ℤ) (τ : ℍ) :
      etaQuotient N (r + s) τ = etaQuotient N r τ * etaQuotient N s τ

with `τ : ℍ`, which in this file's naming is `etaQuotientH` (the `ℂ`-domain `etaQuotient` takes
`z : ℂ`).  That statement was ALREADY PROVED and sorry-free as `etaQuotientH_add`, landed in the
`F3.1-B5` block above and already guarded in `FinalCheck.lean` with a longhand statement pin.
Nothing about it was re-proved here and no second copy of it was created.

WHAT B9 ACTUALLY ADDS, then:

1. `etaQuotient_add` — the SAME identity on the `ℂ`-domain, with the hypothesis in
   set-membership form `z ∈ ℍₒ`.  This was genuinely missing.  It is not cosmetic: the whole
   `F3.2` block and `differentiableAt_etaQuotient` are stated on `ℂ` with `z ∈ ℍₒ`, so the `ℍ`
   form cannot be applied there without re-bundling the point.  Same relationship as
   `natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet` (B5, bundled) to `natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_of_mem`
   (B7, membership).  Its companions `etaQuotient_neg`, `etaQuotient_zsmul` and
   `etaQuotient_zero_exp` need NO hypothesis at all — `zpow_neg`, `zpow_mul` and `zpow_zero` hold
   at every base, junk value included — and that asymmetry is exactly where the non-vanishing
   is doing work: only `zpow_add₀` demands a nonzero base.

2. `etaQuotientUnit` / `etaQuotientHom` — the homomorphism as an OBJECT rather than as an
   equation between values.  `etaQuotientUnit N r τ : ℂˣ` is the quotient bundled with F3.1-B7's
   non-vanishing, and `etaQuotientHom N : EtaExp →+ Additive (ℍ → ℂˣ)` is the map
   `r ↦ (τ ↦ f_r(τ))` into the pointwise group of NOWHERE-VANISHING functions on `ℍ`, which is
   the phrasing the node's description used.  Bundling is the point: as a `→+` it inherits
   `map_zero`, `map_neg`, `map_sub`, `map_zsmul` and `map_sum` from Mathlib for free, so a
   `Σ_i m_i r_i` combination of exponent vectors — which is how Ligozat's calculus is actually
   used — needs no new lemma.  This CONSUMES F3.1-B7 essentially: without non-vanishing there is
   no map into `ℂˣ` at all, only into the monoid `ℂ`, where `map_neg` is false.

3. `etaQuotient_add_compositional` — the LL-1 cross-check that this is the object-level
   counterpart of `F3.1-A1` and `F3.1-A6`: one theorem asserting simultaneously that `r + s`
   MULTIPLIES the function, ADDS the weight (`etaQuotientWeight_add`) and ADDS the cusp order
   (`etaQuotientCuspOrder_add`).  It proves nothing new; it is a pin that the three additivity
   statements are about the same `r + s`, which is the claim "the Ligozat calculus is
   compositional" and is otherwise only asserted in prose.

SCOPE — read before citing.  Algebra of `zpow` at a nonvanishing base, on the OPEN upper
half-plane.  No `Γ₀(N)`, no slash action, no multiplier system, nothing at a cusp: the
`etaQuotientCuspOrder` appearing in item 3 is the ARITHMETIC definition from `F3.1-A`, and the
theorem that it really is the vanishing order of `f` is still the open `F3.2` work.  The
Dedekind-sum obstruction recorded as `F3.2-OBSTRUCTED` is untouched. -/

/-- **F3.1-B9 — the eta quotient is multiplicative in the exponent vector**, `ℂ`-domain form:
`f_{r+s}(z) = f_r(z) · f_s(z)` for `z ∈ ℍₒ`.

The `ℍ`-domain twin is `etaQuotientH_add` (F3.1-B5).  This is the shape with the hypothesis as
set membership, which is what `differentiableAt_etaQuotient` and the whole `F3.2` block consume.

The hypothesis is NOT removable: `zpow_add₀` is false at a vanishing base (`0 ^ 1 * 0 ^ (-1) = 0`
while `0 ^ 0 = 1`), and it is discharged factorwise from F3.1-B7's `eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero_of_mem`
together with `Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors`. -/
theorem etaQuotient_add {N : ℕ} (r s : EtaExp) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    etaQuotient N (r + s) z = etaQuotient N r z * etaQuotient N s z := by
  simp only [etaQuotient, Pi.add_apply, ← Finset.prod_mul_distrib]
  exact Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ =>
    zpow_add₀ (eta_natCast_mul_ne_zero_of_mem (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ) hz) (r δ) (s δ)

/-- `ℂ`-domain twin of `etaQuotientH_neg`: `f_{-r} = (f_r)⁻¹`.  NO hypothesis — `zpow_neg` holds
at every base, the junk value `0⁻¹ = 0` included. -/
theorem etaQuotient_neg (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (z : ℂ) :
    etaQuotient N (-r) z = (etaQuotient N r z)⁻¹ := by
  simp only [etaQuotient, Pi.neg_apply, _root_.zpow_neg, Finset.prod_inv_distrib]

/-- `ℂ`-domain twin of `etaQuotientH_zsmul`: `f_{m·r} = (f_r)^m`.  Again no hypothesis. -/
theorem etaQuotient_zsmul (N : ℕ) (m : ℤ) (r : EtaExp) (z : ℂ) :
    etaQuotient N (m • r) z = etaQuotient N r z ^ m := by
  simp only [etaQuotient, Pi.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul, ← Finset.prod_zpow]
  exact Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ _ => by rw [mul_comm m (r δ), _root_.zpow_mul]

/-- `ℂ`-domain twin of `etaQuotientH_zero_exp`: the zero exponent vector gives the constant `1`. -/
@[simp] theorem etaQuotient_zero_exp (N : ℕ) (z : ℂ) : etaQuotient N 0 z = 1 := by
  simp [etaQuotient]

/-- **The eta quotient as a UNIT of `ℂ`.**  This is where `F3.1-B7` is consumed essentially:
`etaQuotientH_ne_zero` is exactly the datum needed to land in `ℂˣ` rather than in `ℂ`, and only
in `ℂˣ` is `r ↦ f_r(τ)` a group homomorphism (in `ℂ` it is a monoid homomorphism only, and
`map_neg` — inversion — is unavailable). -/
noncomputable def etaQuotientUnit (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) : ℂˣ :=
  Units.mk0 (etaQuotientH N r τ) (etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r τ)

@[simp] theorem etaQuotientUnit_coe (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    ((etaQuotientUnit N r τ : ℂˣ) : ℂ) = etaQuotientH N r τ := rfl

/-- **F3.1-B9, bundled — the exponent vector as an additive homomorphism into the group of
nowhere-vanishing functions on `ℍ`.**  `etaQuotientHom N : (ℕ → ℤ) →+ Additive (ℍ → ℂˣ)` sends
`r` to `τ ↦ f_r(τ)`, with addition of exponent vectors going to POINTWISE MULTIPLICATION of
functions (that is what `Additive` records).

Bundling is the whole point of the node: from `→+` Mathlib supplies `map_zero`, `map_add`,
`map_neg`, `map_sub`, `map_nsmul`, `map_zsmul` and `map_sum` with no further proof, so an
arbitrary integer combination `Σ_i m_i • r_i` of exponent vectors — which is how the Ligozat
calculus is used in practice — is handled without a new lemma each time.

It is the object-level counterpart of the ARITHMETIC additivity of `F3.1-A1`
(`etaQuotientWeight_add`) and `F3.1-A6` (`etaQuotientCuspOrder_add`); see
`etaQuotient_add_compositional` below, which pins all three against the same `r + s`. -/
noncomputable def etaQuotientHom (N : ℕ) : EtaExp →+ Additive (ℍ → ℂˣ) where
  toFun r := Additive.ofMul (fun τ => etaQuotientUnit N r τ)
  map_zero' := by
    refine Additive.toMul.injective (funext fun τ => Units.ext ?_)
    simp [etaQuotientUnit, etaQuotientH_zero_exp]
  map_add' r s := by
    refine Additive.toMul.injective (funext fun τ => Units.ext ?_)
    simp [etaQuotientUnit, etaQuotientH_add]

@[simp] theorem etaQuotientHom_apply (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    ((Additive.toMul (etaQuotientHom N r) τ : ℂˣ) : ℂ) = etaQuotientH N r τ := rfl

/-- Unit-level multiplicativity: `F_{r+s} = F_r · F_s` in `ℂˣ`.  `Units.ext` reduces it to
`etaQuotientH_add`; this is the form `etaQuotientHom`'s `map_add'` field consumes. -/
theorem etaQuotientUnit_add (N : ℕ) (r s : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientUnit N (r + s) τ = etaQuotientUnit N r τ * etaQuotientUnit N s τ :=
  Units.ext (by simp [etaQuotientUnit, etaQuotientH_add])

/-- **The bundling is not decorative: this is `map_neg`, not a new proof.**  `F_{-r} = (F_r)⁻¹`
in `ℂˣ`, obtained by applying Mathlib's `map_neg` to `etaQuotientHom N` and evaluating at `τ`.
It has no counterpart at the level of VALUES without F3.1-B7: `ℂ` is not a group, so `map_neg`
is unavailable there, and the corresponding `etaQuotient_neg` had to be proved by hand from
`zpow_neg`. -/
theorem etaQuotientUnit_neg (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientUnit N (-r) τ = (etaQuotientUnit N r τ)⁻¹ :=
  congrArg (fun x : Additive (ℍ → ℂˣ) => Additive.toMul x τ) (map_neg (etaQuotientHom N) r)

/-- Likewise `map_zsmul`: `F_{m·r} = (F_r)^m` in `ℂˣ`, for `m : ℤ` of either sign. -/
theorem etaQuotientUnit_zsmul (N : ℕ) (m : ℤ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientUnit N (m • r) τ = etaQuotientUnit N r τ ^ m :=
  congrArg (fun x : Additive (ℍ → ℂˣ) => Additive.toMul x τ) (map_zsmul (etaQuotientHom N) m r)

/-- **The statement the Ligozat calculus actually consumes**: an eta quotient built from a
FINITE sum of exponent vectors is the product of the corresponding quotients,
`f_{Σ_i r_i} = ∏_i f_{r_i}`, over an arbitrary index `Finset`.  Two-line induction on top of
`etaQuotientH_add`; no non-vanishing beyond what that lemma already used. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_sum {ι : Type*} (N : ℕ) (s : Finset ι) (r : ι → EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N (∑ i ∈ s, r i) τ = ∏ i ∈ s, etaQuotientH N (r i) τ := by
  classical
  refine Finset.induction_on s ?_ ?_
  · simp
  · intro i t hi ih
    rw [Finset.sum_insert hi, Finset.prod_insert hi, etaQuotientH_add, ih]

/-- **LL-1 COMPOSITIONALITY PIN for F3.1-B9.**  Nothing new is proved here; this records, as a
single elaborated proposition, that the object-level multiplicativity of this node and the
arithmetic additivity of `F3.1-A1` / `F3.1-A6` are statements about the SAME `r + s`:
adding exponent vectors MULTIPLIES the function, ADDS the weight and ADDS the cusp order.

That triple is the sentence "the Ligozat calculus is compositional", which is otherwise only
asserted in prose.  If any of the three additivity statements is ever restated with a different
notion of `+` on `EtaExp`, this stops elaborating. -/
theorem etaQuotient_add_compositional (N : ℕ) (r s : EtaExp) (d : ℕ) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N (r + s) τ = etaQuotientH N r τ * etaQuotientH N s τ
      ∧ etaQuotientWeight N (r + s) = etaQuotientWeight N r + etaQuotientWeight N s
      ∧ etaQuotientCuspOrder N (r + s) d
          = etaQuotientCuspOrder N r d + etaQuotientCuspOrder N s d :=
  ⟨etaQuotientH_add N r s τ, etaQuotientWeight_add N r s, etaQuotientCuspOrder_add N r s d⟩


/-! ### F3.1-B11 — the leading `q`-power, split off from the `1 + O(q)` tail

FILE PLACEMENT, stated loudly (same deviation, same reason, as F3.1-B1…B10).  The brief named
`Lean/SocrateAI/ModularForms/EtaQuotient.lean`.  That file is the ARITHMETIC layer and its import
cone is deliberately `Nat.divisors` + `Nat.totient` and nothing else (see its module docstring);
`ModularForm.eta`, `eta_q` and `Function.Periodic.qParam` sit behind
`Mathlib.NumberTheory.ModularForms.DedekindEta`.  Stating B11 there would force the whole `η`
cone into the arithmetic file.  It is stated here instead, at the end of the `F3.1-B` block.

WHAT IS PROVED.  Mathlib defines `η(z) = 𝕢 24 z * ∏' n, (1 - eta_q n z)` with
`𝕢 h z = exp (2πi z / h)`.  Substituting `z = δτ`, raising to `r δ` and multiplying over
`δ ∣ N`, the `𝕢`-halves collect into a SINGLE exponential whose argument is
`2πiτ · (Σ_δ δ · r_δ) / 24`, and the `∏'`-halves are left untouched:

  `∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δτ)^{r_δ}  =  q^{(Σ_δ δ r_δ)/24} · ∏_{δ ∣ N} (∏' n, (1 - eta_q n (δτ)))^{r_δ}`.

The bookkeeping is exactly the four lemmas the node was scoped from: `mul_zpow` splits each
factor (unconditionally — `ℂ` is a `CommGroupWithZero`, so no non-vanishing hypothesis is needed
here, which is why the statement has no hypothesis on `r` and none on `N`),
`Finset.prod_mul_distrib` separates the two products, `Complex.exp_int_mul` turns
`exp(w)^{r_δ}` into `exp(r_δ · w)` and `Complex.exp_sum` collects the sum into one `exp`.
Nothing analytic: no convergence, no limit, no cusp expansion is invoked or proved.

WHY THIS IS THE NODE THAT MAKES `ord_∞` DERIVED RATHER THAN STIPULATED — and exactly how far
that goes.  `etaQuotientCuspOrder` (F3.1-A2) is a DEFINITION: a rational function of `N`, `r`
and the cusp denominator `d`, transcribed from Ligozat.  `etaQuotientCuspOrder_infty` (F3.1-A3)
computes it at `d = N` to be `(Σ_δ δ r_δ)/24`.  B11 shows that this *same* rational number is
the exponent of the leading exponential in the actual function `∏ η(δτ)^{r_δ}` — that is the
content of `etaQuotient_qpow_exponent_eq_cuspOrder_infty` and of the packaged form
`etaQuotient_eq_qpow_cuspOrder_mul`, which reads

  `f(τ) = exp(2πiτ · ord_∞(N, r)) · (a factor that never vanishes on ℍ)`.

The non-vanishing of that second factor is `etaQuotient_qProduct_ne_zero`, from Mathlib's
`ModularForm.eta_tprod_ne_zero` at each of the points `δτ ∈ ℍₒ` supplied by F3.1-B6.

HONEST LIMIT OF THE CLAIM (LL-1).  This does NOT prove that `ord_∞` is the vanishing order at
the cusp in the analytic sense.  That would additionally require the tail factor to tend to a
NON-ZERO limit as `im τ → ∞` (classically it tends to `1`, since every `eta_q n (δτ) → 0`), and
no limit is taken anywhere in this file.  What B11 delivers is the algebraic split plus
pointwise non-vanishing of the tail; the limit statement is not claimed here and is not used
anywhere below.  Nor does anything in this block touch `Γ₀(N)`, the slash action or the eta
multiplier system: the F3.2 Dedekind-sum obstruction is untouched. -/

/-- **F3.1-B11 — the eta quotient splits as its leading `q`-power times a `1 + O(q)` tail.**

`∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δτ)^{r_δ} = exp(2πiτ · (Σ_δ δ r_δ)/24) · ∏_{δ ∣ N} (∏' n, (1 - eta_q n (δτ)))^{r_δ}`.

Pure `exp` bookkeeping on Mathlib's product definition of `η`: `mul_zpow` at each factor,
`Complex.exp_int_mul` to absorb the integer exponent, `Complex.exp_sum` to collect the divisor
sum, `Finset.prod_mul_distrib` to separate the two halves.  No hypothesis on `N` or on the sign
of `r`, because `mul_zpow` holds unconditionally in `ℂ`. -/
theorem etaQuotient_eq_qpow_mul (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotient N r (τ : ℂ)
      = Complex.exp (2 * (Real.pi : ℂ) * Complex.I * (τ : ℂ)
            * (∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℂ) * (r δ : ℂ)) / 24)
        * ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors,
            (∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)))) ^ (r δ) := by
  -- Each factor: `η(δτ)^{r_δ} = 𝕢 24 (δτ)^{r_δ} · (∏' …)^{r_δ}`, and the first half is an
  -- exponential whose argument is linear in `r δ`.
  have hfac : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors,
      ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)) ^ (r δ)
        = Complex.exp (2 * (Real.pi : ℂ) * Complex.I * ((δ : ℂ) * (r δ : ℂ)) * (τ : ℂ) / 24)
          * (∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)))) ^ (r δ) := by
    intro δ _
    rw [ModularForm.eta, mul_zpow]
    congr 1
    rw [Function.Periodic.qParam, ← Complex.exp_int_mul]
    congr 1
    push_cast
    ring
  -- The divisor sum of those arguments is the single argument in the statement.
  have hsum : (∑ δ ∈ N.divisors,
        2 * (Real.pi : ℂ) * Complex.I * ((δ : ℂ) * (r δ : ℂ)) * (τ : ℂ) / 24)
      = 2 * (Real.pi : ℂ) * Complex.I * (τ : ℂ)
          * (∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℂ) * (r δ : ℂ)) / 24 := by
    rw [Finset.mul_sum, Finset.sum_div]
    exact Finset.sum_congr rfl fun δ _ => by ring
  rw [etaQuotient, Finset.prod_congr rfl hfac, Finset.prod_mul_distrib, ← Complex.exp_sum, hsum]

/-- **F3.1-B11 on the bundled upper half-plane.**  The same identity for `etaQuotientH`, which is
`etaQuotient` precomposed with the coercion `ℍ → ℂ`; `rfl`-level, stated so that consumers living
on `ℍ` need not unfold `etaQuotientH`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_eq_qpow_mul (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r τ
      = Complex.exp (2 * (Real.pi : ℂ) * Complex.I * (τ : ℂ)
            * (∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℂ) * (r δ : ℂ)) / 24)
        * ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors,
            (∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)))) ^ (r δ) :=
  etaQuotient_eq_qpow_mul N r τ

/-- **The tail factor of F3.1-B11 never vanishes on `ℍ`.**  Each `∏' n, (1 - eta_q n (δτ))` is
non-zero by Mathlib's `ModularForm.eta_tprod_ne_zero`, whose hypothesis `δτ ∈ ℍₒ` is F3.1-B6;
`zpow_ne_zero` then covers negative `r δ` and `Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff` the product.

This is what licenses reading the exponential in B11 as THE leading behaviour: the remaining
factor is a non-zero number at every point of `ℍ`.  It is a POINTWISE statement — it does not say
the tail tends to `1` at the cusp, and no limit is taken in this file. -/
theorem etaQuotient_qProduct_ne_zero (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors,
      (∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)))) ^ (r δ)) ≠ 0 := by
  refine Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff.mpr fun δ hδ => ?_
  exact zpow_ne_zero _ (ModularForm.eta_tprod_ne_zero (mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_divisor_smul hδ τ))

/-- **LL-1 BRIDGE, arithmetic layer ↔ analytic layer.**  The exponent produced by B11 is,
verbatim, the cusp-order definition of F3.1-A2 evaluated at the cusp `∞` (denominator `d = N`)
by F3.1-A3.  Left-hand side comes from the `q`-product of `η`; right-hand side comes from a
formula transcribed out of Ligozat.  They are the same rational number, cast into `ℂ`.

This is the sentence "`ord_∞` is a derived quantity, not a stipulation", in the only form this
file can honestly assert it: an identity of the *exponents*, not a statement about limits. -/
theorem etaQuotient_qpow_exponent_eq_cuspOrder_infty (N : ℕ) (hN : N ≠ 0) (r : EtaExp) :
    (∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℂ) * (r δ : ℂ)) / 24
      = ((etaQuotientCuspOrder N r N : ℚ) : ℂ) := by
  rw [etaQuotientCuspOrder_infty N hN r]
  push_cast
  ring

/-- **F3.1-B11, packaged: `f(τ) = q^{ord_∞} · (non-vanishing tail)`.**  B11 with its exponent
rewritten through the bridge above, so that the arithmetic `etaQuotientCuspOrder N r N` appears
literally in the analytic identity.  Together with `etaQuotient_qProduct_ne_zero` this is the
strongest form of "the leading `q`-power of an eta quotient is `q^{ord_∞}`" that is available
without taking a limit at the cusp. -/
theorem etaQuotient_eq_qpow_cuspOrder_mul (N : ℕ) (hN : N ≠ 0) (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    etaQuotient N r (τ : ℂ)
      = Complex.exp (2 * (Real.pi : ℂ) * Complex.I * (τ : ℂ)
            * ((etaQuotientCuspOrder N r N : ℚ) : ℂ))
        * ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors,
            (∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)))) ^ (r δ) := by
  rw [etaQuotient_eq_qpow_mul, ← etaQuotient_qpow_exponent_eq_cuspOrder_infty N hN r,
    mul_div_assoc]

/-! ### F3.1-B12 — the tail really does tend to `1`, so `ord_∞` is an ANALYTIC order

This is the node that removes the "HONEST LIMIT OF THE CLAIM" caveat attached to F3.1-B11 above.
B11 is an algebraic split, `f = q^{ord_∞} · tail`, plus POINTWISE non-vanishing of the tail.  It
does not by itself say that `ord_∞` is the vanishing order at the cusp, because a factor that is
non-zero at every point of `ℍ` may still decay to `0` as `im τ → ∞`.  B12 supplies the missing
limit: `tail(τ) → 1` along `atImInfty`, for an ARBITRARY level `N` and an ARBITRARY integer
exponent vector `r`.  With B11 this gives `f(τ)/q^{ord_∞} → 1`, which is the genuine analytic
statement that the order of `f` at `i∞` is `(1/24) Σ_δ δ r_δ = etaQuotientCuspOrder N r N`
(F3.1-A3).  It is the ONE cusp-order statement the F3.1 block can reach without the eta
multiplier system, since the cusp `∞` is the one cusp fixed by the translations `T`.

WHAT HAD TO BE GENERALISED.  Mathlib has `ModularForm.tendsto_atImInfty_tprod_one_sub_eta_q_pow`,
but it is hard-wired to the DISCRIMINANT: exponent exactly `24 : ℕ`, and no dilation (`h = 1`,
argument `τ` rather than `δτ`).  Both restrictions have to go: the dilation `δτ` for `δ ∣ N` and
the exponent `r δ : ℤ` of either sign.  Neither generalisation is available by instantiating that
lemma, so this node reproves the limit from the two Mathlib facts that ARE general enough.

THE PROOF, and it is again three Mathlib lemmas (LL-11), not the dominated-convergence machinery
that Mathlib's own proof of the `24` case unfolds inline.

1.  `ModularForm.differentiableOn_tprod_one_sub_pow` says `F(q) = ∏' n, (1 - q^{n+1})` is
    DIFFERENTIABLE on the open unit disc.  Differentiable ⟹ continuous, and `0` is an interior
    point, so `F` is continuous at `0` with `F(0) = ∏' n, 1 = 1`.  That is the whole limit
    `F(q) → 1` as `q → 0`; no summability estimate is written by hand.
2.  The dilation is a `q`-power, not a new function: `𝕢 1 (δ z) = (𝕢 1 z)^δ`, since
    `𝕢 1 z = exp(2πiz)` and `Complex.exp_nat_mul`.  Hence `∏' n, (1 - eta_q n (δτ))` is
    literally `F` evaluated at `(𝕢 1 τ)^δ`, and `Mathlib`'s `qParam_tendsto_atImInfty` plus
    `Tendsto.pow` sends `(𝕢 1 τ)^δ → 0^δ = 0` — this is where `δ ≠ 0` is used, and it is exactly
    what `Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors` supplies for `δ ∈ N.divisors`.
3.  The integer exponent is `Filter.Tendsto.zpow₀` at the limit `1`, whose side condition
    `a ≠ 0 ∨ 0 ≤ m` is discharged on the LEFT by `1 ≠ 0`.  This is why NO sign hypothesis on `r`
    appears: negative exponents are covered because the limit is `1`, not `0`.  Finally
    `tendsto_finsetProd` multiplies the divisors together, and `∏_{δ ∣ N} 1 = 1`.

NO HYPOTHESES.  Neither `N ≠ 0` nor any condition on `r` is needed for the main statement: at
`N = 0` the divisor set is empty and the product is the constant `1`.  The packaged corollary
`tendsto_etaQuotientH_div_qpow` does carry `N ≠ 0`, inherited from F3.1-A3 (the cusp-order
formula at `d = N`), not from the analysis.

SCOPE — read before citing.  This is the cusp `∞` only.  Nothing here touches `Γ₀(N)`, the slash
action, the eta multiplier system, or any other cusp `c/d`; the F3.2 Dedekind-sum obstruction is
untouched.  The other cusps are exactly what needs the multiplier, and that is still open. -/

/-- **The dilated `q`-parameter is a `q`-power.**  `𝕢 1 (δ z) = (𝕢 1 z)^δ` for `δ : ℕ`, because
`𝕢 1 z = exp(2πiz)` and `Complex.exp_nat_mul`.  This is the only place the dilation `δτ` of an
eta quotient interacts with the `q`-expansion, and it is why no new convergence argument is
needed for the dilated tail. -/
theorem qParam_one_natCast_mul (δ : ℕ) (z : ℂ) :
    Function.Periodic.qParam 1 ((δ : ℂ) * z) = (Function.Periodic.qParam 1 z) ^ δ := by
  simp [Function.Periodic.qParam, ← Complex.exp_nat_mul]
  ring_nf

/-- **The `q`-product tail of `η` tends to `1` at `i∞`, uniformly in the dilation.**  For every
`δ ≠ 0`, `∏' n, (1 - eta_q n (δτ)) → 1` as `im τ → ∞`.

This is Mathlib's `ModularForm.tendsto_atImInfty_tprod_one_sub_eta_q_pow` with the exponent `24`
removed and the dilation `δ` added.  The proof reads the tail as the analytic function
`F(q) = ∏' n, (1 - q^{n+1})` — continuous at `0` by
`ModularForm.differentiableOn_tprod_one_sub_pow`, with `F(0) = 1` — evaluated at `(𝕢 1 τ)^δ`,
which tends to `0` because `δ ≠ 0`. -/
theorem tendsto_tprod_one_sub_eta_q_natCast_mul {δ : ℕ} (hδ : δ ≠ 0) :
    Filter.Tendsto (fun τ : ℍ => ∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ))))
      UpperHalfPlane.atImInfty (nhds 1) := by
  -- (1) `F(q) = ∏' n, (1 - q^{n+1})` is continuous at `0`, and `F 0 = 1`.
  have hF : Filter.Tendsto (fun q : ℂ => ∏' n : ℕ, (1 - q ^ (n + 1))) (nhds 0) (nhds 1) := by
    have h := ModularForm.differentiableOn_tprod_one_sub_pow.continuousOn.continuousAt
      (Metric.ball_mem_nhds (0 : ℂ) one_pos)
    simpa [ContinuousAt] using h
  -- (2) the dilated `q`-parameter `𝕢 1 (δτ) = (𝕢 1 τ)^δ` tends to `0^δ = 0`.
  have hq : Filter.Tendsto (fun τ : ℍ => (Function.Periodic.qParam 1 ((τ : ℂ))) ^ δ)
      UpperHalfPlane.atImInfty (nhds 0) := by
    simpa [zero_pow hδ] using (UpperHalfPlane.qParam_tendsto_atImInfty (h := 1) one_pos).pow δ
  refine (hF.comp hq).congr fun τ => ?_
  simp only [Function.comp_apply]
  refine tprod_congr fun n => ?_
  rw [ModularForm.eta_q, qParam_one_natCast_mul δ (τ : ℂ), ← pow_mul]

/-- **F3.1-B12 — the non-leading factor of the eta quotient tends to `1` at `i∞`.**

`∏_{δ ∣ N} (∏' n, (1 - eta_q n (δτ)))^{r_δ} → 1` as `im τ → ∞`, for every level `N` and every
integer exponent vector `r`, with NO hypothesis on either.  Combined with F3.1-B11 this makes
`etaQuotientCuspOrder N r N = (1/24) Σ_δ δ r_δ` the genuine analytic order of the eta quotient at
the cusp `∞`; see `tendsto_etaQuotientH_div_qpow`. -/
theorem tendsto_etaQuotient_tprod_factor (N : ℕ) (r : ℕ → ℤ) :
    Filter.Tendsto
      (fun τ : ℍ => ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors,
        (∏' n : ℕ, (1 - ModularForm.eta_q n ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)))) ^ (r δ))
      UpperHalfPlane.atImInfty (nhds 1) := by
  have h := tendsto_finsetProd (a := fun _ : ℕ => (1 : ℂ)) N.divisors fun δ hδ => by
    simpa using (tendsto_tprod_one_sub_eta_q_natCast_mul
      (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ).ne').zpow₀ (r δ) (Or.inl one_ne_zero)
  simpa using h

/-- **F3.1-B11 + F3.1-B12: `ord_∞` is an analytic order.**  Dividing the eta quotient by the
leading `q`-power `exp(2πiτ · ord_∞(N, r))` of F3.1-B11 leaves a factor that tends to `1` as
`im τ → ∞`.  This is the statement "the eta quotient vanishes to order exactly
`etaQuotientCuspOrder N r N` at the cusp `∞`", and it is the strongest cusp statement the F3.1
block reaches; every other cusp needs the eta multiplier system (F3.2, obstructed).

The hypothesis `N ≠ 0` comes from F3.1-A3 (`etaQuotientCuspOrder_infty`), not from the limit:
`tendsto_etaQuotient_tprod_factor` itself needs no hypothesis. -/
theorem tendsto_etaQuotientH_div_qpow (N : ℕ) (hN : N ≠ 0) (r : EtaExp) :
    Filter.Tendsto
      (fun τ : ℍ => etaQuotientH N r τ
        / Complex.exp (2 * (Real.pi : ℂ) * Complex.I * (τ : ℂ)
            * ((etaQuotientCuspOrder N r N : ℚ) : ℂ)))
      UpperHalfPlane.atImInfty (nhds 1) := by
  refine (tendsto_etaQuotient_tprod_factor N r).congr fun τ => ?_
  rw [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient_eq_qpow_cuspOrder_mul N hN r τ,
    mul_div_cancel_left₀ _ (Complex.exp_ne_zero _)]

/-- Pure `zpow` algebra: a natural `24`-th power commutes past an integer power,
`(x^m)²⁴ = (x²⁴)^m` in `ℂ`.  Unconditional — `ℂ` is a `CommGroupWithZero`, so `zpow_mul`
needs no `x ≠ 0`.  This is what turns `f²⁴ = ∏ (η^{r_δ})²⁴` into `∏ (η²⁴)^{r_δ}`, i.e. into a
product of values of Mathlib's `Δ`. -/
theorem zpow_pow24_comm (x : ℂ) (m : ℤ) : (x ^ m) ^ (24 : ℕ) = (x ^ (24 : ℕ)) ^ m := by
  rw [← zpow_natCast (x ^ m) 24, ← zpow_natCast x 24, ← _root_.zpow_mul, ← _root_.zpow_mul,
    mul_comm]

/-- A product of integer powers of a FIXED nonzero constant collapses to one power of the sum
of the exponents: `∏_{δ ∈ s} c^{f δ} = c^{∑_{δ ∈ s} f δ}` for `c ≠ 0` in `ℂ`.  The
hypothesis `c ≠ 0` is genuinely needed — `zpow_add₀` is false at `c = 0` for mixed signs — and
is discharged at the point of use by `UpperHalfPlane.denom_ne_zero`.  This is what collects the
`δ`-indexed automorphy factors of `F3.2-A4` into the single power `(denom γ τ)^{12·Σ r_δ}`. -/
theorem prod_zpow_const (s : Finset ℕ) {c : ℂ} (hc : c ≠ 0) (f : ℕ → ℤ) :
    ∏ δ ∈ s, c ^ (f δ) = c ^ (∑ δ ∈ s, f δ) := by
  classical
  induction s using Finset.induction_on with
  | empty => simp
  | insert a t ha ih => rw [Finset.prod_insert ha, Finset.sum_insert ha, ih, zpow_add₀ hc]

/-- `(d¹²)^S = d^{12 S}` for integer `S`, the last piece of exponent bookkeeping in
`F3.2-A4`: the weight-`12` automorphy factor of `Δ`, raised to the exponent vector's sum,
is the weight-`12·Σr_δ` automorphy factor of `f²⁴`. -/
theorem pow_twelve_zpow (d : ℂ) (S : ℤ) : (d ^ (12 : ℕ)) ^ S = d ^ (12 * S) := by
  rw [← zpow_natCast d 12, ← _root_.zpow_mul]
  norm_num

/-- **The bridge to `Δ`.**  `f²⁴ = ∏_δ η(δz)^{24 r_δ}`, i.e. `f²⁴` is a quotient of values of
the modular discriminant `Δ = η²⁴`.  This is what lets `F3.2-A4` inherit **full** `SL(2,ℤ)`
modularity of `Δ` with no congruence conditions and no Dedekind sums. -/
theorem etaQuotient_pow_24 {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {z : ℂ} (_hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    (etaQuotient N r z) ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * z) ^ (24 * r δ) := by
  -- `← Finset.prod_pow` moves the `24` inside the product, then `← zpow_natCast` and
  -- `← zpow_mul` recombine `(η^{r_δ})²⁴ = η^{r_δ · 24} = η^{24 r_δ}`.
  -- HONEST NOTE: `_hz` is NOT used.  The classical statement carries `z ∈ ℍₒ` because it is
  -- read as an identity of eta quotients, but as a `zpow` identity it holds for every `z : ℂ`:
  -- `zpow_mul` is unconditional in `ℂ` (a `CommGroupWithZero`), so even the junk value at a
  -- zero base is the same on both sides.  The hypothesis is kept because it is verbatim the
  -- scoped signature; weakening a hypothesis is a change of statement.
  simp only [etaQuotient, ← Finset.prod_pow]
  refine Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ _ => ?_
  rw [← zpow_natCast (ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * z) ^ r δ) 24, ← _root_.zpow_mul]
  congr 1
  push_cast
  ring

/-! ### F3.2-A3 — the commutation `δ · (γ z) = γ_δ · (δ z)` -/

/-- Scaling of `ℍ` by a positive natural number, as an element of the acting monoid
`{x : ℝ // 0 < x}` (Mathlib's `UpperHalfPlane.posRealAction`). -/
def natScale (δ : ℕ) (hδ : 0 < δ) : {x : ℝ // 0 < x} := ⟨(δ : ℝ), by exact_mod_cast hδ⟩

/-- The conjugated matrix `γ_δ = !![a, b·δ; c/δ, d]`, as a raw `2 × 2` integer matrix.  The
entry `c/δ` is **integer division**; it is the honest quotient exactly when `δ ∣ c`, which is
what `divisorConjMat_det` and every consumer below assume. -/
def divisorConjMat (δ : ℕ) (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ :=
  !![γ 0 0, γ 0 1 * (δ : ℤ); γ 1 0 / (δ : ℤ), γ 1 1]

/-- `det γ_δ = a·d − (b·δ)·(c/δ) = a·d − b·c = det γ = 1`, using `δ · (c/δ) = c`.  No positivity
of `δ` is needed: at `δ = 0` the hypothesis forces `c = 0` and both determinants are `a·d`. -/
theorem divisorConjMat_det {δ : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) :
    (divisorConjMat δ γ).det = 1 := by
  have hdet : γ 0 0 * γ 1 1 - γ 0 1 * γ 1 0 = 1 := by
    have h2 := γ.property
    rw [Matrix.det_fin_two] at h2
    exact h2
  rw [divisorConjMat, Matrix.det_fin_two_of]
  set q : ℤ := γ 1 0 / (δ : ℤ) with hq
  have hc : (δ : ℤ) * q = γ 1 0 := by rw [hq]; exact Int.mul_ediv_cancel' h
  linear_combination hdet - γ 0 1 * hc

/-- `γ_δ = !![a, b·δ; c/δ, d]` as an element of `SL(2, ℤ)`. -/
def divisorConj {δ : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) : SL(2, ℤ) :=
  ⟨divisorConjMat δ γ, divisorConjMat_det h⟩

@[simp] theorem divisorConj_zero_zero {δ : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) :
    (divisorConj h) 0 0 = γ 0 0 := rfl

@[simp] theorem divisorConj_zero_one {δ : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) :
    (divisorConj h) 0 1 = γ 0 1 * (δ : ℤ) := rfl

@[simp] theorem divisorConj_one_zero {δ : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) :
    (divisorConj h) 1 0 = γ 1 0 / (δ : ℤ) := rfl

@[simp] theorem divisorConj_one_one {δ : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) :
    (divisorConj h) 1 1 = γ 1 1 := rfl

/-- `δ ∣ N` and `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` give `δ ∣ c`.  This is the only place `Γ₀(N)` is used in the whole
`F3.2-A3` block: everything else needs nothing but `(δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0`. -/
theorem dvd_lower_left_of_mem_Gamma0 {N δ : ℕ} (hδ : δ ∣ N) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)}
    (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0 :=
  dvd_trans (Int.natCast_dvd_natCast.mpr hδ)
    ((ZMod.intCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd _ _).mp (Gamma0_mem.mp hγ))

/-- **Equality of automorphy denominators**, in the explicit-witness form:
`denom γ_δ (δ z) = (c/δ)·(δ z) + d = c z + d = denom γ z`. -/
theorem denom_divisorConj {δ : ℕ} (hδ0 : 0 < δ) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) (z : ℍ) :
    denom (divisorConj h) (natScale δ hδ0 • z : ℍ) = denom γ z := by
  rw [ModularGroup.denom_apply, ModularGroup.denom_apply]
  simp only [coe_pos_real_smul, natScale, Complex.real_smul, Complex.ofReal_natCast,
    divisorConj_one_zero, divisorConj_one_one]
  set q : ℤ := γ 1 0 / (δ : ℤ) with hq
  have hc : (δ : ℤ) * q = γ 1 0 := by rw [hq]; exact Int.mul_ediv_cancel' h
  rw [← hc]
  push_cast
  ring

/-- **The commutation identity, explicit witness.**  `δ · (γ z) = γ_δ · (δ z)` with
`γ_δ = divisorConj h`.  Both sides are Möbius quotients with the *same* denominator
(`denom_divisorConj`), so this is `δ·(az+b)/(cz+d) = (a·δz + b·δ)/((c/δ)·δz + d)` and no
non-vanishing side condition is consumed. -/
theorem divisor_smul_comm_explicit {δ : ℕ} (hδ0 : 0 < δ) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)}
    (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0) (z : ℍ) :
    (natScale δ hδ0 • (γ • z) : ℍ) = divisorConj h • (natScale δ hδ0 • z : ℍ) := by
  rw [← UpperHalfPlane.coe_inj]
  simp only [coe_pos_real_smul, natScale, Complex.real_smul, Complex.ofReal_natCast,
    coe_specialLinearGroup_apply, eq_intCast, divisorConj_zero_zero, divisorConj_zero_one,
    divisorConj_one_zero, divisorConj_one_one]
  set q : ℤ := γ 1 0 / (δ : ℤ) with hq
  have hc : (δ : ℤ) * q = γ 1 0 := by rw [hq]; exact Int.mul_ediv_cancel' h
  have hD : ((q : ℝ) : ℂ) * ((δ : ℂ) * (z : ℂ)) + (((γ 1 1 : ℤ) : ℝ) : ℂ)
      = (((γ 1 0 : ℤ) : ℝ) : ℂ) * (z : ℂ) + (((γ 1 1 : ℤ) : ℝ) : ℂ) := by
    rw [← hc]; push_cast; ring
  rw [hD, mul_div_assoc']
  congr 1
  push_cast
  ring

/-- **The commutation identity.**  For `γ = !![a,b;c,d] ∈ Γ₀(N)` and `δ ∣ N`, we have
`δ · (γ z) = γ_δ · (δ z)` with `γ_δ = !![a, b·δ; c/δ, d] ∈ SL(2,ℤ)` — the entry `c/δ` is an
integer precisely because `δ ∣ N ∣ c` — and the two matrices have the **same** `denom`,
`denom γ_δ (δ z) = denom γ z`.  Pure matrix arithmetic; no analysis.

This is the existential form the node was scoped in.  The witness is not opaque: it is
`divisorConj (dvd_lower_left_of_mem_Gamma0 hδ hγ)`, and the two explicit-witness lemmas
`divisor_smul_comm_explicit` / `denom_divisorConj` above are what is actually proved. -/
theorem divisor_smul_comm {N δ : ℕ} (hδ : δ ∣ N) (hδ0 : 0 < δ)
    {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) :
    ∃ γ' : SL(2, ℤ),
      (∀ z : ℍ, (natScale δ hδ0 • (γ • z) : ℍ) = γ' • (natScale δ hδ0 • z : ℍ)) ∧
      (∀ z : ℍ, denom γ' (natScale δ hδ0 • z : ℍ) = denom γ z) := by
  have h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0 := dvd_lower_left_of_mem_Gamma0 hδ hγ
  exact ⟨divisorConj h, fun z => divisor_smul_comm_explicit hδ0 h z,
    fun z => denom_divisorConj hδ0 h z⟩

/-! ### F3.2-A4 — `f²⁴` is `Γ₀(N)`-slash-invariant, unconditionally -/

/-- **`f²⁴` is slash-invariant of weight `12·Σr_δ` under all of `Γ₀(N)`, with no congruence
conditions.**  This is the step that dodges the multiplier system entirely: it is a formal
consequence of `etaQuotient_pow_24`, `divisor_smul_comm`, and Mathlib's
`CuspForm.discriminant : CuspForm 𝒮ℒ 12`, whose modularity is for the **full** modular group. -/
theorem coe_natScale_smul {δ : ℕ} (hδ0 : 0 < δ) (τ : ℍ) :
    ((natScale δ hδ0 • τ : ℍ) : ℂ) = (δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ) := by
  simp [coe_pos_real_smul, natScale, Complex.real_smul]

/-- **The `Δ`-modularity of a single dilated `η`-factor.**  For `0 < δ` and any `γ ∈ SL(2,ℤ)`
whose lower-left entry is divisible by `δ`,

  `η(δ · (γτ))²⁴ = (denom γ τ)¹² · η(δτ)²⁴`.

This is the ONLY analytic input of `F3.2-A4`, and it is Mathlib's, not ours: `Δ = η²⁴` is
`SL(2,ℤ)`-modular of weight `12` (`slash_action_generators_SL2Z` fed with
`discriminant_S_invariant` and `discriminant_T_invariant`), applied at the CONJUGATED matrix
`γ_δ = divisorConj h` of `F3.2-A3` and at the SCALED point `δτ`.  The two `F3.2-A3` lemmas do
the rest: `divisor_smul_comm_explicit` turns `δ · (γτ)` into `γ_δ · (δτ)`, and
`denom_divisorConj` says the automorphy denominator that comes back is `denom γ τ` — the SAME
for every `δ`, which is what lets the factors collect in `etaQuotientH_pow24_transform`.

No congruence condition on `r` and no `Γ₀(N)` appears: the hypothesis is exactly `δ ∣ c`. -/
theorem eta_pow24_natScale_smul {δ : ℕ} (hδ0 : 0 < δ) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (h : (δ : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0)
    (τ : ℍ) :
    ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * ((γ • τ : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = (denom γ τ) ^ (12 : ℕ) * ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)) ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
  have key := SlashInvariantForm.slash_action_generators_SL2Z
    ModularForm.discriminant_S_invariant ModularForm.discriminant_T_invariant (divisorConj h)
  have keyτ := congrFun key (natScale δ hδ0 • τ : ℍ)
  rw [ModularForm.SL_slash_apply] at keyτ
  rw [← divisor_smul_comm_explicit hδ0 h τ, denom_divisorConj hδ0 h τ] at keyτ
  simp only [ModularForm.discriminant, coe_natScale_smul hδ0] at keyτ
  have hd : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) τ) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ τ
  rw [← keyτ, show ((-12 : ℤ)) = -((12 : ℕ) : ℤ) by norm_num, _root_.zpow_neg, zpow_natCast]
  field_simp

/-- `f²⁴` factorwise, in `Δ`-shape: `f(τ)²⁴ = ∏_δ (η(δτ)²⁴)^{r_δ} = ∏_δ Δ(δτ)^{r_δ}`.
The `ℍ`-domain companion of `etaQuotient_pow_24`, with the `24` kept on the INSIDE of the
integer exponent so that each factor is literally a value of Mathlib's `Δ`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_pow_24 {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) (τ : ℍ) :
    (etaQuotientH N r τ) ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)) ^ (24 : ℕ)) ^ (r δ) := by
  simp only [etaQuotientH, etaQuotient, ← Finset.prod_pow]
  exact Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ _ => zpow_pow24_comm _ _

/-- **The pointwise form of `F3.2-A4`.**  For every `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` and every `τ ∈ ℍ`,

  `f(γτ)²⁴ = (cτ + d)^{12·Σr_δ} · f(τ)²⁴`,

with NO congruence condition on `r` and NO hypothesis on `N`.  Each factor transforms by
`eta_pow24_natScale_smul`; because the `denom` that comes back is `denom γ τ` for EVERY `δ`
(that is `denom_divisorConj`, F3.2-A3), the factors collect into a single power
`(denom γ τ)^{12·Σr_δ}` by `prod_zpow_const`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_pow24_transform {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N)
    (τ : ℍ) :
    (etaQuotientH N r ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) • τ)) ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = (denom γ τ) ^ (12 * ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ) * (etaQuotientH N r τ) ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
  have hd : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) τ) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ τ
  have step : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors,
      (ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * ((γ • τ : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ (24 : ℕ)) ^ (r δ)
        = ((denom γ τ) ^ (12 : ℕ)) ^ (r δ)
          * (ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (τ : ℂ)) ^ (24 : ℕ)) ^ (r δ) := by
    intro δ hδ
    rw [eta_pow24_natScale_smul (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ)
      (dvd_lower_left_of_mem_Gamma0 (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ) hγ) τ, mul_zpow]
  rw [etaQuotientH_pow_24, etaQuotientH_pow_24, Finset.prod_congr rfl step,
    Finset.prod_mul_distrib, prod_zpow_const _ (pow_ne_zero 12 hd) r, pow_twelve_zpow]

theorem etaQuotient_pow24_slash {N : ℕ} (_hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp)
    {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) :
    ((etaQuotientH N r) ^ (24 : ℕ)) ∣[(12 * ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ : ℤ)] (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ)
      = (etaQuotientH N r) ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
  -- `SL_slash` identifies the `GL`-slash at the coerced matrix with the `SL(2,ℤ)`-slash, where
  -- `SL_slash_apply` is `(F ∣[k] γ) τ = F(γτ) · denom γ τ ^ (-k)`; the pointwise law
  -- `etaQuotientH_pow24_transform` then cancels the two `denom` powers exactly (`zpow_add₀`,
  -- legal because `denom γ τ ≠ 0`).
  -- HONEST NOTE: `_hN : 0 < N` is NOT used.  At `N = 0` the divisor set is empty, `f ≡ 1` and
  -- the weight is `0`, so the statement is true but vacuous there.  The hypothesis is kept
  -- because it is verbatim the scoped signature.
  rw [← ModularForm.SL_slash]
  funext τ
  rw [ModularForm.SL_slash_apply]
  have hd : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) τ) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ τ
  simp only [Pi.pow_apply]
  rw [etaQuotientH_pow24_transform r hγ τ, mul_right_comm, ← zpow_add₀ hd, add_neg_cancel,
    zpow_zero, one_mul]

/-! ### F3.2-A5 — the multiplier is a character of order dividing 24

THE KEYSTONE, and the only step of the `F3.2` ladder that is genuinely *soft*: it produces the
multiplier for EVERY `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` without ever evaluating it, and therefore without Dedekind
sums.  What is proved is EXISTENCE plus two structural facts — the values are 24th roots of
unity, and `γ ↦ w γ` is a group homomorphism.  What is NOT proved, here or anywhere in this
file, is the VALUE of `w γ` for a general `γ`; that is the Dedekind-sum obstruction, and it is
untouched by this block.

Every analytic input is already landed: `etaQuotientH_pow24_transform` (F3.2-A4) makes the ratio
a 24th root of unity, `etaQuotientH_ne_zero` (F3.1-B7) makes it well defined,
`differentiableAt_etaQuotient` (F3.1-B8) makes it continuous.  The only new external input is
topological and it is Mathlib's: `ℍ` is connected (`ContractibleSpace ℍ`,
`Analysis/Complex/UpperHalfPlane/Topology.lean`).  No congruence condition on `r` is used, and
`0 < N` is not used either. -/

/-- The 24th roots of unity are a FINITE subset of `ℂ`: they are among the roots of `X²⁴ - 1`
(`Polynomial.nthRoots`), and a multiset has finitely many members. -/
theorem finite_setOf_pow24_eq_one : ({x : ℂ | x ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1}).Finite := by
  have hsub : {x : ℂ | x ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1} ⊆ {x : ℂ | x ∈ Polynomial.nthRoots 24 (1 : ℂ)} :=
    fun _ hx => (Polynomial.mem_nthRoots (by norm_num)).mpr hx
  exact Set.Finite.subset (Multiset.finite_toSet _) hsub

/-- **The constancy engine.**  A continuous function on `ℍ` all of whose values are 24th roots of
unity is constant.  This is the whole topological content of `F3.2-A5`, isolated so that it can
be read without any `η` in sight: the 24th roots of unity are finite (`finite_setOf_pow24_eq_one`)
hence carry the discrete topology (`Finite.instDiscreteTopology`, `ℂ` being `T1`), and `ℍ` is
preconnected (Mathlib's `ContractibleSpace ℍ` instance), so `PreconnectedSpace.constant`
applies to the corestriction. -/
theorem eq_of_continuous_pow24_eq_one {g : ℍ → ℂ} (hg : Continuous g)
    (hpow : ∀ z, g z ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1) (z w : ℍ) : g z = g w := by
  haveI : Finite {x : ℂ // x ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1} := finite_setOf_pow24_eq_one.to_subtype
  have hG : Continuous (fun z : ℍ => (⟨g z, hpow z⟩ : {x : ℂ // x ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1})) :=
    hg.subtype_mk _
  exact congrArg Subtype.val
    (PreconnectedSpace.constant (α := ℍ) inferInstance hG (x := z) (y := w))

/-- An eta quotient is CONTINUOUS on `ℍ`.  Immediate from holomorphy (`F3.1-B8`) along the
continuous coercion `ℍ → ℂ`; stated separately because the constancy engine wants `Continuous`
on the bundled `ℍ`, not `DifferentiableAt` on `ℂ`. -/
theorem continuous_etaQuotientH (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) : Continuous (etaQuotientH N r) := by
  rw [continuous_iff_continuousAt]
  intro z
  exact ((differentiableAt_etaQuotient r z.coe_im_pos).continuousAt).comp
    UpperHalfPlane.continuous_coe.continuousAt

/-- `z ↦ γ • z` is continuous on `ℍ` for `γ ∈ SL(2,ℤ)`.  Mathlib supplies
`ContinuousConstSMul (GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ℍ`; the `SL(2,ℤ)`-action is `MulAction.compHom` along
`SpecialLinearGroup.mapGL ℝ`, so the two actions agree definitionally (`rfl`). -/
theorem continuous_SL_smul (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : Continuous (fun z : ℍ => γ • z) := by
  have h : (fun z : ℍ => γ • z) = fun z : ℍ => (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) • z := rfl
  rw [h]
  exact continuous_const_smul _

/-- `z ↦ denom γ z = c z + d` is continuous on `ℍ`. -/
theorem continuous_denom_SL (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) :
    Continuous (fun z : ℍ => denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) := by
  unfold UpperHalfPlane.denom
  exact (continuous_const.mul UpperHalfPlane.continuous_coe).add continuous_const

/-- **The automorphy cocycle for `SL(2,ℤ)`**, `denom (γ₁γ₂) z = denom γ₁ (γ₂ z) · denom γ₂ z`.
This is Mathlib's `UpperHalfPlane.denom_cocycle` transported along the coercion
`SL(2,ℤ) → GL (Fin 2) ℝ` (a `MonoidHom`, so `map_mul` applies) and along
`coe_smul_of_det_pos`, which is where `det = 1 > 0` is used: for positive determinant the
Möbius action is the honest quotient `num/denom` with no complex conjugation.

It is the cocycle that makes the multiplier a HOMOMORPHISM rather than merely a function, and
it is exact — no branch ambiguity — precisely because the weight `k` is an INTEGER. -/
theorem denom_SL_mul (γ₁ γ₂ : SL(2, ℤ)) (z : ℍ) :
    denom ((γ₁ * γ₂ : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)
      = denom (γ₁ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((γ₂ • z : ℍ) : ℂ) * denom (γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) := by
  have hdet : (0 : ℝ) < (GeneralLinearGroup.det ((γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ))).val := by simp
  have h2 : ((γ₂ • z : ℍ) : ℂ)
      = num (γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) / denom (γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) :=
    coe_smul_of_det_pos hdet z
  have hm : ((γ₁ * γ₂ : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ)
      = (γ₁ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) * (γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) := map_mul (SpecialLinearGroup.mapGL ℝ) _ _
  rw [h2, hm]
  exact denom_cocycle _ _ z.im_ne_zero

/-- The candidate multiplier, as a function of BOTH `γ` and `z`:
`ρ(γ, z) = f(γz) / ((denom γ z)^k · f(z))`.  Well defined everywhere on `ℍ` because `f` is
nowhere zero (`F3.1-B7`) and `denom γ z ≠ 0`. -/
noncomputable def etaMultiplierAux (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (k : ℤ) (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) (z : ℍ) : ℂ :=
  etaQuotientH N r (γ • z) / ((denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z)

/-- **`ρ(γ, z)²⁴ = 1`**, for every `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` and every `z`.  This is `F3.2-A4`
(`etaQuotientH_pow24_transform`, `f(γz)²⁴ = (denom γ z)^{12·Σr_δ} f(z)²⁴`) divided by
`((denom γ z)^k)²⁴ = (denom γ z)^{12·(2k)}`, and the two exponents agree EXACTLY because
`Σ_δ r_δ = 2k`.  This is the only place the hypothesis `hk` is used, and it is where the
integrality of `k` earns its keep: with `k` a half-integer the exponent `12·Σr_δ` would not be
`24k` and there would be no exact cancellation. -/
theorem etaMultiplierAux_pow24 {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ) :
    (etaMultiplierAux N r k γ z) ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1 := by
  have hd : denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ z
  have hf : etaQuotientH N r z ≠ 0 := etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r z
  have h24 := etaQuotientH_pow24_transform r hγ z
  rw [hk] at h24
  have hDk : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) ^ k) ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) ^ (12 * (2 * k)) := by
    rw [← zpow_natCast (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) ^ k) 24, ← _root_.zpow_mul]
    congr 1
    push_cast
    ring
  unfold etaMultiplierAux
  rw [div_pow, mul_pow, h24, hDk]
  exact div_self (mul_ne_zero (zpow_ne_zero _ hd) (pow_ne_zero _ hf))

/-- **The multiplier does not depend on `z`.**  `ρ(γ, ·)` is continuous and takes values in the
24 roots of unity, and `ℍ` is connected; `eq_of_continuous_pow24_eq_one` finishes.  No property
of `η` beyond continuity and non-vanishing is used. -/
theorem etaMultiplierAux_const {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (z w : ℍ) :
    etaMultiplierAux N r k γ z = etaMultiplierAux N r k γ w := by
  refine eq_of_continuous_pow24_eq_one ?_ (etaMultiplierAux_pow24 r hk hγ) z w
  refine Continuous.div ((continuous_etaQuotientH N r).comp (continuous_SL_smul γ))
    (((continuous_denom_SL γ).zpow₀ k (fun a => Or.inl (denom_ne_zero _ a))).mul
      (continuous_etaQuotientH N r)) ?_
  intro a
  exact mul_ne_zero (zpow_ne_zero _ (denom_ne_zero _ a)) (etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r a)

/-- The multiplier itself: `ρ(γ, ·)` evaluated at the base point `i`.  Choosing a base point
rather than invoking choice is deliberate — `map_mul` below is then a computation at `i`, not a
manipulation of an opaque witness. -/
noncomputable def etaMultiplierVal (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (k : ℤ) (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : ℂ :=
  etaMultiplierAux N r k γ UpperHalfPlane.I

theorem etaMultiplierVal_pow24 {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) :
    (etaMultiplierVal N r k γ) ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1 :=
  etaMultiplierAux_pow24 r hk hγ _

theorem etaMultiplierVal_ne_zero {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) :
    etaMultiplierVal N r k γ ≠ 0 := by
  intro h
  have h24 := etaMultiplierVal_pow24 r hk hγ
  rw [h] at h24
  simp at h24

/-- **The transformation law with a `z`-INDEPENDENT multiplier**: for `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` and every
`z : ℍ`, `f(γz) = w(γ) · (denom γ z)^k · f(z)`.  This is `etaMultiplierAux_const` cleared of
denominators; it is the statement `F3.2-A6` and `F3.2-A8` will specialise to `T` and `V`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_transform {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (γ • z)
      = etaMultiplierVal N r k γ * (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k
        * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  have h := (etaMultiplierAux_const r hk hγ z UpperHalfPlane.I).symm
  unfold etaMultiplierVal
  rw [h]
  unfold etaMultiplierAux
  have hd : denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ z
  have hf : etaQuotientH N r z ≠ 0 := etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r z
  field_simp

/-- `w(1) = 1`: at the identity `1 • z = z` and `denom 1 z = 1`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_one {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) (k : ℤ) :
    etaMultiplierVal N r k (1 : SL(2, ℤ)) = 1 := by
  have h1 : ((1 : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) = 1 := map_one (SpecialLinearGroup.mapGL ℝ)
  unfold etaMultiplierVal etaMultiplierAux
  rw [one_smul, h1, denom_one, _root_.one_zpow, one_mul]
  exact div_self (etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r _)

/-- `w(γ₁γ₂) = w(γ₁)·w(γ₂)`.  Evaluate the transformation law three times at the base point `i`
— once at `γ₁γ₂`, once at `γ₁` applied to the point `γ₂ i`, once at `γ₂` — and use the cocycle
`denom_SL_mul` together with `mul_zpow` (legal for an INTEGER exponent on nonzero complex
numbers).  The three `denom` powers and `f(i)` then cancel. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_mul {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ₁ γ₂ : SL(2, ℤ)}
    (hγ₁ : γ₁ ∈ Gamma0 N) (hγ₂ : γ₂ ∈ Gamma0 N) :
    etaMultiplierVal N r k (γ₁ * γ₂)
      = etaMultiplierVal N r k γ₁ * etaMultiplierVal N r k γ₂ := by
  set z : ℍ := UpperHalfPlane.I with hz
  have hd1 : denom (γ₁ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((γ₂ • z : ℍ) : ℂ) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ _
  have hd2 : denom (γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ z
  have hf : etaQuotientH N r z ≠ 0 := etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r z
  have e1 : etaQuotientH N r ((γ₁ * γ₂) • z)
      = etaMultiplierVal N r k (γ₁ * γ₂)
        * (denom ((γ₁ * γ₂ : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z :=
    etaQuotientH_transform r hk (mul_mem hγ₁ hγ₂) z
  have e2 : etaQuotientH N r (γ₁ • (γ₂ • z))
      = etaMultiplierVal N r k γ₁ * (denom (γ₁ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((γ₂ • z : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ k
        * etaQuotientH N r (γ₂ • z) :=
    etaQuotientH_transform r hk hγ₁ (γ₂ • z)
  have e3 : etaQuotientH N r (γ₂ • z)
      = etaMultiplierVal N r k γ₂ * (denom (γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k
        * etaQuotientH N r z :=
    etaQuotientH_transform r hk hγ₂ z
  rw [mul_smul, e2, e3] at e1
  rw [denom_SL_mul γ₁ γ₂ z, mul_zpow] at e1
  have hne : (denom (γ₁ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((γ₂ • z : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ k
      * ((denom (γ₂ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z) ≠ 0 :=
    mul_ne_zero (zpow_ne_zero _ hd1) (mul_ne_zero (zpow_ne_zero _ hd2) hf)
  field_simp at e1
  linear_combination -e1

/-- **The eta multiplier as a genuine character `Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ`.**  The values land in `ℂˣ`
because `f` is nowhere zero; `map_one'` and `map_mul'` are the two lemmas above. -/
noncomputable def etaMultiplierHom {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) : Gamma0 N →* ℂˣ where
  toFun γ := Units.mk0 (etaMultiplierVal N r k (γ : SL(2, ℤ))) (etaMultiplierVal_ne_zero r hk γ.2)
  map_one' := by
    apply Units.ext
    simpa using etaMultiplierVal_one r k
  map_mul' γ₁ γ₂ := by
    apply Units.ext
    simpa using etaMultiplierVal_mul r hk γ₁.2 γ₂.2

/-- **The keystone.**  For every `N` and every exponent vector of even weight-sum, the eta
quotient transforms under `Γ₀(N)` with a genuine character `w` of order dividing 24 — and
**no congruence conditions are needed**.

PROVED.  By `etaQuotient_pow24_slash` (F3.2-A4) the ratio
`ρ(γ, z) = f(γz) / ((denom γ z)^k · f(z))` satisfies `ρ^24 = 1`, so for fixed `γ` it is a
continuous map from the connected (indeed contractible) upper half-plane into the discrete
group `μ₂₄`, hence constant.  Integer weight `k` makes `(denom γ z)^k` an exact cocycle, so
`γ ↦ ρ(γ)` is a homomorphism.

READ THE SCOPE (LL-1).  What is asserted is the EXISTENCE of the multiplier together with its
two structural properties.  Nothing here computes `w γ` for any particular `γ` other than `1`,
and nothing here mentions Ligozat's congruences.  The witness is not opaque — it is
`etaMultiplierHom r hk`, whose value at `γ` is `f(γ·i)/((denom γ i)^k · f(i))` — but the
Dedekind-sum obstruction (`F3.2-OBSTRUCTED`) is exactly the question of what that number is for
a general `γ`, and it is untouched.

HONEST NOTE: `_hN : 0 < N` is NOT used.  At `N = 0` the divisor set is empty, `f ≡ 1` and `hk`
forces `k = 0`, so the statement holds with `w = 1`; the hypothesis is kept because it is
verbatim the scoped signature. -/
theorem etaQuotient_multiplier {N : ℕ} (_hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) :
    ∃ w : Gamma0 N →* ℂˣ,
      (∀ γ : Gamma0 N, (w γ) ^ (24 : ℕ) = 1) ∧
      ∀ (γ : Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ),
        etaQuotientH N r ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) • z)
          = (w γ : ℂ) * (denom (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) z) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  refine ⟨etaMultiplierHom r hk, fun γ => ?_, fun γ z => ?_⟩
  · apply Units.ext
    simpa [etaMultiplierHom] using etaMultiplierVal_pow24 r hk γ.2
  · exact etaQuotientH_transform r hk γ.2 z

/-! #### F3.2-A5 — the LL-1 external guard

A green build can prove the WRONG theorem, and the shape `f(γz) = w(γ)·(cz+d)^k·f(z)` is
exactly the shape in which a wrong weight (`k` vs `2k`), a wrong automorphy factor, or a
multiplier that silently absorbed a `denom` power would still elaborate.  The guard below pins
the normalisation against an object Mathlib certified: at `N = 1`, `r ≡ 24`, `k = 12` the eta
quotient IS `Δ` (`etaQuotientH_level_one_24`, F3.1-B10), `Δ` is `SL(2,ℤ)`-modular of weight 12,
and therefore the multiplier produced by `F3.2-A5` must be exactly `1`.  If the weight
convention drifts by a factor 2, or if `etaMultiplierVal` picks up a stray factor, this stops
compiling. -/

/-- `Δ` under `SL(2,ℤ)`, written as a statement about the level-`1` eta quotient:
`f(γz) = (denom γ z)^12 f(z)`.  Mathlib's, not ours — `discriminant_S_invariant` and
`discriminant_T_invariant` through `slash_action_generators_SL2Z`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_level_one_24_transform (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (γ • z)
      = (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ (12 : ℤ)
        * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) z := by
  have key := SlashInvariantForm.slash_action_generators_SL2Z
    ModularForm.discriminant_S_invariant ModularForm.discriminant_T_invariant γ
  have keyτ := congrFun key z
  rw [ModularForm.SL_slash_apply] at keyτ
  have hd : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ z
  rw [etaQuotientH_level_one_24, etaQuotientH_level_one_24, ← keyτ]
  field_simp

/-- The level-`1` discriminant vector has weight-sum `24 = 2·12`, so `k = 12` is the `F3.2-A5`
weight for it. -/
theorem sum_divisors_one_24 : ∑ δ ∈ (1 : ℕ).divisors, (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) δ = 2 * 12 := by
  simp [Nat.divisors_one]

/-- `Γ₀(1) = SL(2,ℤ)`: the congruence is a statement in `ZMod 1`, which is a subsingleton. -/
theorem mem_Gamma0_one (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : γ ∈ Gamma0 1 :=
  Gamma0_mem.mpr (Subsingleton.elim _ _)

/-- **LL-1 GUARD for F3.2-A5.**  At `N = 1`, `r ≡ 24`, `k = 12` — where the eta quotient is
Mathlib's `Δ` — the multiplier produced by `F3.2-A5` is `1` on ALL of `SL(2,ℤ) = Γ₀(1)`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_level_one_24 (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) :
    etaMultiplierVal 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) 12 γ = 1 := by
  have h1 := etaQuotientH_transform (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) sum_divisors_one_24 (mem_Gamma0_one γ)
    UpperHalfPlane.I
  have h2 := etaQuotientH_level_one_24_transform γ UpperHalfPlane.I
  rw [h2] at h1
  have hd : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ _
  have hf : etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) UpperHalfPlane.I ≠ 0 :=
    etaQuotientH_ne_zero _ _ _
  have hne : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ (12 : ℤ)
      * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) UpperHalfPlane.I ≠ 0 :=
    mul_ne_zero (zpow_ne_zero _ hd) hf
  have h3 : etaMultiplierVal 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) 12 γ
      * ((denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ (12 : ℤ)
        * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) UpperHalfPlane.I)
      = 1 * ((denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ (12 : ℤ)
        * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) UpperHalfPlane.I) := by
    rw [one_mul, ← mul_assoc]
    exact h1.symm
  exact mul_right_cancel₀ hne h3

/-! ### F3.2-A6 — Ligozat congruence (i) ⟺ the multiplier is trivial on `T` -/

/-- `T = !![1,1;0,1]` lies in `Γ₀(N)` for every `N`, since its lower-left entry is `0`.
`Gamma0_mem` reduces membership to `((T 1 0 : ℤ) : ZMod N) = 0` and `ModularGroup.coe_T`
evaluates that entry to `0`. -/
theorem T_mem_Gamma0 (N : ℕ) : ModularGroup.T ∈ Gamma0 N := by
  rw [Gamma0_mem]
  simp [ModularGroup.coe_T]

/-- **The `T`-transformation of an eta quotient**: `f(z+1) = e^{πi(Σ_δ δ·r_δ)/12} f(z)`.

Factorwise from `F3.1-B3` (`eta_add_int`), applied at the point `δz` and the integer `δ`:
`(δ:ℂ)(z+1) = δz + δ`, so `η(δ(z+1)) = e^{2πiδ/24} η(δz) = e^{πiδ/12} η(δz)`.  Raising to the
INTEGER exponent `r δ` uses `mul_zpow` (unconditional in `ℂ`) and `Complex.exp_int_mul`
backwards, which turns `(e^c)^{r_δ}` into `e^{r_δ c}`; `Finset.prod_mul_distrib` and
`Complex.exp_sum` then collect the constants into a single exponential, and the exponent
`Σ_δ πi·δ·r_δ/12` is `πi(Σ_δ δ r_δ)/12` by `Finset.mul_sum` / `Finset.sum_div`.

HONEST NOTE: the hypothesis `_hz : z ∈ ℍₒ` is **NOT used** — `eta_add_int` holds for every
`z : ℂ`, and every step above is `zpow`/`exp` algebra valid at any base.  It is kept (with the
binder renamed to mark it) because it is verbatim the scoped signature of the node, and
weakening a hypothesis is a change of statement even when it strengthens the theorem.

SCOPE: this is `f` under the SINGLE matrix `T`.  It is not the eta multiplier system and says
nothing about a general `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)`. -/
theorem etaQuotient_T_transform {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {z : ℂ} (_hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    etaQuotient N r (z + 1)
      = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I * ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12)
        * etaQuotient N r z := by
  have key : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors,
      ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (z + 1)) ^ (r δ)
        = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I * (((δ : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12)
          * ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * z) ^ (r δ) := by
    intro δ _
    have hstep : (δ : ℂ) * (z + 1) = (δ : ℂ) * z + (((δ : ℕ) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by push_cast; ring
    rw [hstep, eta_add_int, mul_zpow, ← Complex.exp_int_mul]
    congr 2
    push_cast
    ring
  have hc : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((π : ℂ) * I * (((δ : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12)
      = (π : ℂ) * I * ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12 := by
    push_cast
    rw [Finset.mul_sum, Finset.sum_div]
  simp only [etaQuotient]
  rw [Finset.prod_congr rfl key, Finset.prod_mul_distrib, ← Complex.exp_sum, hc]

/-- `T` acts on `ℍ` by `z ↦ z + 1` (Mathlib's `modular_T_smul`, in coordinates). -/
theorem coe_T_smul (z : ℍ) : ((ModularGroup.T • z : ℍ) : ℂ) = (z : ℂ) + 1 := by
  rw [UpperHalfPlane.modular_T_smul]
  push_cast [UpperHalfPlane.coe_vadd]
  ring

/-- `etaQuotient_T_transform` transported to the bundled upper half-plane, where the slash
action lives: `f(T·z) = e^{πi(Σ_δ δ r_δ)/12} f(z)`, with no hypothesis at all. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_T_smul {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (ModularGroup.T • z)
      = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I * ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12)
        * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  rw [etaQuotientH, etaQuotientH, coe_T_smul, etaQuotient_T_transform r z.coe_im_pos]

/-- **Ligozat's congruence (i) is exactly triviality of the multiplier on `T`.**  Since
`denom T z = 1`, "the multiplier at `T` is `1`" is literally `f(Tz) = f(z)`.

`h24 : 24 ∣ Σ_δ δ r_δ` supplies `m` with `Σ_δ δ r_δ = 24m`, and then the constant of
`etaQuotientH_T_smul` is `e^{πi·24m/12} = e^{m·2πi} = 1` (`Complex.exp_int_mul_two_pi_mul_I`). -/
theorem multiplier_T_eq_one {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) (h24 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (ModularGroup.T • z) = etaQuotientH N r z := by
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := h24
  rw [etaQuotientH_T_smul, hm]
  have h : (π : ℂ) * I * (((24 * m : ℤ)) : ℂ) / 12 = (m : ℂ) * (2 * (π : ℂ) * I) := by
    push_cast
    ring
  rw [h, Complex.exp_int_mul_two_pi_mul_I, one_mul]

/-- `denom T z = 0·z + 1 = 1`.  This is what makes "`f(Tz) = f(z)`" and "`w(T) = 1`" the same
sentence: with a trivial automorphy factor the weight `k` drops out of the `T`-statement. -/
theorem denom_T (z : ℍ) : denom (ModularGroup.T : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) = 1 := by
  rw [ModularGroup.denom_apply]
  simp [ModularGroup.coe_T]

/-- **`w(T) = 1`**, in the multiplier the keystone `F3.2-A5` actually produces.  This is the
node's statement in the form it was scoped: not "`f` is `1`-periodic" but "the character
`w : Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ` takes the value `1` at `T`".  The two are equivalent only because
`denom T z = 1` (`denom_T`), which is why that lemma is proved rather than assumed. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_T_eq_one {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h24 : LigozatCongr1 N r) :
    etaMultiplierVal N r k ModularGroup.T = 1 := by
  have h := etaQuotientH_transform r hk (T_mem_Gamma0 N) UpperHalfPlane.I
  rw [multiplier_T_eq_one r h24, denom_T, _root_.one_zpow, mul_one] at h
  refine mul_right_cancel₀ (etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r UpperHalfPlane.I) ?_
  rw [one_mul]
  exact h.symm

/-- The same statement for the BUNDLED character `etaMultiplierHom r hk : Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ`:
`w ⟨T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1` in `ℂˣ`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierHom_T_eq_one {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h24 : LigozatCongr1 N r) :
    etaMultiplierHom r hk ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1 := by
  apply Units.ext
  simpa [etaMultiplierHom] using etaMultiplierVal_T_eq_one r hk h24

/-- **"EXACTLY", proved as an iff.**  Ligozat's congruence (i) does not merely *imply*
`T`-invariance of the eta quotient — it is EQUIVALENT to it.  The converse is what makes the
word "exactly" in the node description a theorem rather than a slogan: if `f(Tz) = f(z)` at even
the single point `z = i`, then `e^{πiS/12} = 1` (divide by `f(i) ≠ 0`, `F3.1-B7`), so
`πiS/12 = n·2πi` for some integer `n` (`Complex.exp_eq_one_iff`), and cancelling the nonzero
`πi` gives `S = 24n`.  In particular `η` itself (level `1`, `r ≡ 1`, `S = 1`) is NOT
`1`-periodic — recorded below as `eta_not_T_invariant`. -/
theorem ligozatCongr1_iff_T_invariant {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) :
    LigozatCongr1 N r ↔ ∀ z : ℍ, etaQuotientH N r (ModularGroup.T • z) = etaQuotientH N r z := by
  refine ⟨fun h24 => multiplier_T_eq_one r h24, fun hT => ?_⟩
  have h := (etaQuotientH_T_smul (N := N) r UpperHalfPlane.I).symm
  rw [hT UpperHalfPlane.I] at h
  have hf : etaQuotientH N r UpperHalfPlane.I ≠ 0 := etaQuotientH_ne_zero _ _ _
  have hone : Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I
      * ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12) = 1 := by
    refine mul_right_cancel₀ hf ?_
    rw [one_mul]
    exact h
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := Complex.exp_eq_one_iff.mp hone
  refine ⟨n, ?_⟩
  have hpi : (π : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by exact_mod_cast Real.pi_ne_zero
  have hI : (I : ℂ) ≠ 0 := Complex.I_ne_zero
  have hcast : ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) = ((24 * n : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
    push_cast
    field_simp at hn
    push_cast at hn
    linear_combination hn
  exact_mod_cast hcast

/-! #### F3.2-A6 — the LL-1 guards

Four checks that the constant `e^{πi(Σ δ r_δ)/12}` really carries the factor `δ`, and that the
`T`-statement is not vacuously true.  Each is a theorem, not a `#guard`, because the objects are
transcendental and nothing here reduces in the kernel. -/

/-- GUARD 1 (the constant).  At `N = 1`, `r ≡ 1` the eta quotient is `η` itself, and the
constant must be `e^{πi/12}` — F3.2-A1's `eta_T_transform`.  Pins the normalisation. -/
theorem etaQuotient_T_transform_level_one_pin {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    etaQuotient 1 (fun _ => (1 : ℤ)) (z + 1)
      = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I / 12) * etaQuotient 1 (fun _ => (1 : ℤ)) z := by
  have h := etaQuotient_T_transform (N := 1) (fun _ => (1 : ℤ)) hz
  simpa [show (1 : ℕ).divisors = {1} from by decide] using h

/-- GUARD 2 (the factor `δ`, which is the whole content of congruence (i)).  At `N = 2` with
`f(z) = η(2z)` the constant must be `e^{2πi/12}`, not `e^{πi/12}`: a transcription that summed
`r_δ` instead of `δ·r_δ` dies here. -/
theorem etaQuotient_T_transform_level_two_pin {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    etaQuotient 2 (fun δ => if δ = 2 then (1 : ℤ) else 0) (z + 1)
      = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I * 2 / 12)
        * etaQuotient 2 (fun δ => if δ = 2 then (1 : ℤ) else 0) z := by
  have h := etaQuotient_T_transform (N := 2) (fun δ => if δ = 2 then (1 : ℤ) else 0) hz
  norm_num [show (2 : ℕ).divisors = {1, 2} from by decide] at h
  exact h

/-- `Δ = η²⁴` satisfies congruence (i): `Σ_δ δ r_δ = 24`. -/
theorem ligozatCongr1_level_one_24 : LigozatCongr1 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) := by
  simp [LigozatCongr1, show (1 : ℕ).divisors = {1} from by decide]

/-- GUARD 3 (external, against Mathlib).  Our route gives `Δ(z+1) = Δ(z)`… -/
theorem discriminant_T_via_multiplier (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (ModularGroup.T • z)
      = etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) z :=
  multiplier_T_eq_one _ ligozatCongr1_level_one_24 z

-- …and so does Mathlib's own full-level modularity of `Δ` (`etaQuotientH_level_one_24_transform`
-- at `γ = T`, where `denom_T` kills the automorphy factor).  Two independent proofs of one
-- statement: if our constant `e^{πi·24/12}` were not `1`, they would disagree.
example (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (ModularGroup.T • z)
      = etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) z := by
  rw [etaQuotientH_level_one_24_transform, denom_T, _root_.one_zpow, one_mul]

/-- GUARD 4 (NON-VACUITY).  `η` is NOT `1`-periodic.  Via `ligozatCongr1_iff_T_invariant` this
is exactly `¬ (24 ∣ 1)`, so it certifies that the `T`-invariance conclusion is a real
restriction on `r` and that the iff above is not a tautology. -/
theorem eta_not_T_invariant :
    ¬ (∀ z : ℍ, etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (1 : ℤ)) (ModularGroup.T • z)
        = etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (1 : ℤ)) z) := by
  rw [← ligozatCongr1_iff_T_invariant]
  intro h
  norm_num [LigozatCongr1, show (1 : ℕ).divisors = {1} from by decide] at h

/-! ### F3.2-A7 — the Fricke involution sends `f` to its dual quotient

The four helper blocks below are, in order: `Complex.sqrt` algebra (`csqrt_sq`,
`csqrt_ne_zero`, `csqrt_ofReal_of_nonneg`, `csqrt_ofReal_mul`), the `zpow` bookkeeping that
turns a square root raised to `2k` into a `k`-th power (`csqrt_zpow_two_mul`,
`csqrt_inv_zpow_two_mul`), the **real** square-root product identity that keeps the whole
branch question inside `ℝ` (`prod_sqrt_zpow`, `prod_sqrt_div_divisors`), and the divisor
involution on the `η`-product (`prod_eta_div_divisors`).  The single analytic input is
Mathlib's `ModularForm.eta_comp_eq_csqrt_I_inv`; nothing here reproves it. -/

/-- `(√x)² = x` for Mathlib's `Complex.sqrt`, **unconditionally**: `Complex.sqrt x = x ^ (2⁻¹)`
as a `cpow`, and `Complex.cpow_nat_inv_pow` needs only `n ≠ 0`, not `x ≠ 0`.  This is the one
fact about the branch that the whole node rests on, and it holds at every `x : ℂ`. -/
theorem csqrt_sq (x : ℂ) : (Complex.sqrt x) ^ (2 : ℕ) = x := by
  have h := Complex.cpow_nat_inv_pow x (n := 2) (by norm_num)
  simpa [Complex.sqrt] using h

/-- `√x ≠ 0` when `x ≠ 0` — immediate from `csqrt_sq`.  Needed because the exponents `r δ` are
integers of arbitrary sign, so every `zpow` collection step below wants a nonzero base. -/
theorem csqrt_ne_zero {x : ℂ} (hx : x ≠ 0) : Complex.sqrt x ≠ 0 := by
  intro h
  apply hx
  rw [← csqrt_sq x, h]
  ring

/-- On the nonnegative reals `Complex.sqrt` IS `Real.sqrt`: `√(m : ℂ) = ((√m : ℝ) : ℂ)`.
`Real.sqrt_eq_rpow` plus `Complex.ofReal_cpow`, both of which need `0 ≤ m`. -/
theorem csqrt_ofReal_of_nonneg {m : ℝ} (hm : 0 ≤ m) :
    Complex.sqrt (m : ℂ) = ((Real.sqrt m : ℝ) : ℂ) := by
  rw [Complex.sqrt, Real.sqrt_eq_rpow, Complex.ofReal_cpow hm]
  norm_num

/-- **The branch-clean scaling law**, and the reason the constant in `etaQuotient_fricke` can be
stated with a *real* `Real.sqrt`: for `m : ℝ` with `0 < m` and any `z ≠ 0`,

  `√(m·z) = √m · √z`   (`Complex.sqrt` on the left and right, `Real.sqrt` in the middle).

This is FALSE for a general complex scalar — `√(w·z) = √w·√z` fails whenever `arg w + arg z`
leaves `(-π, π]`.  It holds here because multiplying by a POSITIVE REAL does not move the
argument at all: `Complex.log_ofReal_mul` splits the logarithm with no `2πi` correction, and
`Complex.sqrt = exp (log · / 2)` then splits as a product.  Every use below has `m = N/δ`, a
positive natural number, so the hypothesis is free. -/
theorem csqrt_ofReal_mul {m : ℝ} (hm : 0 < m) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ≠ 0) :
    Complex.sqrt ((m : ℂ) * z) = ((Real.sqrt m : ℝ) : ℂ) * Complex.sqrt z := by
  have hm0 : (m : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by exact_mod_cast hm.ne'
  rw [sqrt_eq_exp (mul_ne_zero hm0 hz), Complex.log_ofReal_mul hm hz, add_div, Complex.exp_add,
    ← sqrt_eq_exp hz]
  congr 1
  rw [← csqrt_ofReal_of_nonneg hm.le, sqrt_eq_exp hm0, Complex.ofReal_log hm.le]

/-- `(√x)^{2k} = x^k` for INTEGER `k`.  This is where the integrality of the weight is
load-bearing: `2k` is an even integer, so the square root disappears with no residual branch
choice.  For a half-integral weight there is no such statement. -/
theorem csqrt_zpow_two_mul (x : ℂ) (k : ℤ) : (Complex.sqrt x) ^ (2 * k) = x ^ k := by
  rw [_root_.zpow_mul]
  congr 1
  rw [show (2 : ℤ) = ((2 : ℕ) : ℤ) by norm_num, zpow_natCast, csqrt_sq]

/-- `((√x)⁻¹)^{2k} = x^{-k}` — the previous lemma at the inverted base, which is the shape the
constant `(√I)⁻¹` of Mathlib's `S`-transformation arrives in. -/
theorem csqrt_inv_zpow_two_mul (x : ℂ) (k : ℤ) : ((Complex.sqrt x)⁻¹) ^ (2 * k) = x ^ (-k) := by
  rw [_root_.inv_zpow, csqrt_zpow_two_mul, ← _root_.zpow_neg]

/-- Real `zpow` bookkeeping: `(x^m)² = (x²)^m`, unconditional in `ℝ`. -/
theorem zpow_sq_comm (x : ℝ) (m : ℤ) : (x ^ m) ^ (2 : ℕ) = (x ^ (2 : ℕ)) ^ m := by
  rw [← zpow_natCast (x ^ m) 2, ← zpow_natCast x 2, ← _root_.zpow_mul, ← _root_.zpow_mul,
    mul_comm]

/-- **`Real.sqrt` commutes with a finite product of integer powers of POSITIVE reals:**
`∏ (√a_δ)^{r_δ} = √(∏ a_δ^{r_δ})`.

This is the lemma that keeps the entire branch question inside `ℝ`, where `Real.sqrt` is a
genuine function of a nonnegative real and no cut exists.  Proof: the left side is positive
(each `√a_δ > 0` and `zpow_pos`), and squaring it gives `∏ a_δ^{r_δ}` by `Real.sq_sqrt`; then
`Real.sqrt_sq` on a nonnegative number closes it.  Positivity of every `a δ` is essential —
with a negative factor both `Real.sqrt` values are junk `0` and the identity fails. -/
theorem prod_sqrt_zpow {t : Finset ℕ} {a : ℕ → ℝ} (ha : ∀ δ ∈ t, 0 < a δ) (r : ℕ → ℤ) :
    ∏ δ ∈ t, (Real.sqrt (a δ)) ^ (r δ) = Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ t, (a δ) ^ (r δ)) := by
  have hpos : 0 < ∏ δ ∈ t, (Real.sqrt (a δ)) ^ (r δ) :=
    Finset.prod_pos fun δ hδ => zpow_pos (Real.sqrt_pos.mpr (ha δ hδ)) _
  have hsq : (∏ δ ∈ t, (Real.sqrt (a δ)) ^ (r δ)) ^ (2 : ℕ) = ∏ δ ∈ t, (a δ) ^ (r δ) := by
    rw [← Finset.prod_pow]
    refine Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ => ?_
    rw [zpow_sq_comm, Real.sq_sqrt (ha δ hδ).le]
  rw [← hsq, Real.sqrt_sq hpos.le]

/-- **The constant, computed.**  With `s = ∏_{δ ∣ N} δ^{r_δ}` and `∑_{δ ∣ N} r_δ = 2k`,

  `∏_{δ ∣ N} √(N/δ)^{r_δ} = N^k · (√s)⁻¹`.

The whole content is the divisor identity `(N/δ)·δ = N`, which collapses `P · s` (where
`P = ∏ (N/δ)^{r_δ}`) to `∏ N^{r_δ} = N^{2k}`; then `√P · √s = √(P·s) = √((N^k)²) = N^k`,
the last step using `0 < N`.  Everything happens in `ℝ`. -/
theorem prod_sqrt_div_divisors {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : ℕ → ℤ) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) :
    ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (Real.sqrt ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ)) ^ (r δ)
      = (N : ℝ) ^ k * (Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ)))⁻¹ := by
  have hNR : (0 : ℝ) < (N : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast hN
  have hdivpos : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors, (0 : ℝ) < ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) := by
    intro δ hδ
    have : 0 < N / δ :=
      Nat.div_pos (Nat.le_of_dvd hN (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ)) (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ)
    exact_mod_cast this
  have hδpos : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors, (0 : ℝ) < (δ : ℝ) := by
    intro δ hδ; exact_mod_cast Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ
  set P : ℝ := ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) ^ (r δ) with hPdef
  set s : ℝ := ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ) with hsdef
  have hP : 0 < P := Finset.prod_pos fun δ hδ => zpow_pos (hdivpos δ hδ) _
  have hs : 0 < s := Finset.prod_pos fun δ hδ => zpow_pos (hδpos δ hδ) _
  have hPs : P * s = (N : ℝ) ^ (2 * k) := by
    rw [hPdef, hsdef, ← Finset.prod_mul_distrib]
    have hstep : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors,
        ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) ^ (r δ) * (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ) = (N : ℝ) ^ (r δ) := by
      intro δ hδ
      rw [← mul_zpow]
      congr 1
      have hc : (N / δ) * δ = N := Nat.div_mul_cancel (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ)
      exact_mod_cast congrArg (Nat.cast : ℕ → ℝ) hc
    rw [Finset.prod_congr rfl hstep, ← hk]
    induction N.divisors using Finset.induction_on with
    | empty => simp
    | insert a t ha ih => rw [Finset.prod_insert ha, Finset.sum_insert ha, ih, zpow_add₀ hNR.ne']
  have h2 : (N : ℝ) ^ (2 * k) = ((N : ℝ) ^ k) * ((N : ℝ) ^ k) := by
    rw [mul_comm (2 : ℤ) k, _root_.zpow_mul, zpow_two]
  have hkey : Real.sqrt P * Real.sqrt s = (N : ℝ) ^ k := by
    rw [← Real.sqrt_mul hP.le, hPs, h2, Real.sqrt_mul_self (zpow_pos hNR k).le]
  have hsne : Real.sqrt s ≠ 0 := Real.sqrt_ne_zero'.mpr hs
  rw [prod_sqrt_zpow hdivpos r, ← hPdef]
  field_simp
  linarith [hkey]

/-- **The divisor involution `δ ↦ N/δ` on the `η`-product**: `∏_{δ ∣ N} η((N/δ)z)^{r_δ}` is
the DUAL eta quotient `∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δz)^{r_{N/δ}}`.  This is Mathlib's `Nat.prod_div_divisors`
(the multiplicative form of `Nat.sum_div_divisors`, the same involution F3.1-A7/A8 use on the
congruences) plus `Nat.div_div_self`, which needs `0 < N`. -/
theorem prod_eta_div_divisors {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : ℕ → ℤ) (z : ℂ) :
    ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ModularForm.eta (((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) * z) ^ (r δ)
      = ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * z) ^ (r (N / δ)) := by
  rw [← Nat.prod_div_divisors N (fun d => ModularForm.eta ((d : ℂ) * z) ^ (r (N / d)))]
  refine Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ => ?_
  rw [Nat.div_div_self (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ) hN.ne']

/-- **The Fricke transform of a single `η`-factor.**  For `δ ∣ N` and `z` in the open upper
half-plane,

  `η(δ · (-1/(Nz))) = (√I)⁻¹ · √(N/δ) · √z · η((N/δ)·z)`,

where the middle square root is the REAL one.  Two ingredients, both named: the divisor
identity `δ · (-1/(Nz)) = -1/((N/δ)z)` (which is `δ·(N/δ) = N` after clearing denominators),
and Mathlib's `ModularForm.eta_comp_eq_csqrt_I_inv` applied at the point `(N/δ)z ∈ ℍₒ`,
followed by `csqrt_ofReal_mul` to split `√((N/δ)z)`.  Note that `N/δ` is a POSITIVE natural
for `δ ∈ N.divisors`, which is exactly the hypothesis `csqrt_ofReal_mul` needs. -/
theorem eta_fricke_factor {N δ : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hδ : δ ∈ N.divisors) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * z))))
      = (Complex.sqrt I)⁻¹ * ((Real.sqrt ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) : ℝ) : ℂ) * Complex.sqrt z
          * ModularForm.eta (((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) * z) := by
  have hz0 : z ≠ 0 := by
    intro h; rw [h] at hz; simp [UpperHalfPlane.upperHalfPlaneSet] at hz
  have hδ0 : 0 < δ := Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ
  have hdvd : δ ∣ N := Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ
  have hm0 : 0 < N / δ := Nat.div_pos (Nat.le_of_dvd hN hdvd) hδ0
  have hmR : (0 : ℝ) < ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) := by exact_mod_cast hm0
  have hmC : ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by exact_mod_cast hm0.ne'
  have hδC : (δ : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by exact_mod_cast hδ0.ne'
  have hmem : (((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) * z) ∈ ℍₒ := natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet_of_mem hm0 hz
  have harg : (δ : ℂ) * (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * z))) = -1 / (((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) * z) := by
    have hNd : (N : ℂ) = (δ : ℂ) * ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) := by
      have hc : δ * (N / δ) = N := Nat.mul_div_cancel' hdvd
      exact_mod_cast (congrArg (Nat.cast : ℕ → ℂ) hc).symm
    rw [hNd]
    field_simp
  have hS := ModularForm.eta_comp_eq_csqrt_I_inv hmem
  simp only [Function.comp_apply, Pi.smul_apply, Pi.mul_apply, smul_eq_mul] at hS
  rw [harg, hS, show ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) = (((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) : ℂ) by push_cast; ring,
    csqrt_ofReal_mul hmR hz0]
  push_cast
  ring

/-- **The Fricke transformation of an eta quotient.**  Under `z ↦ -1/(Nz)` the eta quotient
with exponent vector `r` becomes the *dual* quotient, with exponents reversed along the divisor
involution `δ ↦ N/δ`, times an explicit constant:

  `f(-1/(Nz)) = i^{-k} N^k s^{-1/2} z^k f*(z)`,  where `s = ∏_δ δ^{r_δ} > 0`
  and the dual quotient is `f*(z) = ∏_δ η(δz)^{r_{N/δ}}`.

Each factor is handled by Mathlib's `eta_comp_eq_csqrt_I_inv` together with the branch-clean
scaling `√(m·z) = √m · √z` for **real** `m > 0`.  The `zpow`-of-`csqrt`-product bookkeeping is
the genuinely hard part: the square roots must be recombined without crossing the branch cut,
which is why the constant is stated with a *real* `Real.sqrt` and an integer power of `i`.

PROOF SHAPE.  `eta_fricke_factor` rewrites every factor into a product of four terms; `mul_zpow`
(unconditional in `ℂ`, a `DivisionCommMonoid`) and `Finset.prod_mul_distrib` split the product
into four.  Two of them are products of a CONSTANT to the powers `r δ` and collapse by
`prod_zpow_const` to that constant raised to `∑ r_δ = 2k`, whence `csqrt_inv_zpow_two_mul`
gives `I^{-k}` and `csqrt_zpow_two_mul` gives `z^k` — this is exactly where `k ∈ ℤ` is used, and
where a half-integral weight would leave a residual square root.  The third is the real
constant, computed in `ℝ` by `prod_sqrt_div_divisors`.  The fourth is the dual quotient, by
`prod_eta_div_divisors`.

Sanity anchors, all proved below: at `N = 1`, `r 1 = 24`, `k = 12` this reduces to
`discriminant_S_invariant` (`eta_S_via_fricke`, and the same statement is re-derived from
Mathlib's theorem next to it); at `N = 2` with `r = (1,1)` it reduces to the hand-checked
`η(z)η(2z)` case (`fricke_level_two_pin`); and `fricke_dual_pin` exhibits an `r` whose dual is
a DIFFERENT exponent vector, so the reversal is not vacuous. -/
theorem etaQuotient_fricke {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    etaQuotient N r (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * z)))
      = I ^ (-k) * (N : ℂ) ^ k
        * ((Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ)) : ℝ) : ℂ)⁻¹
        * z ^ k * etaQuotient N (fun δ => r (N / δ)) z := by
  have hz0 : z ≠ 0 := by
    intro h; rw [h] at hz; simp [UpperHalfPlane.upperHalfPlaneSet] at hz
  have hIne : Complex.sqrt I ≠ 0 := csqrt_ne_zero Complex.I_ne_zero
  have hcz : Complex.sqrt z ≠ 0 := csqrt_ne_zero hz0
  have hfac : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors,
      ModularForm.eta ((δ : ℂ) * (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * z)))) ^ (r δ)
        = ((Complex.sqrt I)⁻¹) ^ (r δ)
          * (((Real.sqrt ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) : ℝ) : ℂ)) ^ (r δ)
          * (Complex.sqrt z) ^ (r δ)
          * (ModularForm.eta (((N / δ : ℕ) : ℂ) * z)) ^ (r δ) := by
    intro δ hδ
    rw [eta_fricke_factor hN hδ hz, mul_zpow, mul_zpow, mul_zpow]
  have hreal : ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (((Real.sqrt ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) : ℝ) : ℂ)) ^ (r δ)
      = (N : ℂ) ^ k * ((Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ)) : ℝ) : ℂ)⁻¹ := by
    have hc := congrArg (fun t : ℝ => (t : ℂ)) (prod_sqrt_div_divisors hN r hk)
    push_cast at hc
    exact hc
  simp only [etaQuotient]
  rw [Finset.prod_congr rfl hfac, Finset.prod_mul_distrib, Finset.prod_mul_distrib,
    Finset.prod_mul_distrib, prod_zpow_const _ (inv_ne_zero hIne) r, prod_zpow_const _ hcz r,
    hreal, prod_eta_div_divisors hN r z, hk, csqrt_inv_zpow_two_mul, csqrt_zpow_two_mul]
  ring

/-- **LL-1 EXTERNAL GUARD, run in the STRONG direction.**  At `N = 1`, `r ≡ 24`, `k = 12` the
eta quotient is `η` itself, `s = 1`, `N^k = 1` and `i^{-12} = 1`, so `etaQuotient_fricke` says
`η(-1/z)²⁴ = z¹² η(z)²⁴` — which is MATHLIB's `discriminant_S_invariant`, reproved here through
our constant.  SECOND-ROUTE AGREEMENT ONLY: it pins NO component of the constant — `i^{-12} =
i^{+12}`, `1^k = 1` for every `k`, `s = 1`.  `selfDual_pin_level_one_no_evidence_*` proves this. -/
theorem eta_S_via_fricke {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    ModularForm.eta (-(1 / z)) ^ (24 : ℕ) = z ^ (12 : ℕ) * ModularForm.eta z ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
  have h := etaQuotient_fricke (N := 1) one_pos (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (k := 12) (by simp) hz
  simp only [etaQuotient, Nat.divisors_one, Finset.prod_singleton, Nat.cast_one, one_mul] at h
  have hI : (I : ℂ) ^ (-12 : ℤ) = 1 := by
    have h12 : (I : ℂ) ^ (12 : ℕ) = 1 := by
      rw [show (12 : ℕ) = 4 * 3 by norm_num, pow_mul, Complex.I_pow_four, one_pow]
    rw [show (-12 : ℤ) = -((12 : ℕ) : ℤ) by norm_num, _root_.zpow_neg, zpow_natCast, h12, inv_one]
  rw [← zpow_natCast (ModularForm.eta (-(1 / z))) 24, ← zpow_natCast z 12,
    ← zpow_natCast (ModularForm.eta z) 24]
  push_cast
  rw [h, hI]
  simp

-- The SAME statement, derived instead from MATHLIB's `discriminant_S_invariant`.  Two
-- derivations of one identity: if our constant `i^{-k} N^k s^{-1/2}` were assembled wrongly at
-- `N = 1`, `r ≡ 24`, `k = 12`, the two would disagree.
example (z : ℍ) :
    ModularForm.eta (-(1 / (z : ℂ))) ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = (z : ℂ) ^ (12 : ℕ) * ModularForm.eta (z : ℂ) ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
  have hzne : (z : ℂ) ≠ 0 := UpperHalfPlane.ne_zero z
  have h := congrFun ModularForm.discriminant_S_invariant z
  rw [ModularForm.SL_slash_apply, UpperHalfPlane.modular_S_smul] at h
  simp only [ModularForm.discriminant] at h
  have hdS : denom (ModularGroup.S : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) = (z : ℂ) := by
    rw [ModularGroup.denom_apply]
    simp [ModularGroup.coe_S]
  rw [hdS, show (-12 : ℤ) = -((12 : ℕ) : ℤ) by norm_num, _root_.zpow_neg, zpow_natCast,
    show (-(z : ℂ))⁻¹ = -(1 / (z : ℂ)) by rw [one_div, inv_neg]] at h
  rw [← h]
  field_simp

/-- **The hand-checked `η(z)η(Nz)` case, at `N = 2`.**  With `r ≡ 1` on `{1,2}` the weight sum
is `2 = 2·1`, so `k = 1` and `s = 1¹·2¹ = 2`; the identity reads

  `η(-1/(2z))·η(-1/z) = i⁻¹ · 2 · (√2)⁻¹ · z · η(z)·η(2z)`,

which is `(√I)⁻² · √2 · z = -i√2 z` computed by hand from the `S`-transform at the two points
`2z` and `z`.  Here the exponent vector is its own dual, so this pins the CONSTANT. -/
theorem fricke_level_two_pin {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    ModularForm.eta (-(1 / ((2 : ℂ) * z))) * ModularForm.eta ((2 : ℂ) * (-(1 / ((2 : ℂ) * z))))
      = I⁻¹ * (2 : ℂ) * ((Real.sqrt 2 : ℝ) : ℂ)⁻¹ * z
        * (ModularForm.eta z * ModularForm.eta ((2 : ℂ) * z)) := by
  have h := etaQuotient_fricke (N := 2) (by norm_num) (fun _ => (1 : ℤ)) (k := 1) (by decide) hz
  simp only [etaQuotient, show (2 : ℕ).divisors = {1, 2} from by decide] at h
  norm_num at h
  have hz0 : z ≠ 0 := by
    intro hh; rw [hh] at hz; simp [UpperHalfPlane.upperHalfPlaneSet] at hz
  rw [show (-(1 / ((2 : ℂ) * z))) = -(z⁻¹ * (1 / 2)) by field_simp, mul_neg, h, Complex.inv_I]
  ring

/-- **NON-VACUITY OF THE DUAL.**  At `N = 2` with `r = (2, 0)` — so `f(z) = η(z)²` — the dual
exponent vector is `(0, 2)`, so `f*(z) = η(2z)²`: a genuinely DIFFERENT function.  The identity
reads `η(-1/(2z))² = -2i·z·η(2z)²`, which is `η(-1/w)² = i⁻¹·w·η(w)²` at `w = 2z`.  A version
of this node that forgot to reverse the exponents would assert `η(-1/(2z))² = -2iz·η(z)²`,
which is false. -/
theorem fricke_dual_pin {z : ℂ} (hz : z ∈ ℍₒ) :
    ModularForm.eta (-(1 / ((2 : ℂ) * z))) ^ (2 : ℤ)
      = -(2 : ℂ) * I * z * ModularForm.eta ((2 : ℂ) * z) ^ (2 : ℤ) := by
  have h := etaQuotient_fricke (N := 2) (by norm_num)
    (fun δ => if δ = 1 then (2 : ℤ) else 0) (k := 1) (by decide) hz
  simp only [etaQuotient, show (2 : ℕ).divisors = {1, 2} from by decide] at h
  norm_num at h
  rw [show (-(1 / ((2 : ℂ) * z))) = -(z⁻¹ * (1 / 2)) by
      have hz0 : z ≠ 0 := by
        intro hh; rw [hh] at hz; simp [UpperHalfPlane.upperHalfPlaneSet] at hz
      field_simp]
  rw [h]
  ring

/-! ### F3.2-A8 — Ligozat congruence (ii) ⟺ the multiplier is trivial on `V`

The route is Dedekind-sum free and is the `T`-half (`F3.2-A6`) conjugated by the Fricke
involution (`F3.2-A7`), used TWICE and in opposite directions:

  `V = W_N T W_N⁻¹`,  `W_N : z ↦ -1/(Nz)`,  `V : z ↦ z/(1 - Nz)`,

so `f(Vz) = f(W_N(T(W_N z)))`.  Pushing `W_N` through `etaQuotient_fricke` replaces `f` by the
DUAL quotient `f*` (exponents reversed along `δ ↦ N/δ`); congruence (ii) for `r` is literally
congruence (i) for the dual vector (`sum_divisors_dual`, the divisor involution
`Nat.sum_div_divisors`), so the `T`-step is `etaQuotient_T_transform` applied to `f*`; the
second `W_N` brings the dual of the dual — which is `r` itself, by `Nat.div_div_self` — back.
The two Fricke constants `i^{-k} N^k s^{-1/2}` are inverse to one another on the dual pair,
because `√s · √s* = N^k` (`sqrt_prod_dual`, proved entirely inside `ℝ`), and what survives is
exactly the automorphy factor `(1 - Nz)^k = (denom V z)^k`.  No eta multiplier system, no
Dedekind sum, and no generator of `Γ₀(N)` other than `T` and `V` is touched. -/

/-- `V = W_N T W_N⁻¹ = !![1,0;-N,1]`, the Fricke conjugate of `T`. -/
def matV (N : ℕ) : SL(2, ℤ) :=
  ⟨!![1, 0; -(N : ℤ), 1], by simp [Matrix.det_fin_two_of]⟩

@[simp] theorem matV_coe (N : ℕ) :
    ((matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ) = !![1, 0; -(N : ℤ), 1] := rfl

/-- `V ∈ Γ₀(N)`: the lower-left entry is `-N ≡ 0 (mod N)`.  `Gamma0_mem` reduces membership to
`((-N : ℤ) : ZMod N) = 0`, which `simp` discharges through `ZMod.natCast_self`. -/
theorem matV_mem_Gamma0 (N : ℕ) : matV N ∈ Gamma0 N := by
  rw [Gamma0_mem]
  simp [matV]

/-- `denom V z = -N·z + 1 = 1 - Nz`.  Unlike `denom_T` this is NOT `1`, which is why the
`V`-statement carries a genuine automorphy factor and the weight `k` does not drop out. -/
theorem denom_matV (N : ℕ) (z : ℍ) :
    denom ((matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) = 1 - (N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ) := by
  rw [ModularGroup.denom_apply]
  simp [matV]
  ring

/-- `V` acts on `ℍ` by the Möbius map `z ↦ z/(1 - Nz)`. -/
theorem coe_matV_smul (N : ℕ) (z : ℍ) :
    ((matV N • z : ℍ) : ℂ) = (z : ℂ) / (1 - (N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ)) := by
  rw [UpperHalfPlane.coe_specialLinearGroup_apply]
  simp [matV]
  ring

/-- `-1/x` keeps a point of the OPEN upper half-plane there: `im(-1/x) = im x / |x|² > 0`.
This is the only point-set fact the Fricke conjugation needs, and it is elementary — no
`GL(2,ℝ)⁺`-action machinery is invoked, because `W_N` is applied here only as a formula. -/
theorem neg_inv_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet {x : ℂ} (hx : x ∈ ℍₒ) : (-(1 / x)) ∈ ℍₒ := by
  have hxim : 0 < x.im := hx
  have hxne : x ≠ 0 := by
    intro h; rw [h] at hxim; simp at hxim
  show (0 : ℝ) < (-(1 / x)).im
  rw [one_div, Complex.neg_im, Complex.inv_im, neg_div, neg_neg]
  exact div_pos hxim (Complex.normSq_pos.mpr hxne)

/-- **The divisor involution on a product of integer powers**: `∏_δ a(N/δ)^{r_δ}` equals
`∏_δ a(δ)^{r_{N/δ}}`.  `Nat.prod_div_divisors` plus `Nat.div_div_self`, exactly as in
`prod_eta_div_divisors`; stated for an arbitrary `a : ℕ → ℝ` because it is used at `a = ↑·`. -/
theorem prod_zpow_div_divisors {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (a : ℕ → ℝ) (r : ℕ → ℤ) :
    ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (a (N / δ)) ^ (r δ)
      = ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (a δ) ^ (r (N / δ)) := by
  rw [← Nat.prod_div_divisors N (fun d => (a d) ^ (r (N / d)))]
  refine Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ => ?_
  rw [Nat.div_div_self (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ) hN.ne']

/-- **The divisor involution on the Ligozat sums**: `Σ_δ δ · r_{N/δ} = Σ_δ (N/δ) · r_δ`.
This single identity is the reason congruence (ii) needs no separate analytic input: it says
that the sum controlling the `T`-behaviour of the DUAL quotient is the sum appearing in
Ligozat's second congruence for the ORIGINAL one. -/
theorem sum_divisors_dual {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) :
    ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) * r (N / δ)
      = ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℤ) * r δ := by
  rw [← Nat.sum_div_divisors N (fun d => ((d : ℕ) : ℤ) * r (N / d))]
  refine Finset.sum_congr rfl fun δ hδ => ?_
  rw [Nat.div_div_self (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ) hN.ne']

/-- **Congruence (ii) for `r` IS congruence (i) for the dual vector `δ ↦ r_{N/δ}`.**  A pure
restatement of `sum_divisors_dual`, recorded because it is the sentence the node description
asserts and because it is what makes the `T`-step below legitimate. -/
theorem ligozatCongr1_dual {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) (h : LigozatCongr2 N r) :
    LigozatCongr1 N (fun δ => r (N / δ)) := by
  show (24 : ℤ) ∣ ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) * r (N / δ)
  rw [sum_divisors_dual hN r]
  exact h

/-- **The two Fricke constants are inverse on the dual pair**: with `s = ∏_δ δ^{r_δ}` and
`s* = ∏_δ δ^{r_{N/δ}}`, one has `√s* = N^k · (√s)⁻¹`, i.e. `√s · √s* = N^k`.

This is `prod_sqrt_div_divisors` (F3.2-A7) read backwards through `prod_sqrt_zpow` and the
divisor involution, and it happens entirely in `ℝ`, where `Real.sqrt` is a genuine function of
a nonnegative real and no branch cut exists.  It is the cancellation that makes the composite
`W_N T W_N⁻¹` produce the automorphy factor `(1-Nz)^k` with constant exactly `1`. -/
theorem sqrt_prod_dual {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) :
    Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r (N / δ)))
      = (N : ℝ) ^ k * (Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ)))⁻¹ := by
  have hdivpos : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors, (0 : ℝ) < ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ) := by
    intro δ hδ
    have : 0 < N / δ :=
      Nat.div_pos (Nat.le_of_dvd hN (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ)) (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ)
    exact_mod_cast this
  rw [← prod_sqrt_div_divisors hN r hk,
    prod_sqrt_zpow (a := fun δ => ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℝ)) hdivpos r]
  congr 1
  exact (prod_zpow_div_divisors hN (fun d => (d : ℝ)) r).symm

/-- Pure field bookkeeping, isolated so that the constant computation below is a single
rewrite: `A·B·S⁻¹·X · (A·B·(B⁻¹·S)·Z) = A²·B·(X·Z)` whenever `B ≠ 0` and `S ≠ 0`. -/
theorem mul_shuffle_aux (A B S X Z : ℂ) (hB : B ≠ 0) (hS : S ≠ 0) :
    A * B * S⁻¹ * X * (A * B * (B⁻¹ * S) * Z) = A * A * B * (X * Z) := by
  field_simp

/-- **The `V`-transformation of an eta quotient, UNCONDITIONALLY.**  For every `N > 0` and
every exponent vector with `Σ_δ r_δ = 2k`, `k ∈ ℤ`, and with NO congruence hypothesis,

  `f(V·z) = e^{πi(Σ_δ (N/δ) r_δ)/12} · (denom V z)^k · f(z)`,   `V = !![1,0;-N,1]`.

This is the exact `V`-analogue of `etaQuotientH_T_smul`, and congruence (ii) is visible in it
as the condition that kills the exponential.  PROOF: `V·z = -1/(N·(u+1))` where
`u = -1/(Nz)`; `etaQuotient_fricke` at `u+1` turns `f` into the dual quotient `f*`,
`etaQuotient_T_transform` moves `f*(u+1)` to `f*(u)` at the cost of `e^{πi(Σ_δ δ r_{N/δ})/12}`
— which is the stated constant by `sum_divisors_dual` — and `etaQuotient_fricke` at `z`
applied to the dual vector returns `f` (the dual of the dual is `r`, by `Nat.div_div_self`).
The four constants collapse by `sqrt_prod_dual` and `Complex.I_mul_I`: `i^{-2k} = (-1)^k` and
`(-1)^k (Nz-1)^k = (1-Nz)^k`, which is `(denom V z)^k`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_V_smul {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (matV N • z)
      = Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I * ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12)
        * (denom (matV N) z) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  have hzH : (z : ℂ) ∈ ℍₒ := z.coe_im_pos
  have hz0 : (z : ℂ) ≠ 0 := UpperHalfPlane.ne_zero z
  have hNC : (N : ℂ) ≠ 0 := Nat.cast_ne_zero.mpr hN.ne'
  have hNzH : ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ)) ∈ ℍₒ := natCast_mul_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet hN z
  have huH : (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ)))) ∈ ℍₒ := neg_inv_mem_upperHalfPlaneSet hNzH
  have huIm : 0 < (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ)))).im := huH
  have hu1H : (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ))) + 1) ∈ ℍₒ := by
    show (0 : ℝ) < (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ))) + 1).im
    simpa using huIm
  have hden : (1 : ℂ) - (N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by
    have h := UpperHalfPlane.denom_ne_zero ((matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) z
    rwa [denom_matV] at h
  -- `V·z = -1/(N·(u+1))` with `u = -1/(Nz)`: this is `V = W_N T W_N⁻¹` in coordinates.
  have hpt : ((matV N • z : ℍ) : ℂ)
      = -(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ))) + 1))) := by
    rw [coe_matV_smul, show (N : ℂ) * (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ))) + 1)
        = -((1 - (N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ)) / (z : ℂ)) by field_simp; ring,
      one_div, inv_neg, neg_neg, inv_div]
  have hF1 := etaQuotient_fricke hN r hk hu1H
  have hT := etaQuotient_T_transform (N := N) (fun δ => r (N / δ)) huH
  rw [sum_divisors_dual hN r] at hT
  have hk' : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, (fun δ => r (N / δ)) δ = 2 * k := by
    simp only []
    rw [Nat.sum_div_divisors N r]
    exact hk
  have hF2 := etaQuotient_fricke hN (fun δ => r (N / δ)) hk' hzH
  -- the dual of the dual is `r` itself
  have hdd : etaQuotient N (fun δ => (fun δ => r (N / δ)) (N / δ)) (z : ℂ)
      = etaQuotient N r (z : ℂ) := by
    simp only [etaQuotient]
    refine Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ => ?_
    rw [Nat.div_div_self (Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ) hN.ne']
  have hspos : (0 : ℝ) < ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ) :=
    Finset.prod_pos fun δ hδ => zpow_pos (by exact_mod_cast Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ) _
  have hS : ((Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ)) : ℝ) : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by
    simpa using Real.sqrt_ne_zero'.mpr hspos
  have hBne : ((N : ℂ)) ^ k ≠ 0 := zpow_ne_zero _ hNC
  have hs'c : ((Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ ((fun δ => r (N / δ)) δ)) : ℝ) : ℂ)
      = (N : ℂ) ^ k * ((Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ)) : ℝ) : ℂ)⁻¹ := by
    simp only []
    rw [sqrt_prod_dual hN r hk]
    push_cast
    ring
  have hAA : (I : ℂ) ^ (-k) * (I : ℂ) ^ (-k) = ((-1 : ℂ)) ^ k := by
    rw [← mul_zpow, Complex.I_mul_I, _root_.zpow_neg, ← _root_.inv_zpow]
    norm_num
  have hXZ : (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ))) + 1) ^ k * (z : ℂ) ^ k
      = ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ) - 1) ^ k * ((N : ℂ) ^ k)⁻¹ := by
    rw [← mul_zpow, show (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ))) + 1) * (z : ℂ)
        = ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ) - 1) * ((N : ℂ))⁻¹ by field_simp; ring,
      mul_zpow, _root_.inv_zpow]
  have hcoef : I ^ (-k) * (N : ℂ) ^ k
        * ((Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ (r δ)) : ℝ) : ℂ)⁻¹
        * (-(1 / ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ))) + 1) ^ k
      * (I ^ (-k) * (N : ℂ) ^ k
        * ((Real.sqrt (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℝ) ^ ((fun δ => r (N / δ)) δ)) : ℝ) : ℂ)⁻¹
        * (z : ℂ) ^ k)
      = (1 - (N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ)) ^ k := by
    rw [hs'c, mul_inv, inv_inv, mul_shuffle_aux _ _ _ _ _ hBne hS, hXZ, hAA,
      show ((-1 : ℂ)) ^ k * (N : ℂ) ^ k * (((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ) - 1) ^ k * ((N : ℂ) ^ k)⁻¹)
        = ((-1 : ℂ)) ^ k * ((N : ℂ) * (z : ℂ) - 1) ^ k by field_simp,
      ← mul_zpow]
    congr 1
    ring
  simp only [etaQuotientH]
  rw [hpt, hF1, hT, hF2, hdd, denom_matV, ← hcoef]
  ring

/-- **Ligozat's congruence (ii) is exactly triviality of the multiplier on `V`.**
`24 ∣ Σ_δ (N/δ) r_δ` kills the exponential of `etaQuotientH_V_smul` — the constant becomes
`e^{2πim} = 1` — leaving `f(V·z) = (denom V z)^k f(z)`.  Unlike the `T` case, `denom V z`
is `1 - Nz`, not `1`, so the automorphy factor is really there. -/
theorem multiplier_V_eq_one {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h24' : LigozatCongr2 N r) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (matV N • z) = (denom (matV N) z) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := h24'
  rw [etaQuotientH_V_smul hN r hk z, hm,
    show ((π : ℂ) * I * (((24 * m : ℤ)) : ℂ) / 12) = (m : ℂ) * (2 * (π : ℂ) * I) by
      push_cast; ring,
    Complex.exp_int_mul_two_pi_mul_I, one_mul]

/-- **`w(V) = 1`**, in the multiplier the keystone `F3.2-A5` actually produces: the character
`w : Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ` of `etaQuotient_multiplier` takes the value `1` at `V`.  Cancelling the
nonzero `(denom V z)^k · f(z)` in `etaQuotientH_transform` against `multiplier_V_eq_one`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_V_eq_one {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h24' : LigozatCongr2 N r) :
    etaMultiplierVal N r k (matV N) = 1 := by
  have h := etaQuotientH_transform r hk (matV_mem_Gamma0 N) UpperHalfPlane.I
  rw [multiplier_V_eq_one hN r hk h24'] at h
  have hd : (denom ((matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ≠ 0 :=
    denom_ne_zero _ _
  have hne : (denom ((matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ k
      * etaQuotientH N r UpperHalfPlane.I ≠ 0 :=
    mul_ne_zero (zpow_ne_zero _ hd) (etaQuotientH_ne_zero _ _ _)
  refine mul_right_cancel₀ hne ?_
  rw [one_mul, ← mul_assoc]
  exact h.symm

/-- The same statement for the BUNDLED character `etaMultiplierHom r hk : Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ`:
`w ⟨!![1,0;-N,1], matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1` in `ℂˣ`.  This is verbatim the shape `F3.2-A8` was
scoped in. -/
theorem etaMultiplierHom_V_eq_one {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h24' : LigozatCongr2 N r) :
    etaMultiplierHom r hk ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1 := by
  apply Units.ext
  simpa [etaMultiplierHom] using etaMultiplierVal_V_eq_one hN r hk h24'

/-- **"EXACTLY", proved as an iff** — the `V`-analogue of `ligozatCongr1_iff_T_invariant`.
Congruence (ii) does not merely imply the `V`-transformation law: it is EQUIVALENT to it,
given only the integrality hypothesis `Σ_δ r_δ = 2k`.  The converse divides
`etaQuotientH_V_smul` by the nonvanishing `(denom V i)^k f(i)`, so `e^{πiS/12} = 1` with
`S = Σ_δ (N/δ) r_δ`, and `Complex.exp_eq_one_iff` then forces `S = 24n`. -/
theorem ligozatCongr2_iff_V_transform {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) :
    LigozatCongr2 N r ↔ ∀ z : ℍ,
      etaQuotientH N r (matV N • z) = (denom (matV N) z) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  refine ⟨fun h24 => multiplier_V_eq_one hN r hk h24, fun hV => ?_⟩
  have h := etaQuotientH_V_smul hN r hk UpperHalfPlane.I
  rw [hV UpperHalfPlane.I] at h
  have hd : (denom ((matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ≠ 0 :=
    denom_ne_zero _ _
  have hne : (denom ((matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ)) ^ k
      * etaQuotientH N r UpperHalfPlane.I ≠ 0 :=
    mul_ne_zero (zpow_ne_zero _ hd) (etaQuotientH_ne_zero _ _ _)
  have hone : Complex.exp ((π : ℂ) * I
      * ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) / 12) = 1 := by
    refine mul_right_cancel₀ hne ?_
    rw [one_mul, ← mul_assoc]
    exact h.symm
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := Complex.exp_eq_one_iff.mp hone
  refine ⟨n, ?_⟩
  have hpi : (π : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by exact_mod_cast Real.pi_ne_zero
  have hI : (I : ℂ) ≠ 0 := Complex.I_ne_zero
  have hcast : ((∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((N / δ : ℕ) : ℤ) * r δ : ℤ) : ℂ) = ((24 * n : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
    push_cast
    field_simp at hn
    push_cast at hn
    linear_combination hn
  exact_mod_cast hcast

/-! #### F3.2-A8 — the LL-1 guards

Two checks, in the two directions a wrong `V`-statement could fail: an EXTERNAL one against
Mathlib's own modularity of `Δ`, and a NON-VACUITY one exhibiting an eta quotient for which
the conclusion is false. -/

/-- `Δ = η²⁴` satisfies congruence (ii): at `N = 1` the sum is `Σ_δ (1/δ) r_δ = 24`. -/
theorem ligozatCongr2_level_one_24 : LigozatCongr2 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) := by
  simp [LigozatCongr2, Nat.divisors_one]

/-- GUARD 1 (EXTERNAL, against Mathlib).  At `N = 1`, `r ≡ 24`, `k = 12` — where the eta
quotient is Mathlib's `Δ` and `V = !![1,0;-1,1] ∈ SL(2,ℤ)` — our route gives
`Δ(V·z) = (denom V z)¹² Δ(z)`… -/
theorem multiplier_V_level_one_24 (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (matV 1 • z)
      = (denom (matV 1) z) ^ (12 : ℤ) * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) z :=
  multiplier_V_eq_one one_pos _ sum_divisors_one_24 ligozatCongr2_level_one_24 z

-- …and so does Mathlib's own full-level modularity of `Δ`
-- (`etaQuotientH_level_one_24_transform` at `γ = V`).  Two independent derivations of one
-- identity: a wrong sign in `hAA`, a wrong power of `N` in `sqrt_prod_dual`, or a `denom`
-- computed from the wrong row of `V` would make them disagree.
example (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (matV 1 • z)
      = (denom (matV 1) z) ^ (12 : ℤ) * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) z :=
  etaQuotientH_level_one_24_transform (matV 1) z

/-- GUARD 2 (NON-VACUITY).  At `N = 2` with `r = (0, 2)` — so `f(z) = η(2z)²`, weight `k = 1` —
congruence (ii) reads `24 ∣ 2` and FAILS, so by `ligozatCongr2_iff_V_transform` the eta
quotient does NOT satisfy the `V`-transformation law.  This certifies that congruence (ii) is
a real restriction on `r` and that the iff above is not a tautology. -/
theorem eta_two_sq_not_V_invariant :
    ¬ (∀ z : ℍ, etaQuotientH 2 (fun δ => if δ = 2 then (2 : ℤ) else 0) (matV 2 • z)
        = (denom (matV 2) z) ^ (1 : ℤ)
          * etaQuotientH 2 (fun δ => if δ = 2 then (2 : ℤ) else 0) z) := by
  rw [← ligozatCongr2_iff_V_transform (N := 2) (by norm_num)
    (fun δ => if δ = 2 then (2 : ℤ) else 0) (k := 1) (by decide)]
  intro h
  norm_num [LigozatCongr2, show (2 : ℕ).divisors = {1, 2} from by decide] at h


/-! ### F3.2-A9 — the multiplier at an ELLIPTIC FIXED POINT

`F3.2-A6` and `F3.2-A8` evaluate the character `w` of `F3.2-A5` on `T` and on `V`, and both cost
real analysis: the `η` `T`-transform from the `q`-product, and the Fricke intertwining through
Mathlib's `eta_comp_eq_csqrt_I_inv`.  This node evaluates `w` on **every** element of `Γ₀(N)`
that has a fixed point in `ℍ`, at **zero** analytic cost.

The argument is one substitution.  `F3.2-A5` gives, for all `z : ℍ`,
`f(e·z) = w(e)·(denom e z)^k·f(z)`.  Put `z = τ₀` with `e·τ₀ = τ₀`; the left side becomes
`f(τ₀)`, and `f(τ₀) ≠ 0` because eta quotients are nowhere zero on `ℍ` (`F3.1-B7`,
`etaQuotientH_ne_zero`).  Cancelling it leaves `w(e)·(denom e τ₀)^k = 1`, i.e.

  `w(e) = (denom e τ₀)^{-k}`  —  the INVERSE automorphy factor at the fixed point.

SCOPE, stated before the theorem (LL-1).  This does **not** prove that torsion elements of
`Γ₀(N)` have fixed points in `ℍ`; the fixed point is a HYPOTHESIS, supplied by the caller.  The
classical fact that an elliptic (i.e. `|tr| < 2`) element of `SL(2,ℤ)` fixes a point of `ℍ` is
not formalised here and is not used.  What is proved is the conditional: *given* a fixed point,
the multiplier is forced, with no freedom left.  Nor does this close `F3.2-OBSTRUCTED`: the
elliptic elements do not generate `Γ₀(N)` either.

The elements with fixed points are exactly the elliptic ones together with `±I`; on `-I` the
statement degenerates correctly (`etaMultiplierVal_neg_one`, `w(-I) = (-1)^k`, since `-I` fixes
every point and `denom (-I) z = -1`). -/

/-- **F3.2-A9, the core.**  If `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` fixes `τ₀ ∈ ℍ`, its multiplier is the inverse
automorphy factor at the fixed point: `w(γ) = (denom γ τ₀)^{-k}`.

Proof: `etaQuotientH_transform` (F3.2-A5) at `z = τ₀`, `rw` the fixed point on the left, cancel
`f(τ₀) ≠ 0` (F3.1-B7), and invert.  No analysis, no `q`-product, no Fricke. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_of_fixed {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N)
    {τ₀ : ℍ} (hfix : γ • τ₀ = τ₀) :
    etaMultiplierVal N r k γ = (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (τ₀ : ℂ)) ^ (-k) := by
  have h := etaQuotientH_transform r hk hγ τ₀
  rw [hfix] at h
  have hf : etaQuotientH N r τ₀ ≠ 0 := etaQuotientH_ne_zero N r τ₀
  have hcancel : etaMultiplierVal N r k γ * (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (τ₀ : ℂ)) ^ k = 1 := by
    refine mul_right_cancel₀ hf ?_
    rw [one_mul]
    exact h.symm
  rw [_root_.zpow_neg]
  exact eq_inv_of_mul_eq_one_left hcancel

/-- The pointwise transformation law of an element with a fixed point: the automorphy factor is
completely explicit, the multiplier having been replaced by its value at `τ₀`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_elliptic_transform {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N)
    {τ₀ : ℍ} (hfix : γ • τ₀ = τ₀) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (γ • z)
      = (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (τ₀ : ℂ)) ^ (-k)
        * (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  rw [etaQuotientH_transform r hk hγ z, etaMultiplierVal_of_fixed r hk hγ hfix]

/-- **F3.2-A9, the scoped statement**, in the `ℂˣ`-valued character of `F3.2-A5`:
`w e = (denom e τ₀)^{-k}` for `e : Γ₀(N)` fixing `τ₀`.

HONEST NOTE: `_hN : 0 < N` is not used, exactly as in `F3.2-A5`; it is kept because it is the
scoped signature. -/
theorem multiplier_elliptic {N : ℕ} (_hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (e : Gamma0 N) (τ₀ : ℍ)
    (hfix : (e : SL(2, ℤ)) • τ₀ = τ₀) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk e : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = (denom ((e : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (τ₀ : ℂ)) ^ (-k) := by
  simpa [etaMultiplierHom] using etaMultiplierVal_of_fixed r hk e.2 hfix

/-- `-I ∈ Γ₀(N)`: its lower-left entry is `0`. -/
theorem neg_one_mem_Gamma0 (N : ℕ) : (-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) ∈ Gamma0 N := by
  rw [Gamma0_mem]
  simp

/-- `denom (-I) z = -1`. -/
theorem denom_neg_one (z : ℍ) :
    denom (((-1 : SL(2, ℤ))) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) = -1 := by
  rw [ModularGroup.denom_apply]
  simp

/-- **The degenerate case of F3.2-A9**, and the one classical normalisation of the eta
multiplier that comes for free: `-I` fixes EVERY point of `ℍ` (`ModularGroup.SL_neg_smul`), so
A9 applies at any `τ₀` and gives `w(-I) = (-1)^{-k} = (-1)^k`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_neg_one {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) :
    etaMultiplierVal N r k (-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k := by
  have hfix : (-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) • UpperHalfPlane.I = UpperHalfPlane.I := by
    rw [ModularGroup.SL_neg_smul, one_smul]
  have hsq : ((-1 : ℂ) ^ k) * ((-1 : ℂ) ^ k) = 1 := by
    rw [← zpow_add₀ (by norm_num : (-1 : ℂ) ≠ 0), show k + k = 2 * k by ring,
      _root_.zpow_mul]
    norm_num
  rw [etaMultiplierVal_of_fixed r hk (neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N) hfix, denom_neg_one,
    _root_.zpow_neg]
  exact inv_eq_of_mul_eq_one_left hsq

/-! #### F3.2-A9 — LL-1 GUARD 1 (the SIGN of the exponent, pinned against Mathlib)

A green build would accept `w(e) = (denom e τ₀)^{+k}` just as happily as `^{-k}`, and at every
weight-`12` example (`Δ`) the two agree, because the automorphy factors there are `24`-th roots
of unity raised to `±12`.  The guard therefore uses weight `k = 1`: `N = 1`, `r ≡ 2`, i.e.
`f = η²`, where the `S`-multiplier is `-i` and the wrong sign would give `+i`.

Route A is `F3.2-A9` at the fixed point `S·i = i`, with `denom S i = i`.
Route B is `F3.2-A7` (`etaQuotient_fricke`), whose only analytic input is MATHLIB's
`eta_comp_eq_csqrt_I_inv`.  The two routes share nothing but the definition of the multiplier. -/

/-- `S` fixes `i`.  `modular_S_smul` says `S·z = (-z)⁻¹`, and `(-i)⁻¹ = i`. -/
theorem S_smul_I : ModularGroup.S • UpperHalfPlane.I = UpperHalfPlane.I := by
  rw [UpperHalfPlane.modular_S_smul]
  apply UpperHalfPlane.ext
  simp [UpperHalfPlane.coe_I, inv_neg, Complex.inv_I]

/-- ROUTE A.  `w(S) = -i` for `f = η²` at level `1`, straight from `F3.2-A9`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_eta_sq_S :
    etaMultiplierVal 1 (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) 1 ModularGroup.S = -I := by
  have hk : ∑ δ ∈ (1 : ℕ).divisors, (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) δ = 2 * 1 := by
    simp [Nat.divisors_one]
  rw [etaMultiplierVal_of_fixed (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) hk (mem_Gamma0_one _) S_smul_I]
  rw [ModularGroup.denom_S, UpperHalfPlane.coe_I, _root_.zpow_neg, _root_.zpow_one,
    Complex.inv_I]

/-- ROUTE B (the Fricke transformation law of `η²` at level `1`), from `F3.2-A7`.  At `N = 1`,
`r ≡ 2`, `k = 1` the constant is `i^{-1}·1^1·(√1)⁻¹ = -i` and the dual quotient is `f` itself. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_eta_sq_S_transform (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) (ModularGroup.S • z)
      = (-I) * (z : ℂ) * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) z := by
  have hk : ∑ δ ∈ (1 : ℕ).divisors, (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) δ = 2 * 1 := by
    simp [Nat.divisors_one]
  have h := etaQuotient_fricke (N := 1) one_pos (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) (k := 1) hk z.coe_im_pos
  have hcoe : ((ModularGroup.S • z : ℍ) : ℂ) = -(1 / ((1 : ℕ) * (z : ℂ))) := by
    rw [UpperHalfPlane.modular_S_smul]
    push_cast
    rw [UpperHalfPlane.coe_mk, one_mul, one_div, inv_neg]
  rw [etaQuotientH_eq_etaQuotient, hcoe, h]
  simp [Nat.divisors_one, Complex.inv_I, etaQuotientH_eq_etaQuotient, _root_.zpow_neg]

/-- GUARD 1.  Route B reproves Route A's value.  If `F3.2-A9` carried `^{+k}` instead of
`^{-k}`, Route A would deliver `+i` here and this would stop compiling. -/
example : etaMultiplierVal 1 (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) 1 ModularGroup.S = -I := by
  have hk : ∑ δ ∈ (1 : ℕ).divisors, (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) δ = 2 * 1 := by
    simp [Nat.divisors_one]
  have hA := etaQuotientH_transform (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) hk
    (γ := ModularGroup.S) (mem_Gamma0_one ModularGroup.S) UpperHalfPlane.I
  have hB := etaQuotientH_eta_sq_S_transform UpperHalfPlane.I
  rw [hB, ModularGroup.denom_S, _root_.zpow_one] at hA
  have hf : etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (2 : ℤ)) UpperHalfPlane.I ≠ 0 :=
    etaQuotientH_ne_zero _ _ _
  have hz : ((UpperHalfPlane.I : ℍ) : ℂ) ≠ 0 := by
    rw [UpperHalfPlane.coe_I]; exact Complex.I_ne_zero
  have := mul_right_cancel₀ hf hA
  exact mul_right_cancel₀ hz this.symm

/-! #### F3.2-A9 — LL-1 GUARD 2 (the elliptic point of `Γ₀(2)`, two independent derivations)

`Γ₀(2)` has a single elliptic point, `τ₀ = (1+i)/2`, fixed by `E = !![1,-1;2,-1]` (`tr E = 0`,
so `E² = -I`).  Two derivations of `w(E)`:

* VIA A9: `denom E τ₀ = 2τ₀ - 1 = i`, so `w(E) = i^{-k}`; and Ligozat's congruences (i)+(ii) at
  `N = 2` force `4 ∣ k` by pure arithmetic (`four_dvd_of_ligozat_two` below — add the two
  congruences: `Σ(δ + 2/δ)r_δ = 3Σr_δ = 6k`, so `24 ∣ 6k`), whence `i^{-k} = 1`.
* VIA A6 + A8: `V·E·T = 1` in `SL(2,ℤ)` (checked entrywise), so `w(E) = w(V)⁻¹w(T)⁻¹ = 1`.

The two agree.  This is a genuine cross-check and not a tautology: the first route runs through
the fixed point and the ARITHMETIC of the two congruences, the second through the multiplicative
structure of the character and the `T`/`V` evaluations.  It is also the honest reason `Γ₀(2)` is
not new territory — `E` turns out to lie in `⟨T, V⟩`, which is exactly why it can be checked. -/

/-- The elliptic element of `Γ₀(2)`: `E = !![1,-1;2,-1]`, of trace `0` (so `E² = -I`). -/
def matE2 : SL(2, ℤ) :=
  ⟨!![1, -1; 2, -1], by simp [Matrix.det_fin_two_of]⟩

@[simp] theorem matE2_coe :
    ((matE2 : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ) = !![1, -1; 2, -1] := rfl

theorem matE2_mem_Gamma0 : matE2 ∈ Gamma0 2 := by
  rw [Gamma0_mem]
  simp [matE2]
  decide

/-- The elliptic point `(1+i)/2` of `Γ₀(2)`. -/
noncomputable def tauE2 : ℍ :=
  ⟨(1 + Complex.I) / 2, by norm_num [Complex.div_im, Complex.normSq_apply]⟩

@[simp] theorem coe_tauE2 : (tauE2 : ℂ) = (1 + Complex.I) / 2 := rfl

theorem denom_matE2 (z : ℍ) :
    denom ((matE2 : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) = 2 * (z : ℂ) - 1 := by
  rw [ModularGroup.denom_apply]
  simp [matE2]
  ring

theorem coe_matE2_smul (z : ℍ) :
    ((matE2 • z : ℍ) : ℂ) = ((z : ℂ) - 1) / (2 * (z : ℂ) - 1) := by
  rw [UpperHalfPlane.coe_specialLinearGroup_apply]
  simp [matE2]
  ring

/-- `E` fixes `(1+i)/2`: `((1+i)/2 - 1)/(2·(1+i)/2 - 1) = ((i-1)/2)/i = (1+i)/2`. -/
theorem matE2_smul_tauE2 : matE2 • tauE2 = tauE2 := by
  apply UpperHalfPlane.ext
  rw [coe_matE2_smul, coe_tauE2]
  have hden : 2 * ((1 + Complex.I) / 2) - 1 = Complex.I := by ring
  rw [hden, div_eq_iff Complex.I_ne_zero]
  linear_combination (-1 / 2 : ℂ) * Complex.I_sq

/-- `denom E τ₀ = 2τ₀ - 1 = i`. -/
theorem denom_matE2_tauE2 :
    denom ((matE2 : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (tauE2 : ℂ) = Complex.I := by
  rw [denom_matE2, coe_tauE2]
  ring

/-- **Pure arithmetic.**  Ligozat's two congruences at `N = 2` force `4 ∣ k`.  Adding them gives
`Σ_δ (δ + 2/δ) r_δ = 3 Σ_δ r_δ = 6k`, so `24 ∣ 6k`. -/
theorem four_dvd_of_ligozat_two {r : EtaExp} {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (2 : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k)
    (h1 : LigozatCongr1 2 r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 2 r) : (4 : ℤ) ∣ k := by
  have hdiv : (2 : ℕ).divisors = {1, 2} := by decide
  rw [LigozatCongr1, hdiv] at h1
  rw [LigozatCongr2, hdiv] at h2
  rw [hdiv] at hk
  norm_num [Finset.sum_insert, Finset.mem_singleton] at h1 h2 hk
  omega

/-- GUARD 2, ROUTE A: `w(E) = i^{-k} = 1` for any `r` satisfying Ligozat's congruences at
`N = 2`, via the fixed point `(1+i)/2` and `4 ∣ k`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_matE2_eq_one {r : EtaExp} {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (2 : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k)
    (h1 : LigozatCongr1 2 r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 2 r) :
    etaMultiplierVal 2 r k matE2 = 1 := by
  rw [etaMultiplierVal_of_fixed r hk matE2_mem_Gamma0 matE2_smul_tauE2, denom_matE2_tauE2]
  obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := four_dvd_of_ligozat_two hk h1 h2
  have hI4 : (Complex.I) ^ (4 : ℤ) = 1 := by
    rw [show (4 : ℤ) = ((4 : ℕ) : ℤ) by norm_num, zpow_natCast, Complex.I_pow_four]
  rw [hm, show (-(4 * m) : ℤ) = 4 * (-m) by ring, _root_.zpow_mul, hI4, _root_.one_zpow]

/-- `V · E · T = 1` in `SL(2,ℤ)` for `N = 2`: `V·E = !![1,-1;0,1] = T⁻¹`. -/
theorem matV_two_mul_matE2_mul_T : matV 2 * matE2 * ModularGroup.T = 1 := by
  apply Subtype.ext
  rw [Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul, Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul,
    Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_one, matV_coe, matE2_coe, ModularGroup.coe_T,
    Matrix.one_fin_two]
  norm_num [Matrix.mul_fin_two]

/-- GUARD 2, ROUTE B: the SAME value of `w(E)`, derived instead from `F3.2-A6` (`w(T) = 1`,
congruence (i)) and `F3.2-A8` (`w(V) = 1`, congruence (ii)) through the group law
`V·E·T = 1`.  Both routes must give `1`; a wrong sign in A9, a wrong fixed point, or a wrong
`denom` row would break the agreement. -/
example {r : EtaExp} {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (2 : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k)
    (h1 : LigozatCongr1 2 r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 2 r) :
    etaMultiplierVal 2 r k matE2 = 1 := by
  have hT : etaMultiplierVal 2 r k ModularGroup.T = 1 :=
    etaMultiplierVal_T_eq_one r hk h1
  have hV : etaMultiplierVal 2 r k (matV 2) = 1 :=
    etaMultiplierVal_V_eq_one (by norm_num) r hk h2
  have hmul₁ := etaMultiplierVal_mul r hk (mul_mem (matV_mem_Gamma0 2) matE2_mem_Gamma0)
    (T_mem_Gamma0 2)
  have hmul₂ := etaMultiplierVal_mul r hk (matV_mem_Gamma0 2) matE2_mem_Gamma0
  rw [matV_two_mul_matE2_mul_T, etaMultiplierVal_one, hmul₂, hV, hT] at hmul₁
  simpa using hmul₁.symm


/-! ### F3.2-B1 — `Γ₀(N)` is generated by `-I`, `T` and `V` for `N ≤ 4`

**THE NODE.**  `Subgroup.closure {-I, T, V} = Γ₀(N)` for `N ∈ {1,2,3,4}`, where `T = !![1,1;0,1]`
(`ModularGroup.T`) and `V = !![1,0;-N,1]` (`matV`, F3.2-A8).  There is no Mathlib precedent for
`N > 1`: Mathlib has `SpecialLinearGroup.SL2Z_generators : closure {S, T} = ⊤` and nothing about
generators of a congruence subgroup.

**WHY THIS NODE EXISTS.**  F3.2-A6 computes the eta multiplier `w` on `T`, F3.2-A8 computes it on
`V`, and `etaMultiplierVal_neg_one` computes it on `-I`.  `w` is a HOMOMORPHISM (F3.2-A5,
`etaMultiplierHom`).  A homomorphism is determined by its values on a generating set — so for the
levels this node covers, the three computed values determine `w` on all of `Γ₀(N)` WITHOUT the
eta multiplier system and WITHOUT Dedekind sums, which is exactly what F3.2-OBSTRUCTED is missing
in general.  This node supplies the generation statement; `F3.2-B2` below consumes it and
completes Ligozat's criterion for `N ≤ 4`.  It does NOT close `F3.2-OBSTRUCTED` for `N ≥ 5`.

**THE PROOF** is a Euclidean descent on `|c|`, the lower-left entry, run over `⟨T, V⟩`:

* `T^n · γ` replaces `a` by `A := a + n·c` and leaves `c` fixed (`T_zpow_mul_zero_zero`);
* `V^m · γ` replaces `c` by `-mN·a + c` and leaves `a` fixed (`matV_zpow_mul_one_zero`).

Write `c = N·c'` (legitimate: `N ∣ c` is exactly `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)`).  Choose `n` by BALANCED division
(`exists_balanced_add`, built on `Int.bmod`) so that `2|A| ≤ |c| = N|c'|`, then choose `m` the
same way so that `2|c' + mA| ≤ |A|`.  The new lower-left entry is `N(c' + mA)`, and it is strictly
smaller than `c` in absolute value **provided `|A| < 2|c'|`**.  That is where `N ≤ 4` enters, and
it is the ONLY place:

* `N ≤ 3`: `2|A| ≤ N|c'| ≤ 3|c'| < 4|c'|`, so `|A| < 2|c'|` outright.
* `N = 4`: `2|A| ≤ 4|c'|` gives only `|A| ≤ 2|c'|`.  The boundary `2|A| = |c|` is killed by
  ARITHMETIC, not by analysis: `A ∣ c` there, and `IsCoprime A c` (a restatement of `det γ = 1`)
  forces `A` to be a unit, hence `|c| = 2` — impossible when `4 ∣ c` and `c ≠ 0`.
* `N = 5` the same estimate FAILS, and `descent_bound_fails_at_five` records the counterexample
  `(A, c') = (5, 2)`: `2·5 ≤ 5·2` but `5 ≥ 2·2`.  An adversarial rerun of this whole block with
  `N ≤ 5` in place of `N ≤ 4` fails at exactly that inequality and nowhere else, so the
  hypothesis is load-bearing rather than decorative.

The descent terminates at `c = 0`, where `det γ = 1` forces `a = d = ±1` and `γ = ±T^b`.  One
degenerate branch remains: `A = 0` makes the `V`-step useless.  But `A = 0` forces `c ∣ a`, hence
`|c| = 1` by coprimality, hence `N = 1`; there `S = (T·V₁·T)⁻¹` is available inside the closure
and `S·T^n·γ` has lower-left entry `A = 0`.  So `S` is used, but ONLY at level one, where it
belongs to `Γ₀(1)`.

SCOPE (LL-1).  This is a statement about `Γ₀(N)` as an abstract subgroup of `SL(2,ℤ)`.  It says
nothing about eta quotients, and it is FALSE as stated for general `N` (already `Γ₀(5)` needs a
fourth generator).  `S_not_mem_closure_gens_two` is the guard that the theorem has not silently
degenerated into `closure = ⊤`. -/

/-- **Balanced division with remainder**, the arithmetic engine of the descent: for `y ≠ 0` there
is a `q` with `2|x + qy| ≤ |y|`.  The ordinary `Int.emod` bound `|r| < |y|` is NOT strong enough
here (it would only reach `N ≤ 2`); `Int.bmod` is, because its output lies in `(-|y|/2, |y|/2]`. -/
theorem exists_balanced_add (x : ℤ) {y : ℤ} (hy : y ≠ 0) :
    ∃ q : ℤ, 2 * (x + q * y).natAbs ≤ y.natAbs := by
  have hm : 0 < y.natAbs := Int.natAbs_pos.mpr hy
  have h1 := Int.le_bmod (x := x) (m := y.natAbs) hm
  have h2 := Int.bmod_lt (x := x) (m := y.natAbs) hm
  obtain ⟨q, hq⟩ : y ∣ x - Int.bmod x y.natAbs := Int.natAbs_dvd.mp Int.dvd_self_sub_bmod
  refine ⟨-q, ?_⟩
  have hxy : x + (-q) * y = Int.bmod x y.natAbs := by linear_combination hq
  rw [hxy]
  omega

/-- `det γ = 1` in entry form.  Used only through `IsCoprime (γ 0 0) (γ 1 0)`. -/
theorem sl_det_entries (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : γ 0 0 * γ 1 1 - γ 0 1 * γ 1 0 = 1 := by
  have h := γ.2
  rwa [Matrix.det_fin_two] at h

/-- `S⁻¹ = !![0,1;-1,0]`, from Mathlib's `ModularGroup.S_inv : S⁻¹ = -S`. -/
theorem coe_S_inv :
    ((ModularGroup.S⁻¹ : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ) = !![0, 1; -1, 0] := by
  rw [ModularGroup.S_inv]; simp [ModularGroup.coe_S]

/-- `V = S T^N S⁻¹`.  This is the cheap route to `V^m`: Mathlib already has `coe_T_zpow`, so
conjugation transports it to `V` through `conj_zpow` instead of a fresh `zpow` induction. -/
theorem matV_eq_conj (N : ℕ) :
    matV N = ModularGroup.S * ModularGroup.T ^ (N : ℤ) * ModularGroup.S⁻¹ := by
  apply Subtype.ext
  rw [Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul, Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul,
    ModularGroup.coe_S, ModularGroup.coe_T_zpow, coe_S_inv, matV_coe]
  norm_num [Matrix.mul_fin_two]

/-- `V^m = !![1,0;-mN,1]`. -/
theorem matV_zpow_coe (N : ℕ) (m : ℤ) :
    ((matV N ^ m : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ) = !![1, 0; -(m * (N : ℤ)), 1] := by
  rw [matV_eq_conj, conj_zpow, ← _root_.zpow_mul]
  rw [Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul, Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul,
    ModularGroup.coe_S, ModularGroup.coe_T_zpow, coe_S_inv]
  norm_num [Matrix.mul_fin_two]
  ring_nf

/-- The `T`-step of the descent: `T^n` shifts `a` by `n·c`. -/
theorem T_zpow_mul_zero_zero (n : ℤ) (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) :
    (ModularGroup.T ^ n * γ) 0 0 = γ 0 0 + n * γ 1 0 := by
  simp [Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul, ModularGroup.coe_T_zpow, Matrix.mul_apply,
    Fin.sum_univ_two]

/-- The `T`-step leaves the lower-left entry alone. -/
theorem T_zpow_mul_one_zero (n : ℤ) (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) :
    (ModularGroup.T ^ n * γ) 1 0 = γ 1 0 := by
  simp

/-- The `V`-step of the descent: `V^m` shifts `c` by `-mN·a`. -/
theorem matV_zpow_mul_one_zero (N : ℕ) (m : ℤ) (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) :
    (matV N ^ m * γ) 1 0 = -(m * (N : ℤ)) * γ 0 0 + γ 1 0 := by
  rw [show ((matV N ^ m * γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 0)
      = (((matV N ^ m : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ)
        * (γ : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ)) 1 0 from rfl, matV_zpow_coe]
  simp [Matrix.mul_apply, Fin.sum_univ_two]

/-- The degenerate step, used at level one only: `S` moves `a` into the lower-left slot. -/
theorem S_mul_one_zero (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : (ModularGroup.S * γ) 1 0 = γ 0 0 := by
  rw [show ((ModularGroup.S * γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 0)
      = (((ModularGroup.S : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ)
        * (γ : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ)) 1 0 from rfl, ModularGroup.coe_S]
  simp [Matrix.mul_apply, Fin.sum_univ_two]

/-- `(-I)·T^m = !![-1,-m;0,-1]`. -/
theorem coe_neg_one_mul_T_zpow (m : ℤ) :
    (((-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) * ModularGroup.T ^ m : SL(2, ℤ)) : Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) ℤ)
      = !![-1, -m; 0, -1] := by
  rw [Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul, ModularGroup.coe_T_zpow]
  norm_num [Matrix.mul_fin_two, Matrix.one_fin_two]

/-- Base case of the descent, positive branch: `c = 0` and `a = d = 1` give `γ = T^b`. -/
theorem eq_T_zpow_of_lower_left_zero {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hc : γ 1 0 = 0) (h1 : γ 0 0 = 1)
    (h2 : γ 1 1 = 1) : γ = ModularGroup.T ^ (γ 0 1) := by
  apply Subtype.ext
  rw [ModularGroup.coe_T_zpow]
  ext i j
  fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp [h1, h2, hc]

/-- Base case of the descent, negative branch: `c = 0` and `a = d = -1` give `γ = (-I)·T^{-b}`. -/
theorem eq_neg_T_zpow_of_lower_left_zero {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hc : γ 1 0 = 0) (h1 : γ 0 0 = -1)
    (h2 : γ 1 1 = -1) : γ = (-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) * ModularGroup.T ^ (-(γ 0 1)) := by
  apply Subtype.ext
  rw [coe_neg_one_mul_T_zpow]
  ext i j
  fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;> simp [h1, h2, hc]

/-- The generating set of the node: `{-I, T, V}`.  `matV_coe` (F3.2-A8) is the receipt that the
third element really is the matrix `!![1,0;-N,1]`. -/
def gamma0Gens (N : ℕ) : Set SL(2, ℤ) := {(-1 : SL(2, ℤ)), ModularGroup.T, matV N}

/-- The EASY inclusion, valid for every `N`: all three generators lie in `Γ₀(N)`. -/
theorem closure_gamma0Gens_le (N : ℕ) : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) ≤ Gamma0 N := by
  rw [Subgroup.closure_le]
  rintro g hg
  simp only [gamma0Gens, Set.mem_insert_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff] at hg
  rcases hg with rfl | rfl | rfl
  · exact neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N
  · exact T_mem_Gamma0 N
  · exact matV_mem_Gamma0 N

theorem neg_one_mem_closure_gens (N : ℕ) : (-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) :=
  Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp [gamma0Gens])

theorem T_mem_closure_gens (N : ℕ) : ModularGroup.T ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) :=
  Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp [gamma0Gens])

theorem matV_mem_closure_gens (N : ℕ) : matV N ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) :=
  Subgroup.subset_closure (by simp [gamma0Gens])

/-- `T V₁ T = S⁻¹`.  At level one this puts `S` inside `⟨T, V₁⟩`, which is what rescues the one
branch of the descent (`A = 0`) where the `V`-step is useless. -/
theorem T_mul_matV_one_mul_T : ModularGroup.T * matV 1 * ModularGroup.T = ModularGroup.S⁻¹ := by
  apply Subtype.ext
  rw [Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul, Matrix.SpecialLinearGroup.coe_mul,
    ModularGroup.coe_T, matV_coe, coe_S_inv]
  norm_num [Matrix.mul_fin_two]

theorem S_mem_closure_gens_one : ModularGroup.S ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens 1) := by
  have h : ModularGroup.S = (ModularGroup.T * matV 1 * ModularGroup.T)⁻¹ := by
    rw [T_mul_matV_one_mul_T, inv_inv]
  rw [h]
  exact inv_mem (mul_mem (mul_mem (T_mem_closure_gens 1) (matV_mem_closure_gens 1))
    (T_mem_closure_gens 1))

/-- **The descent step.**  For `1 ≤ N ≤ 4` and `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` with `c ≠ 0` there is a word `g` in the
generators with `|c(g·γ)| < |c(γ)|`.  This is the entire mathematical content of the node; the
induction below is bookkeeping. -/
theorem gamma0_descent {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)}
    (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (hc : γ 1 0 ≠ 0) :
    ∃ g ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N), ((g * γ) 1 0).natAbs < (γ 1 0).natAbs := by
  have hdet := sl_det_entries γ
  obtain ⟨n, hn⟩ := exists_balanced_add (γ 0 0) hc
  set A : ℤ := γ 0 0 + n * γ 1 0 with hAdef
  have hcopA : IsCoprime A (γ 1 0) :=
    ⟨γ 1 1, -(γ 0 1) - n * γ 1 1, by rw [hAdef]; linear_combination hdet⟩
  have hNdvd : (N : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0 := dvd_lower_left_of_mem_Gamma0 dvd_rfl hγ
  by_cases hA0 : A = 0
  · -- `A = 0` forces `|c| = 1`, hence `N = 1`; `S · T^n · γ` then has lower-left entry `0`.
    have hunit : IsUnit (γ 1 0) := by
      rw [hA0] at hcopA; exact isCoprime_zero_left.mp hcopA
    have hc1 : (γ 1 0).natAbs = 1 := Int.isUnit_iff.mp hunit |>.elim
      (fun h => by simp [h]) (fun h => by simp [h])
    have hNdvd' : N ∣ (γ 1 0).natAbs := by simpa using Int.natAbs_dvd_natAbs.mpr hNdvd
    have hN1 : N = 1 := Nat.dvd_one.mp (hc1 ▸ hNdvd')
    subst hN1
    refine ⟨ModularGroup.S * ModularGroup.T ^ n, ?_, ?_⟩
    · exact mul_mem S_mem_closure_gens_one (Subgroup.zpow_mem _ (T_mem_closure_gens 1) n)
    · have hz : (ModularGroup.S * ModularGroup.T ^ n * γ) 1 0 = 0 := by
        rw [mul_assoc, S_mul_one_zero, T_zpow_mul_zero_zero, ← hAdef, hA0]
      rw [hz]
      simp [hc1]
  · obtain ⟨c', hc'⟩ := hNdvd
    have hc'0 : c' ≠ 0 := by
      intro h; apply hc; rw [hc', h, mul_zero]
    have habs : (γ 1 0).natAbs = N * c'.natAbs := by
      rw [hc', Int.natAbs_mul]; simp
    have hcn : 0 < c'.natAbs := Int.natAbs_pos.mpr hc'0
    -- THE inequality that needs `N ≤ 4`.
    have hkey : A.natAbs < 2 * c'.natAbs := by
      rcases Nat.lt_or_ge N 4 with h4 | h4
      · interval_cases N <;> omega
      · have hN4' : N = 4 := le_antisymm hN4 h4
        subst hN4'
        by_contra hcon
        simp only [not_lt] at hcon
        have heq : (γ 1 0).natAbs = (2 * A).natAbs := by
          rw [Int.natAbs_mul]; simp; omega
        have hdvd : A ∣ γ 1 0 := by
          rcases Int.natAbs_eq_natAbs_iff.mp heq with h | h
          · exact ⟨2, by linarith [h]⟩
          · exact ⟨-2, by linarith [h]⟩
        have hu := Int.isUnit_iff.mp (hcopA.isUnit_of_dvd hdvd)
        have hA1 : A.natAbs = 1 := by rcases hu with h | h <;> simp [h]
        omega
    obtain ⟨m, hm⟩ := exists_balanced_add c' hA0
    refine ⟨matV N ^ (-m) * ModularGroup.T ^ n, ?_, ?_⟩
    · exact mul_mem (Subgroup.zpow_mem _ (matV_mem_closure_gens N) _)
        (Subgroup.zpow_mem _ (T_mem_closure_gens N) n)
    · rw [mul_assoc, matV_zpow_mul_one_zero, T_zpow_mul_zero_zero, T_zpow_mul_one_zero, ← hAdef]
      have hval : -(-m * (N : ℤ)) * A + γ 1 0 = (N : ℤ) * (c' + m * A) := by rw [hc']; ring
      rw [hval, Int.natAbs_mul, habs]
      have hlt : (c' + m * A).natAbs < c'.natAbs := by omega
      simp only [Int.natAbs_natCast]
      exact _root_.mul_lt_mul_of_pos_left hlt hN

/-- Strong induction on `|c|`, driven by `gamma0_descent`. -/
theorem mem_closure_gamma0Gens_aux {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) :
    ∀ (k : ℕ) (γ : SL(2, ℤ)), γ ∈ Gamma0 N → (γ 1 0).natAbs = k →
      γ ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) := by
  intro k
  induction k using Nat.strong_induction_on with
  | _ k ih =>
    intro γ hγ hk
    by_cases hc : γ 1 0 = 0
    · have hdet := sl_det_entries γ
      rw [hc, mul_zero, sub_zero] at hdet
      rcases Int.mul_eq_one_iff_eq_one_or_neg_one.mp hdet with ⟨h1, h2⟩ | ⟨h1, h2⟩
      · rw [eq_T_zpow_of_lower_left_zero hc h1 h2]
        exact Subgroup.zpow_mem _ (T_mem_closure_gens N) _
      · rw [eq_neg_T_zpow_of_lower_left_zero hc h1 h2]
        exact mul_mem (neg_one_mem_closure_gens N)
          (Subgroup.zpow_mem _ (T_mem_closure_gens N) _)
    · obtain ⟨g, hg, hlt⟩ := gamma0_descent hN hN4 hγ hc
      have hgγ : g * γ ∈ Gamma0 N := mul_mem (closure_gamma0Gens_le N hg) hγ
      have hmem : g * γ ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) :=
        ih ((g * γ) 1 0).natAbs (hk ▸ hlt) (g * γ) hgγ rfl
      have hsplit : γ = g⁻¹ * (g * γ) := by group
      rw [hsplit]
      exact mul_mem (inv_mem hg) hmem

/-- **F3.2-B1.**  For `N ∈ {1,2,3,4}`, `Γ₀(N)` is generated by `-I`, `T = !![1,1;0,1]` and
`V = !![1,0;-N,1]`.  Both inclusions are proved: `≤` because the three generators lie in `Γ₀(N)`,
`≥` by the Euclidean descent above. -/
theorem Gamma0_generated_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) :
    Subgroup.closure {(-1 : SL(2, ℤ)), ModularGroup.T, matV N} = Gamma0 N := by
  apply le_antisymm (closure_gamma0Gens_le N)
  intro γ hγ
  exact mem_closure_gamma0Gens_aux hN hN4 (γ 1 0).natAbs γ hγ rfl

/-- The node's own `ℕ+` phrasing.  (`CongruenceSubgroup.Gamma0 N` is already a `Subgroup`, so the
`.toSubgroup` of the scoped statement has no counterpart here.) -/
theorem Gamma0_generated_of_le_four_pnat {N : ℕ+} (hN : (N : ℕ) ≤ 4) :
    Subgroup.closure {(-1 : SL(2, ℤ)), ModularGroup.T, matV (N : ℕ)} = Gamma0 (N : ℕ) :=
  Gamma0_generated_of_le_four N.pos hN

theorem closure_gamma0Gens_eq {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) :
    Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) = Gamma0 N :=
  Gamma0_generated_of_le_four hN hN4

/-! #### F3.2-B1 — the LL-1 guards -/

/-- `Γ₀(1) = SL(2,ℤ)`. -/
theorem Gamma0_one_top : Gamma0 1 = ⊤ := by
  ext γ
  simp [Gamma0_mem, Subsingleton.elim ((γ 1 0 : ℤ) : ZMod 1) 0]

/-- GUARD 1 (agreement with Mathlib at level one).  Specialised to `N = 1` the node says
`SL(2,ℤ) = ⟨-I, T, V₁⟩`, which is the `S ↦ (T V₁ T)⁻¹` rewriting of Mathlib's
`SpecialLinearGroup.SL2Z_generators`.  If the descent had the wrong `V`, this would fail. -/
theorem gamma0Gens_one_top : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens 1) = ⊤ := by
  rw [closure_gamma0Gens_eq (N := 1) one_pos (by norm_num), Gamma0_one_top]

/-- GUARD 2 (the theorem is not the trivial one).  `S ∉ Γ₀(2)`, so at `N = 2` the closure is a
PROPER subgroup of `SL(2,ℤ)`: the node did not silently degenerate into `closure = ⊤`. -/
theorem S_not_mem_closure_gens_two : ModularGroup.S ∉ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens 2) := by
  intro h
  have h2 := closure_gamma0Gens_le 2 h
  rw [Gamma0_mem] at h2
  simp [ModularGroup.coe_S] at h2

theorem closure_gamma0Gens_two_ne_top : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens 2) ≠ ⊤ := by
  intro h
  exact S_not_mem_closure_gens_two (h ▸ Subgroup.mem_top _)

/-- GUARD 3 (where `N ≤ 4` is spent).  The descent inequality `2|A| ≤ N|c'| → |A| < 2|c'|` is
FALSE at `N = 5`: take `A = 5`, `c' = 2`.  Rerunning the block above with `N ≤ 5` fails at
`gamma0_descent`'s `hkey` and at no other step, so the hypothesis is load-bearing. -/
theorem descent_bound_fails_at_five :
    ¬ ∀ A c' : ℤ, 2 * A.natAbs ≤ 5 * c'.natAbs → A.natAbs < 2 * c'.natAbs := by
  intro h
  have := h 5 2 (by norm_num)
  norm_num at this

/-- A concrete element of `Γ₀(4)` that no hand computation with `T` and `V` makes obvious. -/
def testGamma0Four : SL(2, ℤ) := ⟨!![3, 1; 8, 3], by norm_num [Matrix.det_fin_two_of]⟩

/-- GUARD 4 (the theorem is usable).  `!![3,1;8,3] ∈ ⟨-I, T, V₄⟩`. -/
theorem testGamma0Four_mem_closure : testGamma0Four ∈ Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens 4) := by
  rw [closure_gamma0Gens_eq (N := 4) (by norm_num) (by norm_num), Gamma0_mem]
  decide

/-! ### F3.2-B2 — full Ligozat for `N ≤ 4`

`multiplier_T_eq_one` (A6) and `multiplier_V_eq_one` (A8) compute the multiplier `w` of `F3.2-A5`
at `T` and at `V`; `etaMultiplierVal_neg_one` (A9) computes it at `-I`, unconditionally.
`Gamma0_generated_of_le_four` (B1) says that for `N ≤ 4` those three matrices GENERATE `Γ₀(N)`.
Since `w` is a group homomorphism, its values on a generating set determine it: that is the whole
content of this block, and it is pure group theory — no new analysis, and in particular no
Dedekind sums.

**A CORRECTION, STATED LOUDLY (LL-1).**  The statement this block was scoped around,

  `f(γz) = (denom γ z)^k · f(z)` for all `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)`, given (i), (ii) and a generation hypothesis,

is **FALSE as written**, and the sorry that used to stand here was a sorry on a false statement.
Taking `γ = -I` — which lies in `Γ₀(N)` and is in the closure of the generators — the left side is
`f(z)` (because `-I` acts trivially on `ℍ`) while the right side is `(-1)^k f(z)`; since `f` never
vanishes the two agree iff `k` is EVEN.  Ligozat's congruences do **not** force `k` even: the
counterexample landed below is `N = 3`, `r₁ = -3`, `r₃ = 9`, so `Σ_δ r_δ = 6 = 2·3` with `k = 3`,
`Σ_δ δ r_δ = 24 ≡ 0 (24)` and `Σ_δ (3/δ) r_δ = 0 ≡ 0 (24)` — both congruences hold, `N ≤ 4` so the
generation hypothesis is a theorem (B1), and `not_multiplier_trivial_odd` proves the conclusion
fails.  Classically this is the familiar nebentypus: `η(z)⁻³η(3z)⁹` is a weight-`3` form on
`Γ₀(3)` **with the quadratic character**, not with trivial character.

So `multiplier_trivial_of_congr` is landed here with one hypothesis ADDED, `Even k`, and with the
counterexample proving that the added hypothesis is load-bearing rather than defensive.  The
character-valued form, `ligozat_of_le_four`, needs no parity hypothesis and is the node's own
statement: `w₀` is THE unique homomorphism `Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ` with `w₀ T = 1`, `w₀ V = 1`,
`w₀ (-I) = (-1)^k`, and `f(γz) = w₀(γ)·(denom γ z)^k·f(z)` for every `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)`.

SCOPE.  `N ≤ 4` is inherited from B1 and is not decoration: at `N = 5` the three matrices no
longer generate (`descent_bound_fails_at_five` records where the descent breaks), and for general
`N` the reduction of `Γ₀(N)` to `⟨-I, T, V⟩` is false.  The Dedekind-sum obstruction
(`F3.2-OBSTRUCTED`, retained in the docstring of this file) is therefore untouched for `N ≥ 5`:
what this block does is show that for `N ≤ 4` the obstruction is *not needed*, because generation
replaces evaluation. -/

/-- The three generators of `Γ₀(N)`, as elements of the SUBGROUP `Γ₀(N)` rather than of
`SL(2,ℤ)`.  A character of `Γ₀(N)` is a homomorphism out of this subtype, so uniqueness has to be
argued there, not in `SL(2,ℤ)`. -/
def gamma0GensSub (N : ℕ) : Set (Gamma0 N) :=
  {⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩, ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩, ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩}

@[simp] theorem image_gamma0GensSub (N : ℕ) :
    (Gamma0 N).subtype '' (gamma0GensSub N) = gamma0Gens N := by
  simp [gamma0GensSub, gamma0Gens, Set.image_insert_eq]

/-- `{-I, T, V}` and `{T, V, -I}` are the same set; only the ORDER of the three elements differs
between `gamma0Gens` (B1's phrasing) and the `hgen` hypothesis this block inherits. -/
theorem gamma0Gens_eq_perm (N : ℕ) :
    gamma0Gens N = ({ModularGroup.T, matV N, -1} : Set SL(2, ℤ)) := by
  ext x
  simp only [gamma0Gens, Set.mem_insert_iff, Set.mem_singleton_iff]
  tauto

theorem closure_gamma0Gens_eq_of_hgen {N : ℕ}
    (hgen : ∀ γ ∈ Gamma0 N, γ ∈ Subgroup.closure ({ModularGroup.T, matV N, -1} : Set SL(2, ℤ))) :
    Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) = Gamma0 N := by
  refine le_antisymm (closure_gamma0Gens_le N) fun γ hγ => ?_
  rw [gamma0Gens_eq_perm]
  exact hgen γ hγ

/-- Generation transported from `SL(2,ℤ)` into the subtype: if `⟨-I, T, V⟩ = Γ₀(N)` inside
`SL(2,ℤ)`, then the same three elements generate the WHOLE group `Γ₀(N)`.  The transport is
`Subgroup.map_subtype_inj` (the subtype inclusion is injective, so `map` reflects equality)
together with `MonoidHom.map_closure` and `Subgroup.range_subtype`. -/
theorem closure_gamma0GensSub_eq_top_of_gen {N : ℕ}
    (hgen : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) = Gamma0 N) :
    Subgroup.closure (gamma0GensSub N) = ⊤ := by
  rw [← Subgroup.map_subtype_inj (H := Gamma0 N), MonoidHom.map_closure, image_gamma0GensSub,
    hgen, ← MonoidHom.range_eq_map, Subgroup.range_subtype]

/-- The `N ≤ 4` instance, via `F3.2-B1`. -/
theorem closure_gamma0GensSub_eq_top {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) :
    Subgroup.closure (gamma0GensSub N) = ⊤ :=
  closure_gamma0GensSub_eq_top_of_gen (closure_gamma0Gens_eq hN hN4)

/-- **Uniqueness of a character by its values on the three generators.**  Stated for an arbitrary
monoid target, because nothing about `ℂˣ` is used: it is `MonoidHom.eq_of_eqOn_dense`. -/
theorem hom_ext_of_gen {N : ℕ} (hgen : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) = Gamma0 N)
    {M : Type*} [Monoid M] {w w' : Gamma0 N →* M}
    (hm : w ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = w' ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩)
    (hT : w ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = w' ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩)
    (hV : w ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = w' ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩) :
    w = w' := by
  refine MonoidHom.eq_of_eqOn_dense (closure_gamma0GensSub_eq_top_of_gen hgen) ?_
  rintro x (rfl | rfl | rfl)
  · exact hm
  · exact hT
  · exact hV

theorem hom_ext_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4)
    {M : Type*} [Monoid M] {w w' : Gamma0 N →* M}
    (hm : w ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = w' ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩)
    (hT : w ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = w' ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩)
    (hV : w ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = w' ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩) :
    w = w' :=
  hom_ext_of_gen (closure_gamma0Gens_eq hN hN4) hm hT hV

/-- `w(-I) = (-1)^k` for the BUNDLED character (`etaMultiplierVal_neg_one` is the `ℂ`-valued
form).  No congruence hypothesis: this holds for every `N` and every `r` of even weight-sum. -/
theorem etaMultiplierHom_neg_one {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) :
    etaMultiplierHom r hk ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = (-1 : ℂˣ) ^ k := by
  apply Units.ext
  simpa [etaMultiplierHom] using etaMultiplierVal_neg_one r hk

/-- **The uniqueness half of F3.2-B2.**  Under `(i)` and `(ii)`, with `⟨-I, T, V⟩ = Γ₀(N)`, the
eta multiplier of `F3.2-A5` is THE unique homomorphism `Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ` taking the values `1`, `1`,
`(-1)^k` at `T`, `V`, `-I`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierHom_eq_of_gen {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N)
    (hgen : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) = Gamma0 N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (w : Gamma0 N →* ℂˣ)
    (hwT : w ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1)
    (hwV : w ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1)
    (hwm : w ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = (-1 : ℂˣ) ^ k) :
    w = etaMultiplierHom r hk := by
  refine hom_ext_of_gen hgen ?_ ?_ ?_
  · rw [hwm, etaMultiplierHom_neg_one]
  · rw [hwT, etaMultiplierHom_T_eq_one r hk h1]
  · rw [hwV, etaMultiplierHom_V_eq_one hN r hk h2]

theorem etaMultiplierHom_eq_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (w : Gamma0 N →* ℂˣ)
    (hwT : w ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1)
    (hwV : w ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1)
    (hwm : w ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = (-1 : ℂˣ) ^ k) :
    w = etaMultiplierHom r hk :=
  etaMultiplierHom_eq_of_gen hN (closure_gamma0Gens_eq hN hN4) r hk h1 h2 w hwT hwV hwm

/-- **F3.2-B2, THE HEADLINE.**  Full Ligozat for `N ≤ 4`, in the node's own phrasing: `w₀` is
*any* homomorphism `Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ` with `w₀ T = 1`, `w₀ V = 1`, `w₀ (-I) = (-1)^k` — by
`etaMultiplierHom_eq_of_le_four` there is exactly one such, so "any" and "the unique one" say the
same thing — and the eta quotient transforms with it on ALL of `Γ₀(N)`:

  `f(γ z) = w₀(γ) · (cz + d)^k · f(z)`   for every `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` and every `z ∈ ℍ`.

WHAT MAKES THIS MORE THAN `F3.2-A5`.  A5 already produces *a* character with this transformation
law, for every `N`, with no congruence conditions; what A5 cannot do is say what that character
is.  Here `(i)` and `(ii)` pin it at `T` and at `V` (A6, A8), `A9` pins it at `-I`, and `B1` says
those three generate — so the character is determined, and Ligozat's congruences have been turned
from "conditions on two generators" into "the transformation law on the whole group". -/
theorem ligozat_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (w : Gamma0 N →* ℂˣ)
    (hwT : w ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1)
    (hwV : w ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1)
    (hwm : w ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = (-1 : ℂˣ) ^ k) :
    ∀ (γ : Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ),
      etaQuotientH N r ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) • z)
        = (w γ : ℂ) * (denom ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k
          * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  intro γ z
  rw [etaMultiplierHom_eq_of_le_four hN hN4 r hk h1 h2 w hwT hwV hwm]
  exact etaQuotientH_transform r hk γ.2 z

/-- The `ℕ+` phrasing of the node, `hN : (N : ℕ) ≤ 4`. -/
theorem ligozat_of_le_four_pnat {N : ℕ+} (hN : (N : ℕ) ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (N : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k)
    (h1 : LigozatCongr1 (N : ℕ) r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 (N : ℕ) r)
    (w : Gamma0 (N : ℕ) →* ℂˣ)
    (hwT : w ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 (N : ℕ)⟩ = 1)
    (hwV : w ⟨matV (N : ℕ), matV_mem_Gamma0 (N : ℕ)⟩ = 1)
    (hwm : w ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 (N : ℕ)⟩ = (-1 : ℂˣ) ^ k) :
    ∀ (γ : Gamma0 (N : ℕ)) (z : ℍ),
      etaQuotientH (N : ℕ) r ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) • z)
        = (w γ : ℂ) * (denom ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k
          * etaQuotientH (N : ℕ) r z :=
  ligozat_of_le_four N.pos hN r hk h1 h2 w hwT hwV hwm

/-- For EVEN weight `k` the character is `1`: the constant homomorphism has the same three
generator values (`(-1)^k = 1` for even `k`), so uniqueness identifies the two. -/
theorem etaMultiplierHom_eq_one_of_gen {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N)
    (hgen : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) = Gamma0 N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (hke : Even k) :
    etaMultiplierHom r hk = 1 :=
  (etaMultiplierHom_eq_of_gen hN hgen r hk h1 h2 1 rfl rfl (by rw [hke.neg_one_zpow]; rfl)).symm

theorem etaMultiplierHom_eq_one_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (hke : Even k) :
    etaMultiplierHom r hk = 1 :=
  etaMultiplierHom_eq_one_of_gen hN (closure_gamma0Gens_eq hN hN4) r hk h1 h2 hke

/-- The pointwise transformation law with TRIVIAL multiplier, for even `k`. -/
theorem etaQuotientH_transform_of_gen {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N)
    (hgen : Subgroup.closure (gamma0Gens N) = Gamma0 N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (hke : Even k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (γ • z)
      = (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  have h := etaQuotientH_transform r hk hγ z
  have hw : etaMultiplierVal N r k γ = 1 := by
    have hone := etaMultiplierHom_eq_one_of_gen hN hgen r hk h1 h2 hke
    simpa [etaMultiplierHom] using congrArg (fun f => ((f ⟨γ, hγ⟩ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)) hone
  rw [h, hw, one_mul]

theorem etaQuotientH_transform_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (hke : Even k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (γ • z)
      = (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z :=
  etaQuotientH_transform_of_gen hN (closure_gamma0Gens_eq hN hN4) r hk h1 h2 hke hγ z

/-- The block's original `hgen`-shaped statement, now PROVED — with the hypothesis `Even k`
ADDED.  See the section docstring and `not_multiplier_trivial_odd`: without `Even k` the
statement is false at `γ = -I`, so this is a correction of the statement, not a strengthening of
the proof. -/
theorem multiplier_trivial_of_congr {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k)
    (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r) (hke : Even k)
    (hgen : ∀ γ ∈ Gamma0 N, γ ∈ Subgroup.closure ({ModularGroup.T, matV N, -1} : Set SL(2, ℤ)))
    {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r (γ • z) = (denom γ z) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z :=
  etaQuotientH_transform_of_gen hN (closure_gamma0Gens_eq_of_hgen hgen) r hk h1 h2 hke hγ z

/-! #### F3.2-B2 — the packaged modular form

Ligozat's condition (iii) enters here and only here, in Mathlib's own `IsBoundedAtImInfty` form
(the node's `hbd`).  Everything else is already proved: slash invariance is the transformation law
with the `denom` powers cancelled, and holomorphy is `differentiableAt_etaQuotient` (F3.1-B8)
transported to the manifold `ℍ` by `UpperHalfPlane.mdifferentiable_iff`.

WHY `hbd` IS A HYPOTHESIS AND NOT A LEMMA: turning the arithmetic condition `LigozatHolomorphic`
(nonnegativity of `etaQuotientCuspOrder` at every cusp) into `IsBoundedAtImInfty` at every cusp
requires knowing that `etaQuotientCuspOrder` really is the order of vanishing at that cusp, which
is exactly `F3.1-OBSTRUCTED`.  Only the cusp `∞` is available (F3.1-B13).  The node scoped `hbd`
as a hypothesis for that reason and it stays one. -/

/-- Slash invariance in weight `k`, for even `k`: `f ∣[k] g = f` for every `g` in the image of
`Γ₀(N)` in `GL(2,ℝ)`.  This is `etaQuotientH_transform_of_le_four` with the two `denom` powers
cancelled (`zpow_add₀`, legal since `denom γ z ≠ 0`). -/
theorem etaQuotientH_slash_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (hke : Even k) :
    ∀ g ∈ (Gamma0 N : Subgroup (GL (Fin 2) ℝ)),
      (etaQuotientH N r) ∣[k] g = etaQuotientH N r := by
  rintro _ ⟨γ, hγ, rfl⟩
  show (etaQuotientH N r) ∣[k] (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) = etaQuotientH N r
  rw [← ModularForm.SL_slash]
  funext τ
  rw [ModularForm.SL_slash_apply]
  have hd : (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (τ : ℂ)) ≠ 0 := denom_ne_zero _ τ
  rw [etaQuotientH_transform_of_le_four hN hN4 r hk h1 h2 hke hγ τ, mul_right_comm,
    ← zpow_add₀ hd, add_neg_cancel, zpow_zero, one_mul]

section Manifold
open scoped Manifold

/-- Holomorphy of the eta quotient in Mathlib's manifold sense, `MDifferentiable 𝓘(ℂ) 𝓘(ℂ)` —
the shape `ModularForm.holo'` asks for.  `differentiableAt_etaQuotient` (F3.1-B8) gives complex
differentiability on the open set `ℍₒ ⊆ ℂ`; `UpperHalfPlane.mdifferentiable_iff` is the bridge,
and `ofComplex` is undone on `ℍₒ` by `ofComplex_apply_of_im_pos`. -/
theorem mdiff_etaQuotientH (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) :
    MDifferentiable 𝓘(ℂ) 𝓘(ℂ) (etaQuotientH N r) := by
  rw [UpperHalfPlane.mdifferentiable_iff]
  have heq : Set.EqOn (etaQuotientH N r ∘ UpperHalfPlane.ofComplex) (etaQuotient N r)
      {z : ℂ | 0 < z.im} := by
    intro z hz
    simp [etaQuotientH, UpperHalfPlane.ofComplex_apply_of_im_pos hz]
  exact DifferentiableOn.congr
    (fun z hz => (differentiableAt_etaQuotient r hz).differentiableWithinAt) heq

end Manifold

/-- The image of `Γ₀(N)` in `GL(2,ℝ)` sits inside the image of `SL(2,ℤ)`. -/
theorem Gamma0_map_le_SL (N : ℕ) : ((Gamma0 N : Subgroup (GL (Fin 2) ℝ))) ≤ 𝒮ℒ := by
  rintro _ ⟨γ, -, rfl⟩
  exact ⟨γ, rfl⟩

/-- Ligozat's condition (iii), in the form `ModularForm.bdd_at_cusps'` wants it.  Every cusp of
`Γ₀(N)` is a cusp of `SL(2,ℤ)` (`IsCusp.mono`), and there Mathlib's
`isBoundedAt_iff_forall_SL2Z` turns "bounded at `c`" into the node's hypothesis `hbd`. -/
theorem isBoundedAt_of_bdd_SL2Z {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hbd : ∀ γ : SL(2, ℤ), IsBoundedAtImInfty ((etaQuotientH N r) ∣[k] γ))
    {c : OnePoint ℝ} (hc : IsCusp c ((Gamma0 N : Subgroup (GL (Fin 2) ℝ)))) :
    c.IsBoundedAt (etaQuotientH N r) k :=
  (OnePoint.isBoundedAt_iff_forall_SL2Z (hc.mono (Gamma0_map_le_SL N))).mpr fun γ _ => hbd γ

/-- **F3.2-B2, packaged.**  For `N ≤ 4`, EVEN weight `k`, Ligozat's congruences (i) and (ii), and
condition (iii) as `hbd`, the eta quotient `∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δz)^{r_δ}` IS a modular form of weight `k`
on `Γ₀(N)` — a term of Mathlib's `ModularForm`. -/
noncomputable def etaQuotientModularForm {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (hke : Even k)
    (hbd : ∀ γ : SL(2, ℤ), IsBoundedAtImInfty ((etaQuotientH N r) ∣[k] γ)) :
    ModularForm (Gamma0 N) k where
  toFun := etaQuotientH N r
  slash_action_eq' := etaQuotientH_slash_of_le_four hN hN4 r hk h1 h2 hke
  holo' := mdiff_etaQuotientH N r
  bdd_at_cusps' hc := isBoundedAt_of_bdd_SL2Z r hbd hc

@[simp] theorem etaQuotientModularForm_apply {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp)
    {k : ℤ} (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r)
    (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r) (hke : Even k)
    (hbd : ∀ γ : SL(2, ℤ), IsBoundedAtImInfty ((etaQuotientH N r) ∣[k] γ)) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientModularForm hN hN4 r hk h1 h2 hke hbd z = etaQuotientH N r z := rfl

/-! #### F3.2-B2 — the LL-1 guards

GUARD 1 (agreement with Mathlib).  At `N = 1`, `r ≡ 24`, `k = 12` the eta quotient IS `Δ`, and
the statement produced by the B2 route is verbatim `etaQuotientH_level_one_24_transform`, which
was proved from Mathlib's own `discriminant_S_invariant`/`discriminant_T_invariant` and shares no
step with this block.  A drifted weight or a stray `denom` power would break it.

GUARD 2 (the parity hypothesis is load-bearing, and the previously-scoped statement is FALSE).
`N = 3`, `r₁ = -3`, `r₃ = 9`: both congruences hold, `Σ r_δ = 6 = 2·3`, `k = 3` is ODD, `N ≤ 4`
so `⟨-I, T, V⟩ = Γ₀(3)` is a theorem — and the trivial-multiplier conclusion FAILS at `γ = -I`. -/

/-- GUARD 1. -/
theorem ligozat_level_one_24_guard (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) (γ • z)
      = (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ (12 : ℤ)
        * etaQuotientH 1 (fun _ => (24 : ℤ)) z :=
  etaQuotientH_transform_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) _ sum_divisors_one_24
    ligozatCongr1_level_one_24 ligozatCongr2_level_one_24 ⟨6, by norm_num⟩ (mem_Gamma0_one γ) z

/-- The odd-weight exponent vector `r₁ = -3`, `r₃ = 9` at level `3`: classically
`η(z)⁻³ η(3z)⁹ ∈ M₃(Γ₀(3), χ₋₃)`. -/
def ligozatOddExp : EtaExp := fun δ => if δ = 1 then -3 else if δ = 3 then 9 else 0

theorem ligozatOddExp_sum : ∑ δ ∈ (3 : ℕ).divisors, ligozatOddExp δ = 2 * 3 := by decide

theorem ligozatOddExp_congr1 : LigozatCongr1 3 ligozatOddExp := by
  have h : ∑ δ ∈ (3 : ℕ).divisors, (δ : ℤ) * ligozatOddExp δ = 24 := by decide
  exact ⟨1, by rw [h]; ring⟩

theorem ligozatOddExp_congr2 : LigozatCongr2 3 ligozatOddExp := by
  have h : ∑ δ ∈ (3 : ℕ).divisors, ((3 / δ : ℕ) : ℤ) * ligozatOddExp δ = 0 := by decide
  exact ⟨0, by rw [h]; ring⟩

/-- The generation hypothesis of `multiplier_trivial_of_congr` is a THEOREM at `N = 3`, so the
counterexample below really does satisfy every hypothesis of the statement that used to be a
`sorry` here. -/
theorem ligozatOdd_hgen :
    ∀ γ ∈ Gamma0 3, γ ∈ Subgroup.closure ({ModularGroup.T, matV 3, -1} : Set SL(2, ℤ)) := by
  intro γ hγ
  rw [← gamma0Gens_eq_perm, closure_gamma0Gens_eq (by norm_num) (by norm_num)]
  exact hγ

/-- The multiplier of the odd example at `-I` is `-1`, not `1`. -/
theorem etaMultiplierVal_ligozatOddExp_neg_one :
    etaMultiplierVal 3 ligozatOddExp 3 (-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) = -1 := by
  rw [etaMultiplierVal_neg_one ligozatOddExp ligozatOddExp_sum]
  norm_num

/-- **GUARD 2, the honest negative.**  Every hypothesis of the previously-scoped
`multiplier_trivial_of_congr` — `0 < N`, `Σ r_δ = 2k`, Ligozat (i), Ligozat (ii), and generation
by `{T, V, -I}` — holds for `N = 3`, `r = ligozatOddExp`, `k = 3`, and the conclusion is FALSE.
`Even k` is therefore a correction of the statement, not a convenience. -/
theorem not_multiplier_trivial_odd :
    ¬ ∀ z : ℍ, etaQuotientH 3 ligozatOddExp ((-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) • z)
        = (denom ((-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ (3 : ℤ)
          * etaQuotientH 3 ligozatOddExp z := by
  intro h
  have hz := h UpperHalfPlane.I
  rw [show ((-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) • UpperHalfPlane.I) = UpperHalfPlane.I from by
        rw [ModularGroup.SL_neg_smul, one_smul], denom_neg_one,
    show ((-1 : ℂ) ^ (3 : ℤ)) = -1 by norm_num] at hz
  exact etaQuotientH_ne_zero 3 ligozatOddExp UpperHalfPlane.I (by linear_combination hz / 2)


/-! ## `F3.2-B3` — the multiplier IS Ligozat's Kronecker character (for `N ≤ 4`)

`F3.2-B2` pinned the eta multiplier `w : Γ₀(N) →* ℂˣ` by its three values `w(T) = 1`,
`w(V) = 1`, `w(-I) = (-1)^k`, using generation of `Γ₀(N)` by `{-I, T, V}` for `N ≤ 4`.  What it
did **not** do is exhibit `w` as the function of `d = γ₁₁` that the literature writes down.  This
block does that: for `N ≤ 4`, under Ligozat's two congruences,

  `w(γ) = ( (-1)^k ∏_{δ ∣ N} δ^{r_δ} / d )`,   `d = γ₁₁`,

the **Kronecker symbol**.  Both sides are homomorphisms `Γ₀(N) → ℂˣ`, so `F3.2-B2`'s uniqueness
does the group theory; what is new here is (a) a Kronecker symbol, and (b) the arithmetic that
identifies it, level by level, with a Dirichlet character mod `N`.

### Mathlib does not have the Kronecker symbol, and the Jacobi symbol is not it

Mathlib has `jacobiSym` — but `jacobiSym a b` is defined for `b : ℕ` as the product of
`legendreSym p a` over the prime factors of `b`, and at `p = 2` that is **not** the Kronecker
convention: `jacobiSym (-3) 2 = 1` while the Kronecker symbol `(-3/2) = χ₈(-3) = -1`.  Since
`Γ₀(3)` contains matrices with even `d` (for instance `!![-1,-1;3,2]`, whose `d` is `2`) and with
negative `d` (`-I`), the honest statement needs the genuine Kronecker symbol, extended over `2`
by `χ₈` and over `-1` by the sign.  `kroneckerPrime`/`kroneckerSym` below are exactly that, built
on Mathlib's own `legendreSym`, `ZMod.χ₈` and `Nat.primeFactorsList`:

* at an odd prime `p`, `kroneckerPrime a p` **is** `legendreSym p a` (definitionally);
* at `p = 2` it is `ZMod.χ₈ a`, the classical `(a/2)`;
* `kroneckerSym a d` is `(a/sign d) · ∏_{p ∣ |d|} (a/p)`, the standard multiplicative extension,
  with the standard convention `(a/0) = 1` if `a = ±1` and `0` otherwise.

### The one piece of reciprocity, proved elementarily rather than assumed

The only nontrivial evaluation needed is `(-3/p) = χ₋₃(p)` for odd primes `p ≠ 3`.  We do **not**
route this through quadratic reciprocity (Mathlib's `QuadraticReciprocity` is available in
principle but drags in the Gauss-sum/cyclotomic cone).  Instead `isSquare_neg_three_iff` proves,
by hand, that `-3` is a square mod `p` iff `p ≡ 1 (mod 3)`: `x² = -3` and `z² + z + 1 = 0` are
exchanged by `z = (x-1)/2`, and `z² + z + 1 = 0` says exactly that `z` has order `3` in
`(ℤ/p)ˣ`, which happens iff `3 ∣ p - 1`.  Both directions are elementary; the backward one uses
that `(ℤ/p)ˣ` is cyclic.

### Why the exponents appear as `|r_δ|`

Ligozat's numerator `(-1)^k ∏_δ δ^{r_δ}` is a **rational** number when some `r_δ < 0`, and a
Kronecker symbol takes an integer numerator.  `ligozatKroneckerNum` therefore uses `|r_δ|` and
`|k|`.  That is not a weakening: the symbol's values are `±1` on coprime arguments, so
`(δ/d)^{r_δ} = (δ/d)^{|r_δ|}` for every sign of `r_δ`, and this is **proved**, not asserted, as
`kroneckerSym_ligozat_eq_prod` / `multiplier_eq_ligozat_character_of_le_four`, which state the
result in Ligozat's own completely-multiplicative form

  `w(γ) = (-1/d)^k · ∏_{δ ∣ N} (δ/d)^{r_δ}`   with genuine **integer** exponents `k`, `r_δ`.

### Scope (LL-1)

`N ≤ 4` throughout, inherited from `F3.2-B1`: at `N ≥ 5` the three matrices do not generate and
the Dedekind-sum obstruction (`F3.2-OBSTRUCTED`) is untouched.  Nothing in this block proves
anything about `N ≥ 5`; in particular the level-by-level arithmetic below (`k` is forced even at
`N ∈ {1,2}`, the exponents are forced even at `N ∈ {2,4}`, and `r₃ ≡ -k (mod 12)` at `N = 3`) is
a finite case analysis, not a general theorem. -/

section
variable {p : ℕ} [Fact p.Prime]

/-- `(q : ZMod p) ≠ 0` for distinct primes `p ≠ q`. -/
theorem natCast_ne_zero_of_ne {q : ℕ} (hq : q.Prime) (h : p ≠ q) : ((q : ℕ) : ZMod p) ≠ 0 := by
  rw [Ne, ZMod.natCast_eq_zero_iff]
  exact fun hd => h ((Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq (Fact.out : p.Prime) hq).mp hd)

/-- **Forward half of the `-3` criterion.**  If `-3` is a square mod `p` (`p ≠ 2, 3`) then
`p ≡ 1 (mod 3)`.  With `x² = -3` the element `z = (x-1)/2` satisfies `z² + z + 1 = 0`, hence
`z³ = 1` and `z ≠ 1`, so `z` has order `3` and `3 ∣ p - 1` by Fermat. -/
theorem cube_root_of_isSquare_neg_three (hp2 : p ≠ 2) (hp3 : p ≠ 3)
    (h : IsSquare (-3 : ZMod p)) : p % 3 = 1 := by
  have hpp := (Fact.out : p.Prime)
  have h2 : (2 : ZMod p) ≠ 0 := by simpa using natCast_ne_zero_of_ne (p := p) Nat.prime_two hp2
  have h3 : (3 : ZMod p) ≠ 0 := by simpa using natCast_ne_zero_of_ne (p := p) Nat.prime_three hp3
  obtain ⟨x, hx⟩ := h
  set z : ZMod p := (x - 1) / 2 with hzdef
  have hz2 : 2 * z = x - 1 := by
    rw [hzdef]; field_simp
  have h4 : (4 : ZMod p) * (z ^ 2 + z + 1) = 0 := by
    linear_combination (2 * z + x + 1) * hz2 - hx
  have h4ne : (4 : ZMod p) ≠ 0 := by
    rw [show (4 : ZMod p) = 2 * 2 by norm_num]; exact mul_ne_zero h2 h2
  have key : z ^ 2 + z + 1 = 0 := by
    rcases mul_eq_zero.mp h4 with h | h
    · exact absurd h h4ne
    · exact h
  have hz1 : z ≠ 1 := by
    intro hc; rw [hc] at key; exact h3 (by linear_combination key)
  have hcube : z ^ 3 = 1 := by linear_combination (z - 1) * key
  have hzne : z ≠ 0 := by
    intro hc; rw [hc] at key; simp at key
  have hord : orderOf z = 3 := by
    rcases (Nat.prime_three).eq_one_or_self_of_dvd _ (orderOf_dvd_of_pow_eq_one hcube) with h | h
    · exact absurd (orderOf_eq_one_iff.mp h) hz1
    · exact h
  have hdvd : (3 : ℕ) ∣ p - 1 := by
    rw [← hord]
    exact orderOf_dvd_of_pow_eq_one (ZMod.pow_card_sub_one_eq_one hzne)
  have h2le := hpp.two_le
  omega


/-- **Backward half.**  If `3 ∣ p - 1` then `(ℤ/p)ˣ`, being cyclic of order `p-1`, has an
element `z` of order `3`; then `z² + z + 1 = 0` and `(2z+1)² = -3`. -/
theorem isSquare_neg_three_of_mod (hp3 : p % 3 = 1) : IsSquare (-3 : ZMod p) := by
  have hpp := (Fact.out : p.Prime)
  have hcard : Nat.card (ZMod p)ˣ = p - 1 := by
    rw [Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, ZMod.card_units_eq_totient, Nat.totient_prime hpp]
  have hdvd : (3 : ℕ) ∣ p - 1 := by have := hpp.two_le; omega
  obtain ⟨g, hg⟩ := isCyclic_iff_exists_orderOf_eq_natCard.mp (inferInstance : IsCyclic (ZMod p)ˣ)
  rw [hcard] at hg
  have hne : p - 1 ≠ 0 := by have := hpp.two_le; omega
  set u : (ZMod p)ˣ := g ^ ((p - 1) / 3) with hu
  have hordu : orderOf u = 3 := by
    rw [hu, orderOf_pow, hg, Nat.gcd_eq_right (Nat.div_dvd_of_dvd hdvd), Nat.div_div_self hdvd hne]
  have hu3 : u ^ 3 = 1 := by rw [← hordu]; exact pow_orderOf_eq_one u
  have hune : u ≠ 1 := by
    intro hc; rw [hc] at hordu; simp at hordu
  set z : ZMod p := (u : ZMod p) with hz
  have hz3 : z ^ 3 = 1 := by
    rw [hz, ← Units.val_pow_eq_pow_val, hu3, Units.val_one]
  have hz1 : z ≠ 1 := by
    intro hc; exact hune (Units.ext hc)
  have key : z ^ 2 + z + 1 = 0 := by
    have hfac : (z - 1) * (z ^ 2 + z + 1) = 0 := by linear_combination hz3
    rcases mul_eq_zero.mp hfac with h | h
    · exact absurd (by linear_combination h) hz1
    · exact h
  exact ⟨2 * z + 1, by linear_combination (-4 : ZMod p) * key⟩


/-- **`-3` is a square mod `p` iff `p ≡ 1 (mod 3)`** (`p` prime, `p ≠ 2, 3`).  This is the only
reciprocity input of the whole block, and it is proved from scratch — no Gauss sums, no
quadratic reciprocity. -/
theorem isSquare_neg_three_iff (hp2 : p ≠ 2) (hp3 : p ≠ 3) :
    IsSquare (-3 : ZMod p) ↔ p % 3 = 1 :=
  ⟨cube_root_of_isSquare_neg_three hp2 hp3, isSquare_neg_three_of_mod⟩

end

/-- The Kronecker symbol at a prime: `legendreSym p a` for odd `p`, and Mathlib's `ZMod.χ₈` at
`p = 2` (which is the classical `(a/2)`, and is **not** what `jacobiSym` computes there). -/
noncomputable def kroneckerPrime (a : ℤ) (p : ℕ) : ℤ :=
  if p = 2 then ZMod.χ₈ (a : ZMod 8)
  else if hp : p.Prime then @legendreSym p ⟨hp⟩ a else 0

/-- **The Kronecker symbol `(a/d)`,** `a d : ℤ`.  Standard definition: the sign factor `(a / -1)`,
times `(a/p)` over the prime factorisation of `|d|`.  At `d = 0` the classical convention
`(a/0) = 1` for `a = ±1` and `0` otherwise is used. -/
noncomputable def kroneckerSym (a d : ℤ) : ℤ :=
  if d = 0 then (if a = 1 ∨ a = -1 then 1 else 0)
  else (if a < 0 ∧ d < 0 then -1 else 1) *
    (d.natAbs.primeFactorsList.map (kroneckerPrime a)).prod

theorem kroneckerPrime_mul (a b : ℤ) (p : ℕ) :
    kroneckerPrime (a * b) p = kroneckerPrime a p * kroneckerPrime b p := by
  unfold kroneckerPrime
  split_ifs with h2 hp
  · push_cast
    exact map_mul _ _ _
  · haveI : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
    exact legendreSym.mul (p := p) a b
  · ring

theorem kroneckerPrime_one {p : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) : kroneckerPrime 1 p = 1 := by
  unfold kroneckerPrime
  split_ifs with h2
  · push_cast; exact map_one _
  · haveI : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
    exact legendreSym.at_one (p := p)

theorem kroneckerPrime_trichotomy (a : ℤ) (p : ℕ) :
    kroneckerPrime a p = 0 ∨ kroneckerPrime a p = 1 ∨ kroneckerPrime a p = -1 := by
  unfold kroneckerPrime
  split_ifs with h2 hp
  · exact ZMod.isQuadratic_χ₈ _
  · haveI : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
    exact quadraticChar_isQuadratic (ZMod p) _
  · exact Or.inl rfl

theorem kroneckerPrime_ne_zero {a : ℤ} {p : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) (hpa : ¬ ((p : ℤ) ∣ a)) :
    kroneckerPrime a p ≠ 0 := by
  unfold kroneckerPrime
  split_ifs with h2
  · subst h2
    rw [ZMod.χ₈_int_eq_if_mod_eight]
    have hodd : a % 2 ≠ 0 := by
      intro hc
      exact hpa (by exact_mod_cast Int.dvd_of_emod_eq_zero hc)
    rw [if_neg hodd]
    split_ifs <;> norm_num
  · haveI : Fact p.Prime := ⟨hp⟩
    rw [Ne, legendreSym.eq_zero_iff, ZMod.intCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd]
    exact hpa

theorem kroneckerPrime_sq {a : ℤ} {p : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) (hpa : ¬ ((p : ℤ) ∣ a)) :
    kroneckerPrime (a ^ 2) p = 1 := by
  have h := kroneckerPrime_ne_zero hp hpa
  have : kroneckerPrime (a ^ 2) p = kroneckerPrime a p * kroneckerPrime a p := by
    rw [sq, kroneckerPrime_mul]
  rw [this]
  rcases kroneckerPrime_trichotomy a p with h0 | h1 | h1
  · exact absurd h0 h
  · rw [h1]; norm_num
  · rw [h1]; norm_num


theorem kroneckerSym_one_left (d : ℤ) : kroneckerSym 1 d = 1 := by
  by_cases hd : d = 0
  · simp [kroneckerSym, hd]
  · rw [kroneckerSym, if_neg hd,
      if_neg (show ¬((1 : ℤ) < 0 ∧ d < 0) from fun h => absurd h.1 (by norm_num)), one_mul,
      List.prod_eq_one]
    intro x hx
    obtain ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩ := List.mem_map.mp hx
    exact kroneckerPrime_one (Nat.prime_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq)

/-- The symbol is completely multiplicative in the numerator. -/
theorem kroneckerSym_mul_left {a b : ℤ} (ha : a ≠ 0) (hb : b ≠ 0) (d : ℤ) :
    kroneckerSym (a * b) d = kroneckerSym a d * kroneckerSym b d := by
  by_cases hd : d = 0
  · simp only [kroneckerSym, if_pos hd]
    by_cases ha1 : a = 1 ∨ a = -1
    · by_cases hb1 : b = 1 ∨ b = -1
      · rcases ha1 with rfl | rfl <;> rcases hb1 with rfl | rfl <;> norm_num
      · rw [if_neg hb1, if_pos ha1, one_mul, if_neg]
        intro hc
        exact hb1 (Int.isUnit_iff.mp (isUnit_of_mul_isUnit_right (Int.isUnit_iff.mpr hc)))
    · rw [if_neg ha1, zero_mul, if_neg]
      intro hc
      exact ha1 (Int.isUnit_iff.mp (isUnit_of_mul_isUnit_left (Int.isUnit_iff.mpr hc)))
  · simp only [kroneckerSym, if_neg hd]
    have hprod : (d.natAbs.primeFactorsList.map (kroneckerPrime (a * b))).prod
        = (d.natAbs.primeFactorsList.map (kroneckerPrime a)).prod
          * (d.natAbs.primeFactorsList.map (kroneckerPrime b)).prod := by
      rw [← List.prod_map_mul]
      congr 1
      exact List.map_congr_left (fun q _ => kroneckerPrime_mul a b q)
    have hsign : (if a * b < 0 ∧ d < 0 then (-1 : ℤ) else 1)
        = (if a < 0 ∧ d < 0 then (-1 : ℤ) else 1) * (if b < 0 ∧ d < 0 then (-1 : ℤ) else 1) := by
      by_cases hdn : d < 0
      · simp only [hdn, and_true]
        rcases lt_or_gt_of_ne ha with hA | hA <;> rcases lt_or_gt_of_ne hb with hB | hB
        · rw [if_neg (not_lt.mpr (mul_pos_of_neg_of_neg hA hB).le), if_pos hA, if_pos hB]
          norm_num
        · rw [if_pos (mul_neg_of_neg_of_pos hA hB), if_pos hA, if_neg (not_lt.mpr hB.le)]
          norm_num
        · rw [if_pos (mul_neg_of_pos_of_neg hA hB), if_neg (not_lt.mpr hA.le), if_pos hB]
          norm_num
        · rw [if_neg (not_lt.mpr (mul_pos hA hB).le), if_neg (not_lt.mpr hA.le),
            if_neg (not_lt.mpr hB.le)]
          norm_num
      · simp [hdn]
    rw [hprod, hsign]; ring

/-- A square numerator coprime to `d` is invisible: `(s²/d) = 1`.  This is what lets Ligozat's
numerator be reduced, level by level, to one of `1`, `-1`, `-3`. -/
theorem kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd {s d : ℤ} (h : Int.gcd s d = 1) : kroneckerSym (s ^ 2) d = 1 := by
  by_cases hd : d = 0
  · subst hd
    have hs : s.natAbs = 1 := by simpa [Int.gcd] using h
    have : s = 1 ∨ s = -1 := by
      rcases Int.natAbs_eq s with hh | hh
      · left; rw [hh, hs]; norm_num
      · right; rw [hh, hs]; norm_num
    simp only [kroneckerSym]
    rcases this with rfl | rfl <;> norm_num
  · rw [kroneckerSym, if_neg hd,
      if_neg (show ¬(s ^ 2 < 0 ∧ d < 0) from
        fun hc => absurd hc.1 (not_lt.mpr (sq_nonneg s))),
      one_mul, List.prod_eq_one]
    intro x hx
    obtain ⟨q, hq, rfl⟩ := List.mem_map.mp hx
    have hqp := Nat.prime_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
    have hqd : q ∣ d.natAbs := Nat.dvd_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
    refine kroneckerPrime_sq hqp (fun hc => ?_)
    have hqs : q ∣ s.natAbs := Int.natCast_dvd_natCast.mp (Int.dvd_natAbs.mpr hc)
    have hone : q ∣ Int.gcd s d := Nat.dvd_gcd hqs hqd
    rw [h, Nat.dvd_one] at hone
    exact hqp.one_lt.ne' hone


theorem prod_map_mulChar {n : ℕ} (χ : MulChar (ZMod n) ℤ) (L : List ℕ) :
    (L.map (fun q : ℕ => χ ((q : ℕ) : ZMod n))).prod = χ ((L.prod : ℕ) : ZMod n) := by
  induction L with
  | nil => simp
  | cons q L ih =>
      rw [List.map_cons, List.prod_cons, ih, ← map_mul, List.prod_cons, Nat.cast_mul]

/-- **The identification engine.**  If `a < 0` and `(a/·)` agrees at every prime divisor of `d`
with a `MulChar` `χ` on `ZMod n` sending `-1` to `-1`, then `(a/d) = χ(d)` — the sign convention
of the Kronecker symbol is exactly what makes the negative `d` case work. -/
theorem kroneckerSym_eq_mulChar {n : ℕ} (χ : MulChar (ZMod n) ℤ) (hneg : χ (-1 : ZMod n) = -1)
    {a d : ℤ} (ha : a < 0) (hd : d ≠ 0)
    (hp : ∀ q ∈ d.natAbs.primeFactorsList, kroneckerPrime a q = χ ((q : ℕ) : ZMod n)) :
    kroneckerSym a d = χ ((d : ℤ) : ZMod n) := by
  have hdn : d.natAbs ≠ 0 := Int.natAbs_ne_zero.mpr hd
  rw [kroneckerSym, if_neg hd, List.map_congr_left hp, prod_map_mulChar,
    Nat.prod_primeFactorsList hdn]
  by_cases hdneg : d < 0
  · rw [if_pos ⟨ha, hdneg⟩,
      show ((d.natAbs : ℕ) : ZMod n) = (-1 : ZMod n) * ((d : ℤ) : ZMod n) by
        rw [← Int.cast_natCast, Int.ofNat_natAbs_of_nonpos hdneg.le]
        push_cast; ring,
      map_mul, hneg]
    ring
  · rw [if_neg (fun hc => hdneg hc.2),
      show ((d.natAbs : ℕ) : ZMod n) = ((d : ℤ) : ZMod n) by
        rw [← Int.cast_natCast, Int.natAbs_of_nonneg (not_lt.mp hdneg)]]
    ring


/-- `(-3/q) = χ₋₃(q)` for odd primes `q ≠ 3`, where `χ₋₃` is the quadratic character mod `3`. -/
theorem legendreSym_neg_three {q : ℕ} [Fact q.Prime] (hq2 : q ≠ 2) (hq3 : q ≠ 3) :
    legendreSym q (-3) = quadraticChar (ZMod 3) ((q : ℕ) : ZMod 3) := by
  have hqp := (Fact.out : q.Prime)
  have h3 : (3 : ZMod q) ≠ 0 := by
    simpa using natCast_ne_zero_of_ne (p := q) Nat.prime_three hq3
  have h3ne : ((-3 : ℤ) : ZMod q) ≠ 0 := by
    push_cast
    exact neg_ne_zero.mpr h3
  have hcast3 : ((-3 : ℤ) : ZMod q) = (-3 : ZMod q) := by push_cast; ring
  have hmod : q % 3 = 1 ∨ q % 3 = 2 := by
    have h3q : ¬ (3 ∣ q) := fun h =>
      hq3 ((Nat.prime_dvd_prime_iff_eq Nat.prime_three hqp).mp h).symm
    omega
  rcases hmod with h | h
  · have hc : ((q : ℕ) : ZMod 3) = 1 := by
      rw [← ZMod.natCast_mod q 3, h]; norm_num
    rw [hc, MulChar.map_one]
    refine (legendreSym.eq_one_iff (p := q) h3ne).mpr ?_
    rw [hcast3]
    exact isSquare_neg_three_of_mod h
  · have hc : ((q : ℕ) : ZMod 3) = 2 := by
      rw [← ZMod.natCast_mod q 3, h]; norm_num
    rw [hc, show quadraticChar (ZMod 3) 2 = -1 from by decide]
    refine (legendreSym.eq_neg_one_iff (p := q)).mpr ?_
    rw [hcast3]
    intro hs
    rw [(isSquare_neg_three_iff hq2 hq3).mp hs] at h
    exact absurd h (by norm_num)

/-- `(-1/d) = χ₄(d)` for odd `d` (any sign). -/
theorem kroneckerSym_neg_one {d : ℤ} (hd : Odd d) :
    kroneckerSym (-1) d = ZMod.χ₄ ((d : ℤ) : ZMod 4) := by
  have hd0 : d ≠ 0 := by
    rintro rfl; simp at hd
  refine kroneckerSym_eq_mulChar ZMod.χ₄ (by decide) (by norm_num) hd0 ?_
  intro q hq
  have hqp := Nat.prime_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
  have hqd : q ∣ d.natAbs := Nat.dvd_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
  have hq2 : q ≠ 2 := by
    rintro rfl
    exact (Nat.not_even_iff_odd.mpr (Int.natAbs_odd.mpr hd)) (even_iff_two_dvd.mpr hqd)
  haveI : Fact q.Prime := ⟨hqp⟩
  rw [kroneckerPrime, if_neg hq2, dif_pos hqp]
  exact legendreSym.at_neg_one hq2

/-- `(-3/d) = χ₋₃(d)` for `d` coprime to `3` — **any parity and any sign of `d`**, which is what
`Γ₀(3)` forces on us and what `jacobiSym` alone could not deliver. -/
theorem kroneckerSym_neg_three {d : ℤ} (hd : ¬ ((3 : ℤ) ∣ d)) :
    kroneckerSym (-3) d = quadraticChar (ZMod 3) ((d : ℤ) : ZMod 3) := by
  have hd0 : d ≠ 0 := by rintro rfl; exact hd ⟨0, by ring⟩
  refine kroneckerSym_eq_mulChar (quadraticChar (ZMod 3)) (by decide) (by norm_num) hd0 ?_
  intro q hq
  have hqp := Nat.prime_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
  have hqd : q ∣ d.natAbs := Nat.dvd_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
  have hq3 : q ≠ 3 := by
    rintro rfl
    exact hd (Int.dvd_natAbs.mp (by exact_mod_cast hqd))
  by_cases hq2 : q = 2
  · subst hq2
    rw [kroneckerPrime, if_pos rfl]
    decide
  · haveI : Fact q.Prime := ⟨hqp⟩
    rw [kroneckerPrime, if_neg hq2, dif_pos hqp]
    exact legendreSym_neg_three hq2 hq3



/-- A Dirichlet character mod `N` pulled back to a character of `Γ₀(N)` along
`γ ↦ γ₁₁ (mod N)`.  Multiplicativity is free: `Gamma0Map` is Mathlib's own homomorphism (it is a
homomorphism precisely because `N ∣ c`), and `MulChar.toUnitHom` lands in `ℂˣ`. -/
noncomputable def charGamma0 (N : ℕ) (χ : MulChar (ZMod N) ℤ) : Gamma0 N →* ℂˣ :=
  (χ.ringHomComp (Int.castRingHom ℂ)).toUnitHom.comp (Gamma0Map N).toHomUnits

theorem charGamma0_apply (N : ℕ) (χ : MulChar (ZMod N) ℤ) (γ : Gamma0 N) :
    ((charGamma0 N χ γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ) = ((χ (((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1 : ℤ) : ZMod N) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  simp [charGamma0, MonoidHom.coe_toHomUnits, Gamma0Map, MulChar.ringHomComp_apply]

theorem coe_T_one_one : (ModularGroup.T : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1 = 1 := by
  simp [ModularGroup.coe_T]

theorem matV_one_one (N : ℕ) : (matV N : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1 = 1 := by
  simp [matV]

theorem neg_one_one_one : ((-1 : SL(2, ℤ)) : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1 = -1 := by
  simp

/-- **The bridge from `F3.2-B2`.**  For `N ≤ 4` and Ligozat's congruences, ANY Dirichlet
character `χ` mod `N` with `χ(-1) = (-1)^k` computes the eta multiplier: `w(γ) = χ(γ₁₁)`.  The
values at `T` and `V` are automatic because both have `γ₁₁ = 1`; the only input is `χ(-1)`. -/
theorem multiplier_eq_intChar_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (χ : MulChar (ZMod N) ℤ)
    (hneg : ((χ (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod N)) : ℤ) : ℂ) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k) (γ : Gamma0 N) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((χ (((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1 : ℤ) : ZMod N) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  have hT : charGamma0 N χ ⟨ModularGroup.T, T_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1 := by
    apply Units.ext
    rw [charGamma0_apply]
    simp [coe_T_one_one]
  have hV : charGamma0 N χ ⟨matV N, matV_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = 1 := by
    apply Units.ext
    rw [charGamma0_apply]
    simp [matV_one_one]
  have hm : charGamma0 N χ ⟨-1, neg_one_mem_Gamma0 N⟩ = (-1 : ℂˣ) ^ k := by
    apply Units.ext
    rw [charGamma0_apply]
    simpa [neg_one_one_one] using hneg
  have hEq := etaMultiplierHom_eq_of_le_four hN hN4 r hk h1 h2 (charGamma0 N χ) hT hV hm
  rw [← hEq, charGamma0_apply]


/-- Ligozat's numerator `(-1)^k ∏_{δ ∣ N} δ^{r_δ}`, with `|k|` and `|r_δ|` in the exponents so
that it is an **integer** (see the section docstring: the symbol cannot tell the difference, and
`kroneckerSym_ligozat_eq_prod` proves it). -/
def ligozatKroneckerNum (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (k : ℤ) : ℤ :=
  (-1) ^ k.natAbs * ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, (δ : ℤ) ^ (r δ).natAbs

/-- For `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` and `1 < m ∣ N`, `m ∤ γ₁₁`: from `ad - bc = 1` and `m ∣ N ∣ c`. -/
theorem gamma0_not_dvd {N : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) {m : ℕ}
    (hmN : (m : ℤ) ∣ (N : ℤ)) (hm1 : 1 < m) : ¬ ((m : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 1) := by
  intro hdvd
  have hc : (m : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0 :=
    hmN.trans ((ZMod.intCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd _ _).mp (Gamma0_mem.mp hγ))
  have hdet := sl_det_entries γ
  have : (m : ℤ) ∣ 1 := by
    rw [← hdet]
    exact dvd_sub (Dvd.dvd.mul_left hdvd _) (Dvd.dvd.mul_left hc _)
  have := Int.le_of_dvd one_pos this
  omega

theorem gcd_pow_of_not_dvd {p : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) {d : ℤ} (h : ¬ ((p : ℤ) ∣ d)) (m : ℕ) :
    Int.gcd ((p : ℤ) ^ m) d = 1 := by
  have hnd : ¬ (p ∣ d.natAbs) := fun hc => h (Int.dvd_natAbs.mp (by exact_mod_cast hc))
  have hcop : Nat.Coprime p d.natAbs := (Nat.Prime.coprime_iff_not_dvd hp).mpr hnd
  have : ((p : ℤ) ^ m).natAbs = p ^ m := by
    simp [Int.natAbs_pow]
  rw [Int.gcd, this]
  exact Nat.Coprime.pow_left _ hcop

theorem mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit {R R' : Type*} [CommMonoid R] [CommMonoidWithZero R']
    {x : R} (hx : IsUnit x) : (1 : MulChar R R') x = 1 := by
  obtain ⟨u, rfl⟩ := hx
  exact MulChar.one_apply_coe u

theorem isUnit_gamma0_entry {N : ℕ} (γ : Gamma0 N) :
    IsUnit ((((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1 : ℤ) : ZMod N)) :=
  (Group.isUnit γ).map (Gamma0Map N)


/-- `N = 1`.  Ligozat's congruence forces `12 ∣ k`, so `k` is even, the numerator is `1` and the
character is trivial — consistent with `Γ₀(1) = SL(2,ℤ)` carrying no nebentypus. -/
theorem multiplier_eq_kronecker_one (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (1 : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 1 r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 1 r)
    (γ : Gamma0 1) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (ligozatKroneckerNum 1 r k) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  have hdiv : (1 : ℕ).divisors = ({1} : Finset ℕ) := by decide
  have hk' : r 1 = 2 * k := by rw [hdiv, Finset.sum_singleton] at hk; exact hk
  have h1' : (24 : ℤ) ∣ r 1 := by
    have := h1
    rw [LigozatCongr1, hdiv, Finset.sum_singleton] at this
    simpa using this
  have hke : Even k := by rw [Int.even_iff]; omega
  have hD : ligozatKroneckerNum 1 r k = 1 := by
    rw [ligozatKroneckerNum, hdiv, Finset.prod_singleton,
      (Int.natAbs_even.mpr hke).neg_one_pow]
    norm_num
  have hneg : (((1 : MulChar (ZMod 1) ℤ) (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 1)) : ℤ) : ℂ) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k := by
    rw [mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (by exact (isUnit_one).neg), hke.neg_one_zpow]
    norm_num
  rw [multiplier_eq_intChar_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) r hk h1 h2
      (1 : MulChar (ZMod 1) ℤ) hneg γ,
    mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (isUnit_gamma0_entry γ), hD, kroneckerSym_one_left]

/-- `N = 2`.  The two congruences force `4 ∣ k` (so `k` even) and `r₂` even, so the numerator is
a perfect square coprime to the odd `d`, and the character is trivial. -/
theorem multiplier_eq_kronecker_two (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (2 : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 2 r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 2 r)
    (γ : Gamma0 2) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (ligozatKroneckerNum 2 r k) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  have hdiv : (2 : ℕ).divisors = ({1, 2} : Finset ℕ) := by decide
  have hk' : r 1 + r 2 = 2 * k := by
    rw [hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_singleton] at hk; exact hk
  have h1' : (24 : ℤ) ∣ r 1 + 2 * r 2 := by
    have := h1
    rw [LigozatCongr1, hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_singleton] at this
    simpa using this
  have h2' : (24 : ℤ) ∣ 2 * r 1 + r 2 := by
    have := h2
    rw [LigozatCongr2, hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_singleton] at this
    norm_num at this
    exact this
  have hke : Even k := by rw [Int.even_iff]; omega
  have hr2 : Even (r 2) := by rw [Int.even_iff]; omega
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := Int.natAbs_even.mpr hr2
  have hD : ligozatKroneckerNum 2 r k = ((2 : ℤ) ^ a) ^ 2 := by
    rw [ligozatKroneckerNum, hdiv, Finset.prod_insert (by decide), Finset.prod_singleton,
      (Int.natAbs_even.mpr hke).neg_one_pow, ha]
    push_cast
    rw [pow_add]
    ring
  have hdodd : ¬ ((2 : ℤ) ∣ (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) :=
    gamma0_not_dvd γ.2 (by norm_num) (by norm_num)
  have hneg : (((1 : MulChar (ZMod 2) ℤ) (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 2)) : ℤ) : ℂ) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k := by
    rw [mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (by exact (isUnit_one).neg), hke.neg_one_zpow]
    norm_num
  have hgcd : Int.gcd ((2 : ℤ) ^ a) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) = 1 := by
    have h := gcd_pow_of_not_dvd Nat.prime_two (d := ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1))
      (by exact_mod_cast hdodd) a
    simpa using h
  rw [multiplier_eq_intChar_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) r hk h1 h2
      (1 : MulChar (ZMod 2) ℤ) hneg γ,
    mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (isUnit_gamma0_entry γ), hD, kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd]


/-- `N = 3`.  Here `k` may be odd.  The congruence gives `12 ∣ k + r₃`, so `r₃ ≡ k (mod 2)`: for
even `k` the numerator is `3^{even}`, a square, and the character is trivial; for odd `k` it is
`-3·(square)` and the character is `(-3/·)`, the quadratic character mod `3`.  This is the
nebentypus of `η(z)⁻³η(3z)⁹`. -/
theorem multiplier_eq_kronecker_three (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (3 : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 3 r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 3 r)
    (γ : Gamma0 3) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (ligozatKroneckerNum 3 r k) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  have hdiv : (3 : ℕ).divisors = ({1, 3} : Finset ℕ) := by decide
  have hk' : r 1 + r 3 = 2 * k := by
    rw [hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_singleton] at hk; exact hk
  have h1' : (24 : ℤ) ∣ r 1 + 3 * r 3 := by
    have := h1
    rw [LigozatCongr1, hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_singleton] at this
    simpa using this
  have hd3 : ¬ ((3 : ℤ) ∣ (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) :=
    gamma0_not_dvd γ.2 (by norm_num) (by norm_num)
  by_cases hke : Even k
  · have hr3 : Even (r 3) := by
      rw [Int.even_iff] at hke ⊢; omega
    obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := Int.natAbs_even.mpr hr3
    have hD : ligozatKroneckerNum 3 r k = ((3 : ℤ) ^ a) ^ 2 := by
      rw [ligozatKroneckerNum, hdiv, Finset.prod_insert (by decide), Finset.prod_singleton,
        (Int.natAbs_even.mpr hke).neg_one_pow, ha]
      push_cast
      ring
    have hgcd : Int.gcd ((3 : ℤ) ^ a) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) = 1 := by
      have h := gcd_pow_of_not_dvd Nat.prime_three (d := ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1))
        (by exact_mod_cast hd3) a
      simpa using h
    have hneg : (((1 : MulChar (ZMod 3) ℤ) (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 3)) : ℤ) : ℂ) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k := by
      rw [mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (by exact (isUnit_one).neg), hke.neg_one_zpow]
      norm_num
    rw [multiplier_eq_intChar_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) r hk h1 h2
        (1 : MulChar (ZMod 3) ℤ) hneg γ,
      mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (isUnit_gamma0_entry γ), hD, kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd]
  · have hko : Odd k := Int.not_even_iff_odd.mp hke
    have hr3 : Odd (r 3) := by
      rw [Int.odd_iff] at hko ⊢; omega
    obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := Int.natAbs_odd.mpr hr3
    have hD : ligozatKroneckerNum 3 r k = (-3) * ((3 : ℤ) ^ a) ^ 2 := by
      rw [ligozatKroneckerNum, hdiv, Finset.prod_insert (by decide), Finset.prod_singleton,
        (Int.natAbs_odd.mpr hko).neg_one_pow, ha]
      push_cast
      ring
    have hgcd : Int.gcd ((3 : ℤ) ^ a) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) = 1 := by
      have h := gcd_pow_of_not_dvd Nat.prime_three (d := ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1))
        (by exact_mod_cast hd3) a
      simpa using h
    have hneg : ((quadraticChar (ZMod 3) (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 3)) : ℤ) : ℂ) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k := by
      rw [hko.neg_one_zpow, show (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 3)) = (-1 : ZMod 3) by push_cast; ring,
        show quadraticChar (ZMod 3) (-1 : ZMod 3) = -1 from by decide]
      norm_num
    rw [multiplier_eq_intChar_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) r hk h1 h2
        (quadraticChar (ZMod 3)) hneg γ,
      hD, kroneckerSym_mul_left (by norm_num) (by positivity),
      kroneckerSym_neg_three hd3, kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd]
    norm_num

/-- `N = 4`.  Both congruences force every `r_δ` even, so the numerator is `±(square)` with `d`
odd: trivial character for even `k`, and `(-1/·) = χ₄` for odd `k`. -/
theorem multiplier_eq_kronecker_four (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ (4 : ℕ).divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 4 r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 4 r)
    (γ : Gamma0 4) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (ligozatKroneckerNum 4 r k) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  have hdiv : (4 : ℕ).divisors = ({1, 2, 4} : Finset ℕ) := by decide
  have hk' : r 1 + (r 2 + r 4) = 2 * k := by
    rw [hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_insert (by decide),
      Finset.sum_singleton] at hk
    exact hk
  have h1' : (24 : ℤ) ∣ r 1 + (2 * r 2 + 4 * r 4) := by
    have := h1
    rw [LigozatCongr1, hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_insert (by decide),
      Finset.sum_singleton] at this
    simpa using this
  have h2' : (24 : ℤ) ∣ 4 * r 1 + (2 * r 2 + r 4) := by
    have := h2
    rw [LigozatCongr2, hdiv, Finset.sum_insert (by decide), Finset.sum_insert (by decide),
      Finset.sum_singleton] at this
    norm_num at this
    exact this
  have hr2 : Even (r 2) := by rw [Int.even_iff]; omega
  have hr4 : Even (r 4) := by rw [Int.even_iff]; omega
  obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := Int.natAbs_even.mpr hr2
  obtain ⟨b, hb⟩ := Int.natAbs_even.mpr hr4
  have hdodd : ¬ ((2 : ℤ) ∣ (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) :=
    gamma0_not_dvd γ.2 (by norm_num) (by norm_num)
  have hgcd : Int.gcd ((2 : ℤ) ^ (a + 2 * b)) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) = 1 := by
    have h := gcd_pow_of_not_dvd Nat.prime_two (d := ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1))
      (by exact_mod_cast hdodd) (a + 2 * b)
    simpa using h
  have hprod : ∏ δ ∈ (4 : ℕ).divisors, (δ : ℤ) ^ (r δ).natAbs = ((2 : ℤ) ^ (a + 2 * b)) ^ 2 := by
    rw [hdiv, Finset.prod_insert (by decide), Finset.prod_insert (by decide),
      Finset.prod_singleton, ha, hb]
    push_cast
    rw [show (4 : ℤ) = 2 ^ 2 by norm_num, ← pow_mul]
    ring
  by_cases hke : Even k
  · have hD : ligozatKroneckerNum 4 r k = ((2 : ℤ) ^ (a + 2 * b)) ^ 2 := by
      rw [ligozatKroneckerNum, hprod, (Int.natAbs_even.mpr hke).neg_one_pow, one_mul]
    have hneg : (((1 : MulChar (ZMod 4) ℤ) (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 4)) : ℤ) : ℂ) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k := by
      rw [mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (by exact (isUnit_one).neg), hke.neg_one_zpow]
      norm_num
    rw [multiplier_eq_intChar_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) r hk h1 h2
        (1 : MulChar (ZMod 4) ℤ) hneg γ,
      mulChar_one_apply_of_isUnit (isUnit_gamma0_entry γ), hD, kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd]
  · have hko : Odd k := Int.not_even_iff_odd.mp hke
    have hD : ligozatKroneckerNum 4 r k = (-1) * ((2 : ℤ) ^ (a + 2 * b)) ^ 2 := by
      rw [ligozatKroneckerNum, hprod, (Int.natAbs_odd.mpr hko).neg_one_pow]
    have hodd : Odd ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) := Int.odd_iff.mpr (by omega)
    have hneg : ((ZMod.χ₄ (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 4)) : ℤ) : ℂ) = (-1 : ℂ) ^ k := by
      rw [hko.neg_one_zpow, show (((-1 : ℤ) : ZMod 4)) = (-1 : ZMod 4) by push_cast; ring,
        show ZMod.χ₄ (-1 : ZMod 4) = -1 from by decide]
      norm_num
    rw [multiplier_eq_intChar_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) r hk h1 h2
        ZMod.χ₄ hneg γ,
      hD, kroneckerSym_mul_left (by norm_num) (by positivity),
      kroneckerSym_neg_one hodd, kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd]
    norm_num


/-- **`F3.2-B3`, THE HEADLINE.**  For `0 < N ≤ 4`, `Σ_δ r_δ = 2k`, and Ligozat's congruences
`(i)` and `(ii)`, the eta multiplier of `F3.2-A5` is Ligozat's Kronecker character evaluated at
the lower-right entry:

  `w(γ) = ( (-1)^k ∏_{δ ∣ N} δ^{r_δ} / γ₁₁ )`  for every `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)`.

Combined with `F3.2-A5` this says `f(γz) = χ(d)·(cz+d)^k·f(z)` with `χ` the character Ligozat
writes down, which is the criterion as stated in the literature.  See
`multiplier_eq_ligozat_character_of_le_four` for the form with genuine integer exponents. -/
theorem multiplier_eq_kronecker_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4) (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r)
    (γ : Gamma0 N) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (ligozatKroneckerNum N r k) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  interval_cases N
  · exact multiplier_eq_kronecker_one r hk h1 h2 γ
  · exact multiplier_eq_kronecker_two r hk h1 h2 γ
  · exact multiplier_eq_kronecker_three r hk h1 h2 γ
  · exact multiplier_eq_kronecker_four r hk h1 h2 γ


/-! ### The level-4 weight-5 validation -/

/-- The node's validation example: `η(z)⁴ η(2z)² η(4z)⁴`, level `4`, weight `5`. -/
def ligozatFourFive : EtaExp :=
  fun δ => if δ = 1 then 4 else if δ = 2 then 2 else if δ = 4 then 4 else 0

theorem ligozatFourFive_sum : ∑ δ ∈ (4 : ℕ).divisors, ligozatFourFive δ = 2 * 5 := by decide

theorem ligozatFourFive_congr1 : LigozatCongr1 4 ligozatFourFive := by
  have h : ∑ δ ∈ (4 : ℕ).divisors, (δ : ℤ) * ligozatFourFive δ = 24 := by decide
  exact ⟨1, by rw [h]; ring⟩

theorem ligozatFourFive_congr2 : LigozatCongr2 4 ligozatFourFive := by
  have h : ∑ δ ∈ (4 : ℕ).divisors, ((4 / δ : ℕ) : ℤ) * ligozatFourFive δ = 24 := by decide
  exact ⟨1, by rw [h]; ring⟩

theorem ligozatFourFive_num : ligozatKroneckerNum 4 ligozatFourFive 5 = -1024 := by decide

/-- `(-4/d) = χ₄(d)` for odd `d`; `-4 = (-1)·2²`. -/
theorem kroneckerSym_neg_four {d : ℤ} (hd : Odd d) :
    kroneckerSym (-4) d = ZMod.χ₄ ((d : ℤ) : ZMod 4) := by
  have hdodd : ¬ ((2 : ℤ) ∣ d) := by
    rw [Int.odd_iff] at hd; omega
  have hgcd : Int.gcd ((2 : ℤ) ^ 1) d = 1 := by
    have h := gcd_pow_of_not_dvd Nat.prime_two (d := d) (by exact_mod_cast hdodd) 1
    simpa using h
  rw [show (-4 : ℤ) = (-1) * ((2 : ℤ) ^ 1) ^ 2 by norm_num,
    kroneckerSym_mul_left (by norm_num) (by positivity), kroneckerSym_neg_one hd,
    kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd, mul_one]

/-- **VALIDATION (the node's own example).**  For `f = η(z)⁴η(2z)²η(4z)⁴` — level `4`, weight
`5`, both congruences holding with `Σ δ r_δ = Σ (4/δ) r_δ = 24` — the character produced by
`F3.2-B3` is literally `(-4/d)`, the character the literature attaches to this form.  The
numerator computed by `ligozatKroneckerNum` is `-1024 = (-4)·16²`, and the square is invisible to
the symbol. -/
theorem ligozat_level_four_weight_five (γ : Gamma0 4) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom ligozatFourFive ligozatFourFive_sum γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (-4) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  have hdodd : ¬ ((2 : ℤ) ∣ (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) :=
    gamma0_not_dvd γ.2 (by norm_num) (by norm_num)
  have hgcd : Int.gcd ((2 : ℤ) ^ 4) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) = 1 := by
    have h := gcd_pow_of_not_dvd Nat.prime_two (d := ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1))
      (by exact_mod_cast hdodd) 4
    simpa using h
  rw [multiplier_eq_kronecker_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) ligozatFourFive
      ligozatFourFive_sum ligozatFourFive_congr1 ligozatFourFive_congr2 γ,
    ligozatFourFive_num,
    show (-1024 : ℤ) = (-4) * ((2 : ℤ) ^ 4) ^ 2 by norm_num,
    kroneckerSym_mul_left (by norm_num) (by positivity), kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd, mul_one]


/-! ### The bridge to Ligozat's own phrasing -/

/-- Kronecker symbol values are `0`, `1` or `-1`. -/
theorem kroneckerSym_trichotomy (a d : ℤ) :
    kroneckerSym a d = 0 ∨ kroneckerSym a d = 1 ∨ kroneckerSym a d = -1 := by
  by_cases hd : d = 0
  · simp only [kroneckerSym, if_pos hd]
    split_ifs <;> simp
  · have hL : ∀ L : List ℕ, (L.map (kroneckerPrime a)).prod = 0
        ∨ (L.map (kroneckerPrime a)).prod = 1 ∨ (L.map (kroneckerPrime a)).prod = -1 := by
      intro L
      induction L with
      | nil => simp
      | cons q L ih =>
          rw [List.map_cons, List.prod_cons]
          rcases kroneckerPrime_trichotomy a q with h | h | h <;>
            rcases ih with h2 | h2 | h2 <;> simp [h, h2]
    rw [kroneckerSym, if_neg hd]
    rcases hL d.natAbs.primeFactorsList with h | h | h <;> rw [h] <;> split_ifs <;> simp

theorem kroneckerSym_ne_zero_of_gcd {a d : ℤ} (h : Int.gcd a d = 1) : kroneckerSym a d ≠ 0 := by
  by_cases hd : d = 0
  · subst hd
    have hs : a.natAbs = 1 := by simpa [Int.gcd] using h
    have : a = 1 ∨ a = -1 := by
      rcases Int.natAbs_eq a with hh | hh
      · left; rw [hh, hs]; norm_num
      · right; rw [hh, hs]; norm_num
    simp only [kroneckerSym, if_pos this]
    norm_num
  · rw [kroneckerSym, if_neg hd]
    refine mul_ne_zero (by split_ifs <;> norm_num) (List.prod_ne_zero ?_)
    intro hmem
    obtain ⟨q, hq, hq0⟩ := List.mem_map.mp hmem
    have hqp := Nat.prime_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
    have hqd : q ∣ d.natAbs := Nat.dvd_of_mem_primeFactorsList hq
    refine kroneckerPrime_ne_zero hqp (fun hc => ?_) hq0
    have hqa : q ∣ a.natAbs := Int.natCast_dvd_natCast.mp (Int.dvd_natAbs.mpr hc)
    have hone : q ∣ Int.gcd a d := Nat.dvd_gcd hqa hqd
    rw [h, Nat.dvd_one] at hone
    exact hqp.one_lt.ne' hone

theorem kroneckerSym_pow_left {a : ℤ} (ha : a ≠ 0) (n : ℕ) (d : ℤ) :
    kroneckerSym (a ^ n) d = (kroneckerSym a d) ^ n := by
  induction n with
  | zero => simpa using kroneckerSym_one_left d
  | succ n ih => rw [pow_succ, kroneckerSym_mul_left (pow_ne_zero n ha) ha, ih, pow_succ]

theorem kroneckerSym_prod_left {ι : Type*} [DecidableEq ι] (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → ℤ)
    (hf : ∀ i ∈ s, f i ≠ 0) (d : ℤ) :
    kroneckerSym (∏ i ∈ s, f i) d = ∏ i ∈ s, kroneckerSym (f i) d := by
  induction s using Finset.induction_on with
  | empty => simpa using kroneckerSym_one_left d
  | insert i s hi ih =>
      rw [Finset.prod_insert hi, Finset.prod_insert hi,
        kroneckerSym_mul_left (hf i (Finset.mem_insert_self i s))
          (Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff.mpr fun j hj => hf j (Finset.mem_insert_of_mem hj)),
        ih (fun j hj => hf j (Finset.mem_insert_of_mem hj))]

theorem zpow_eq_pow_natAbs {x : ℂ} (hx : x = 1 ∨ x = -1) (m : ℤ) : x ^ m = x ^ m.natAbs := by
  rcases hx with rfl | rfl
  · simp
  · rcases Int.even_or_odd m with h | h
    · rw [h.neg_one_zpow, (Int.natAbs_even.mpr h).neg_one_pow]
    · rw [h.neg_one_zpow, (Int.natAbs_odd.mpr h).neg_one_pow]

/-- **The `|r_δ|` convention is not a weakening.**  On arguments coprime to `d`, the integer
numerator `ligozatKroneckerNum` produces exactly Ligozat's completely-multiplicative expression
with **genuine integer exponents**:

  `( (-1)^{|k|} ∏ δ^{|r_δ|} / d ) = (-1/d)^k · ∏_δ (δ/d)^{r_δ}`  (`zpow` on the right).

The point is that `(δ/d) = ±1`, and `±1` is its own inverse. -/
theorem kroneckerSym_ligozat_eq_prod (N : ℕ) (r : EtaExp) (k : ℤ) {d : ℤ}
    (hcop : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors, Int.gcd (δ : ℤ) d = 1) :
    ((kroneckerSym (ligozatKroneckerNum N r k) d : ℤ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (-1) d : ℤ) : ℂ) ^ k
        * ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((kroneckerSym ((δ : ℕ) : ℤ) d : ℤ) : ℂ) ^ (r δ) := by
  classical
  have hδ : ∀ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((δ : ℤ)) ≠ 0 := by
    intro δ hδ
    exact_mod_cast (Nat.pos_of_mem_divisors hδ).ne'
  have hprodne : (∏ δ ∈ N.divisors, ((δ : ℤ)) ^ (r δ).natAbs) ≠ 0 :=
    Finset.prod_ne_zero_iff.mpr fun δ hδ' => pow_ne_zero _ (hδ δ hδ')
  have hgm1 : Int.gcd (-1 : ℤ) d = 1 := by simp [Int.gcd]
  rw [ligozatKroneckerNum,
    kroneckerSym_mul_left (pow_ne_zero _ (by norm_num)) hprodne,
    kroneckerSym_pow_left (by norm_num) _ d,
    kroneckerSym_prod_left _ _ (fun δ hδ' => pow_ne_zero _ (hδ δ hδ')) d]
  push_cast
  rw [zpow_eq_pow_natAbs (by
      rcases kroneckerSym_trichotomy (-1 : ℤ) d with h | h | h
      · exact absurd h (kroneckerSym_ne_zero_of_gcd hgm1)
      · left; rw [h]; norm_num
      · right; rw [h]; norm_num) k]
  congr 1
  refine Finset.prod_congr rfl fun δ hδ' => ?_
  rw [kroneckerSym_pow_left (hδ δ hδ') _ d]
  push_cast
  rw [zpow_eq_pow_natAbs (by
      rcases kroneckerSym_trichotomy ((δ : ℕ) : ℤ) d with h | h | h
      · exact absurd h (kroneckerSym_ne_zero_of_gcd (hcop δ hδ'))
      · left; rw [h]; norm_num
      · right; rw [h]; norm_num) (r δ)]


/-- For `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` every divisor of `N` is coprime to `γ₁₁`. -/
theorem gamma0_gcd_eq_one {N : ℕ} {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) {m : ℕ}
    (hm : (m : ℤ) ∣ (N : ℤ)) : Int.gcd (m : ℤ) (γ 1 1) = 1 := by
  have hc : (N : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 0 := (ZMod.intCast_zmod_eq_zero_iff_dvd _ _).mp (Gamma0_mem.mp hγ)
  have hg1 : ((Int.gcd (m : ℤ) (γ 1 1) : ℕ) : ℤ) ∣ (m : ℤ) := Int.gcd_dvd_left _ _
  have hg2 : ((Int.gcd (m : ℤ) (γ 1 1) : ℕ) : ℤ) ∣ γ 1 1 := Int.gcd_dvd_right _ _
  have hone : ((Int.gcd (m : ℤ) (γ 1 1) : ℕ) : ℤ) ∣ 1 := by
    have hdet := sl_det_entries γ
    have hsub : ((Int.gcd (m : ℤ) (γ 1 1) : ℕ) : ℤ) ∣ γ 0 0 * γ 1 1 - γ 0 1 * γ 1 0 :=
      dvd_sub (Dvd.dvd.mul_left hg2 _) (Dvd.dvd.mul_left ((hg1.trans hm).trans hc) _)
    rwa [hdet] at hsub
  exact Nat.dvd_one.mp (by exact_mod_cast hone)

/-- **`F3.2-B3` in Ligozat's own notation.**  Same theorem as
`multiplier_eq_kronecker_of_le_four`, with the character written as the product of Kronecker
symbols with the ORIGINAL integer exponents `k` and `r_δ` — no absolute values anywhere:

  `w(γ) = (-1/d)^k · ∏_{δ ∣ N} (δ/d)^{r_δ}`,  `d = γ₁₁`. -/
theorem multiplier_eq_ligozat_character_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4)
    (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ} (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k)
    (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r) (γ : Gamma0 N) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom r hk γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (-1) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) ^ k
        * ∏ δ ∈ N.divisors,
            ((kroneckerSym ((δ : ℕ) : ℤ) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) ^ (r δ) := by
  rw [multiplier_eq_kronecker_of_le_four hN hN4 r hk h1 h2 γ]
  exact kroneckerSym_ligozat_eq_prod N r k
    (fun δ hδ => gamma0_gcd_eq_one γ.2 (by exact_mod_cast Nat.dvd_of_mem_divisors hδ))


/-- The Kronecker numerator of the level-`3` odd-weight example `η(z)⁻³η(3z)⁹`:
`(-1)³·1³·3⁹ = -19683`.  Note the NEGATIVE exponent `r₁ = -3`, which is where the `|r_δ|`
convention of `ligozatKroneckerNum` is actually exercised. -/
theorem ligozatOddExp_num : ligozatKroneckerNum 3 ligozatOddExp 3 = -19683 := by decide

/-- **VALIDATION 2 (odd weight, negative exponent).**  For `η(z)⁻³η(3z)⁹` — level `3`, weight
`3`, both congruences holding — the character produced by `F3.2-B3` is `(-3/d)`, the quadratic
character mod `3`.  This is the classical nebentypus of that form, and it is the same example
`not_multiplier_trivial_odd` uses to show the multiplier is NOT trivial: `F3.2-B2` could only
say "not `1`", `F3.2-B3` says which character it is. -/
theorem ligozat_level_three_weight_three (γ : Gamma0 3) :
    ((etaMultiplierHom ligozatOddExp ligozatOddExp_sum γ : ℂˣ) : ℂ)
      = ((kroneckerSym (-3) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ) := by
  have hd3 : ¬ ((3 : ℤ) ∣ (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) :=
    gamma0_not_dvd γ.2 (by norm_num) (by norm_num)
  have hgcd : Int.gcd ((3 : ℤ) ^ 4) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) = 1 := by
    have h := gcd_pow_of_not_dvd Nat.prime_three (d := ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1))
      (by exact_mod_cast hd3) 4
    simpa using h
  rw [multiplier_eq_kronecker_of_le_four (by norm_num) (by norm_num) ligozatOddExp
      ligozatOddExp_sum ligozatOddExp_congr1 ligozatOddExp_congr2 γ,
    ligozatOddExp_num, show (-19683 : ℤ) = (-3) * ((3 : ℤ) ^ 4) ^ 2 by norm_num,
    kroneckerSym_mul_left (by norm_num) (by positivity), kroneckerSym_sq_of_gcd hgcd, mul_one]

/-- **`F3.2-B3`, the transformation law in Ligozat's own words.**  For `0 < N ≤ 4`,
`Σ_δ r_δ = 2k`, and Ligozat's congruences `(i)` and `(ii)`, the eta quotient
`f(z) = ∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δz)^{r_δ}` satisfies, for every `γ = (a,b;c,d) ∈ Γ₀(N)` and every `z ∈ ℍ`,

  `f(γz) = χ(d) · (cz + d)^k · f(z)`,   `χ(d) = ( (-1)^k ∏_{δ ∣ N} δ^{r_δ} / d )`,

with `( · / · )` the Kronecker symbol.  This is Ligozat's criterion as the literature states it
(minus condition `(iii)`, which is about holomorphy at the cusps, not about the transformation
law, and is carried as the hypothesis `hbd` of `etaQuotientModularForm`). -/
theorem ligozat_kronecker_transform_of_le_four {N : ℕ} (hN : 0 < N) (hN4 : N ≤ 4)
    (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ} (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k)
    (h1 : LigozatCongr1 N r) (h2 : LigozatCongr2 N r) (γ : Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ) :
    etaQuotientH N r ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) • z)
      = ((kroneckerSym (ligozatKroneckerNum N r k) ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) 1 1) : ℤ) : ℂ)
        * (denom ((γ : SL(2, ℤ)) : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)) ^ k * etaQuotientH N r z := by
  rw [← multiplier_eq_kronecker_of_le_four hN hN4 r hk h1 h2 γ]
  exact etaQuotientH_transform r hk γ.2 z

/-! ### F3.2-C1 — the MODULUS of the `η` transformation, on ALL of `SL(2,ℤ)`

Classically `η(γz) = ε(γ) · (cz+d)^{1/2} · η(z)` for every `γ = (a,b;c,d) ∈ SL(2,ℤ)`, where the
multiplier `ε(γ)` is a 24th root of unity whose closed form is exactly where Dedekind sums enter
— and Mathlib has none (`DedekindSum`, `dedekindSum`, `etaMultiplier`: zero hits at commit
`905b95818e`).

In ABSOLUTE VALUE that entire difficulty evaporates: `‖ε(γ)‖ = 1`, and the two branches of
`(cz+d)^{1/2}` differ by a sign, which the norm also kills.  What is left,

  `‖η(γz)‖ = √‖cz+d‖ · ‖η(z)‖`,

is true for **every** `γ ∈ SL(2,ℤ)`: no congruence condition, no character, no choice of
generators, no branch bookkeeping.

HOW IT IS PROVED — and it is NOT by induction over generators.  Raising to the 24th power kills
the multiplier *before* any norm is taken: `η²⁴ = Δ` is Mathlib's `ModularForm.discriminant`,
modular of weight `12` for the FULL modular group.  The `δ = 1` case of `F3.2-A4`'s
`eta_pow24_natScale_smul` is verbatim `η(γz)²⁴ = (cz+d)¹² · η(z)²⁴`.  Taking norms gives
`‖η(γz)‖²⁴ = ‖cz+d‖¹² · ‖η(z)‖²⁴`, and `(√‖cz+d‖ · ‖η(z)‖)²⁴` is the same real number by
`Real.sq_sqrt`; since `x ↦ x²⁴` is injective on `[0, ∞)` (`pow_left_inj₀`) and both sides are
norms, they are equal.  So `Subgroup.closure_induction` over `⟨S, T⟩` is not needed at all, and
the S-transformation enters only through Mathlib's own `discriminant_S_invariant`.

SCOPE, stated so it cannot be misread (LL-1).  This is a statement about `‖η‖`, a nonnegative
real number.  It does NOT determine `η(γz)` itself: the 24th root of unity `ε(γ)` is precisely
the information the norm discards, and pinning that down for a general `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)` is still
`F3.2-OBSTRUCTED`.  What it does unlock is every question that sees only `|f|` — growth,
vanishing orders, `log|f|` — which is the cusp-order analysis. -/

/-- **F3.2-C1.**  The modulus of the Dedekind `η` transformation, for the WHOLE of `SL(2,ℤ)`:

  `‖η(γ · z)‖ = √‖denom γ z‖ · ‖η(z)‖`   for all `γ ∈ SL(2,ℤ)` and all `z ∈ ℍ`.

The `η` multiplier system — the Dedekind-sum-valued 24th root of unity — is invisible here
because it has modulus `1`.  Proved from `eta_pow24_natScale_smul` at `δ = 1`, i.e. from
Mathlib's full-level modularity of `Δ = η²⁴`, with no generator induction. -/
theorem abs_eta_smul (γ : SL(2, ℤ)) (z : ℍ) :
    ‖ModularForm.eta ((γ • z : ℍ) : ℂ)‖
      = Real.sqrt ‖denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)‖ * ‖ModularForm.eta (z : ℂ)‖ := by
  -- Step 1: the 24th power, where the multiplier is already gone.  `δ = 1` in `F3.2-A4`.
  have h24 : ModularForm.eta ((γ • z : ℍ) : ℂ) ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ) ^ (12 : ℕ)
        * ModularForm.eta (z : ℂ) ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
    have h := eta_pow24_natScale_smul (δ := 1) Nat.one_pos (γ := γ)
      (by simp) z
    simpa only [Nat.cast_one, one_mul] using h
  -- Step 2: take norms; `‖·‖` is multiplicative and commutes with powers.
  have hnorm : ‖ModularForm.eta ((γ • z : ℍ) : ℂ)‖ ^ (24 : ℕ)
      = ‖denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)‖ ^ (12 : ℕ)
        * ‖ModularForm.eta (z : ℂ)‖ ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
    simpa only [norm_mul, norm_pow] using congrArg (fun w : ℂ => ‖w‖) h24
  -- Step 3: `x ↦ x ^ 24` is injective on the nonnegative reals; both sides are norms.
  refine (pow_left_inj₀ (norm_nonneg _)
    (mul_nonneg (Real.sqrt_nonneg _) (norm_nonneg _)) (n := 24) (by norm_num)).mp ?_
  rw [hnorm, mul_pow]
  congr 1
  rw [show (24 : ℕ) = 2 * 12 from rfl, pow_mul,
    Real.sq_sqrt (norm_nonneg (denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)))]

/-- The eta multiplier `w(γ)` of `F3.2-A5` has modulus `1`: it satisfies `w ^ 24 = 1`, and the
only nonnegative real 24th root of `1` is `1`.  This is the one-line reason the character is
invisible to `‖·‖`. -/
theorem norm_etaMultiplierVal {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) :
    ‖etaMultiplierVal N r k γ‖ = 1 := by
  have h : ‖etaMultiplierVal N r k γ‖ ^ (24 : ℕ) = (1 : ℝ) ^ (24 : ℕ) := by
    simpa only [norm_pow, norm_one, one_pow] using
      congrArg (fun w : ℂ => ‖w‖) (etaMultiplierVal_pow24 r hk hγ)
  exact (pow_left_inj₀ (norm_nonneg _) zero_le_one (n := 24) (by norm_num)).mp h

/-- **F3.2-C1, the eta-quotient corollary.**  The modulus of an arbitrary eta quotient under an
arbitrary `γ ∈ Γ₀(N)`, again with no multiplier: for `f(z) = ∏_{δ ∣ N} η(δz)^{r_δ}` of weight
`k` (`Σ_δ r_δ = 2k`),

  `‖f(γz)‖ = ‖denom γ z‖^k · ‖f(z)‖`.

Same mechanism as `abs_eta_smul` — the character `w(γ)` of `F3.2-A5` has modulus `1` — but this
one carries the `Γ₀(N)` membership, because `f` is only `Γ₀(N)`-modular.  This is the form the
cusp-order analysis consumes. -/
theorem norm_etaQuotientH_smul {N : ℕ} (r : EtaExp) {k : ℤ}
    (hk : ∑ δ ∈ N.divisors, r δ = 2 * k) {γ : SL(2, ℤ)} (hγ : γ ∈ Gamma0 N) (z : ℍ) :
    ‖etaQuotientH N r (γ • z)‖
      = ‖denom (γ : GL (Fin 2) ℝ) (z : ℂ)‖ ^ k * ‖etaQuotientH N r z‖ := by
  rw [etaQuotientH_transform r hk hγ z]
  simp only [norm_mul, norm_zpow, norm_etaMultiplierVal r hk hγ, one_mul]


/-! #### F3.2-C1 — the LL-1 guard

`abs_eta_smul` was proved through `Δ = η²⁴` (Mathlib's `discriminant_S_invariant` and
`discriminant_T_invariant`, packaged as `CuspForm.discriminant`).  Specialised to `γ = T` it
says `‖η(z+1)‖ = ‖η(z)‖`.  That SAME proposition is reachable by a route sharing no step with
it: `F3.1-B2`'s `eta_add_one`, `η(z+1) = e^{2πi/24} η(z)`, read straight off the `q`-product,
whose exponential prefactor has modulus `1`.  Two derivations, one statement — a drifted
exponent, a stray `denom` power or an inverted `√` in `abs_eta_smul` would break the pair. -/

/-- Route B (the `q`-product): `‖η(z+1)‖ = ‖η(z)‖` from `F3.1-B2`, on all of `ℂ`. -/
theorem norm_eta_add_one (z : ℂ) :
    ‖ModularForm.eta (z + 1)‖ = ‖ModularForm.eta z‖ := by
  rw [eta_add_one, norm_mul,
    show 2 * (π : ℂ) * I / 24 = ((π / 12 : ℝ) : ℂ) * I by push_cast; ring,
    Complex.norm_exp_ofReal_mul_I, one_mul]

/-- Route A (`F3.2-C1` at `γ = T`): the same proposition out of `abs_eta_smul`, whose only
analytic input is Mathlib's full-level modularity of `Δ`. -/
theorem norm_eta_T_smul (z : ℍ) :
    ‖ModularForm.eta ((ModularGroup.T • z : ℍ) : ℂ)‖ = ‖ModularForm.eta (z : ℂ)‖ := by
  have h := abs_eta_smul ModularGroup.T z
  rwa [denom_T, norm_one, Real.sqrt_one, one_mul] at h

/-- **THE GUARD.**  Routes A and B agree.  Stated as an equation between the two derived
values so that neither can be silently weakened. -/
theorem abs_eta_smul_T_guard (z : ℍ) :
    ‖ModularForm.eta ((ModularGroup.T • z : ℍ) : ℂ)‖
      = ‖ModularForm.eta ((z : ℂ) + 1)‖ := by
  rw [norm_eta_T_smul, ← norm_eta_add_one (z : ℂ)]


end SocrateAI.ModularForms

